2 CONT Åsa married Halvdan "Hvitbeinn" Olofsson, son of Olav "Tretelgja" Ingjaldsson and Solveig Halvdansdatter av Solør, circa 700 in Norway. (Halvdan "Hvitbeinn" Olofsson was born about 680 in Norway and died about 715 in Toten, Opplan
2 CONC d, Norway.)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Father: Øystein HARDRÅDE
2 CONT
2 CONT Family 1 : Halvdan Olavsson KVITBEIN
2 CONT
2 CONT +Øystein HALVDANSSON
2 CONT
2 CONT Kilde: nermo.org
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Åsa married Halvdan "Kvitbein" Olofsson, son of Olav "Tretelgja" Ingjaldsson and Solveig Halvdansdatter Av Solør. (Halvdan "Kvitbein" Olofsson was born about 660 in Norway and died about 745 in Toten, Oppland, Norway.)
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Född: 680 , Norge
2 CONT
2 CONT Äktenskap: Halvdan " Hvitbeinn "Olofsson ca 700 i Norge
2 CONT
2 CONT Död: 718 , Norge åldern 38
2 CONT
2 CONT Ett annat namn för Åsa var Åsa Øysteinsdatter AV Oplandene .
2 CONT
2 CONT Allmänna hänvisningar: Från "The Ynglinga Saga " :
2 CONT
2 CONT " 49 . AV Halfdan HVITBEIN .
2 CONT
2 CONT Halfdan Hvitbein blev en stor konung . Han var gift med Aasa , en dotter till Eystein den svåra , som var kung i Uppland folket, och härskade över Hedemarken . Halfdan och Aasa fick två söner, Eystein och Gudröd . ... "
2 CONT
2 CONT Noterade händelser i hennes liv var: • Hon var en Drottning i Norge.
2 CONT
2 CONT Åsa gift Halvdan " Hvitbeinn "Olofsson, son till Olav " Tretelgja " Ingjaldsson och Solveig Halvdansdatter AV Solør , ca 700 i Norge. ( Halvdan " Hvitbeinn Olofsson föddes omkring 680 i Norge och dog omkring 715 i Toten , Oppland 
2 CONC , Norge. )
2 CONT
2 CONT Källor
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Wikipedia ( http://en.wikipedia.org/ ).
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Snorre Sturlasson (ca 1179 till 1241 ), http://sunsite.berkeley.edu/OMACL/ ( Om 1225 ) , "Den YNGLINGA Saga " .
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Åsa var datter av kong Øystein Hardråde som styrte i Oppland og Hedemark
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Han var gift med Åsa, dotter av opplendingskongen Øystein. Dei fekk to søner, Øystein og Gudrød.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Halfdan was to become a great king, who married Åsa, the daughter of king Eystein, the ruler of Oppland and Hedmark. They had two sons, Öystein Halfdansson and Gudröd.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT More than twenty years ago, a teaching colleague announced over lunch: "Osberg, I see your name in print." He held a sugar packet depicting a Viking Ship with the caption: The Oseberg Ship - A fine example of a Viking Longship. Th
2 CONC is launched me on a journey to discover not only the origins of my name but its intimate link to Viking history. Despite being proud of my Swedish ancestry (my great grandfather was a Swedish sea captain), my name derives from a s
2 CONC mall town in Norway (somewhere along the way my ancestors dropped an 'e'). The town of Oseberg was the site of the excavation of the Oseberg Ship, one of the few Viking Ships whose remains were reconstructed. The other is the Goks
2 CONC tad Ship. Buried along with the Oseberg Ship were numerous artifacts reflecting Viking art and culture. Of course, you know it was Leif Erickson, and not Columbus, who discovered America! See the Viking ship photos and links below
2 CONC  to learn more about Viking Ships and the Vikings.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Oseberg Ship - A Fine Example
2 CONT
2 CONT of a Viking Longship
2 CONT
2 CONT (Click on the image above to
2 CONT
2 CONT access a larger image.)
2 CONT
2 CONT Oseberg Ship Facts:
2 CONT
2 CONT Excavated in 1904 in Oseberg, Vestfold County, Norway after being discovered by a farmer.
2 CONT
2 CONT One of the two most complete Viking ships unearthed in Norway (the other being the Gokstad), it is 21.5 meters long (nearly 70 feet).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Oseberg Ship burial, including bodies of two women, is considered the most richly furnished Scandinavian grave ever found. One of the women may have been the famous Queen Asa from which the name Oseberg derives: Asa's mound.
2 CONT
2 CONT Items buried included an ornate cart, beds, sledges, wooden chests, riding equipment, and twelve horses.
2 CONT
2 CONT Built in oak and contains 12 board planks and 15 oar ports on each side, indicating a crew of 35-40.
2 CONT
2 CONT The stem and stern were decorated with ornate wood carvings, with the ship likely used for voyages in the fjords along the coasts of Norway by people of high rank.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Oseberg/Gokstad finds were joined by the discovery of five Viking Ships buried in the Roskilde fjord near Skuldelev, Denmark in 1962.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Title: Ancestral Roots of Certain American Colonists, 7th Edition, by Frederick Lewis Weis, additions by Walter Lee Shippard Jr., 1999
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: Page: 243a-15 Note: Text: Asa, wife of "White Legs" - no last name -------------------- Åsa Eysteinsdotter var datter av Eystein Hardråde. Hun var gift med Halvdan Kvitbein og de to hadde sønnene Gudrød Halvdansson og Eystei
2 CONC n Halvdansson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Åsas sønn Eystein ble gift med Hild Eiriksdotter og de to fikk sønnen Halvdan Eysteinsson den gavmilde/Milde.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://lind.no/nor/index.asp?lang=&emne=nor&person=%C5sa%20%D8ysteinsdotter
2 CONT
2 CONT En del oppgir (708-739) som år for fødsel og død.
2 CONT
2 CONT English: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/H%C3%A1lfdanar_saga_Eysteinssonar
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT From "The Ynglinga Saga":
2 CONT
2 CONT "49. OF HALFDAN HVITBEIN.
2 CONT
2 CONT Halfdan Hvitbein became a great king. He was married to Aasa, a daughter of Eystein the Severe, who was king of the Upland people, and ruled over Hedemark. Halfdan and Aasa had two sons, Eystein and Gudrod. ..."
2 CONT
2 CONT Noted events in her life were: • She was a Drottning in Norway.
2 CONT
2 CONT Åsa married Halvdan "Hvitbeinn" Olofsson, son of Olav "Tretelgja" Ingjaldsson and Solveig Halvdansdatter av Solør, circa 700 in Norway. (Halvdan "Hvitbeinn" Olofsson was born about 680 in Norway and died about 715 in Toten, Opplan
2 CONC d, Norway.)
2 CONT
