2 CONC sently situated). Here, they settled and cleared land. Soon they had created a whole province called Värmland, where they could make good living.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the Swedes learnt that Olof was clearing land, they were amused and called him the Tree-feller. Olof married a woman named Solveig who was a daughter of Halfdan Guldtand of Soleyar. Olof and Solveigh had two sons, Ingjald Olo
2 CONC fsson and Halfdan Hvitbeinn, who were brought up in Soleyar in the house of his mother's uncle Sölve.
2 CONT
2 CONT Because of king Ivar Vidfamne and his harsh rule many Swedes emigrated to Värmland, and they became so numerous that the province could not sustain them. The land was afflicted by famine of which the Swedes accused the king. It wa
2 CONC s an old tradition in Sweden of holding the king responsible for the wealth of the land (see Domalde). The Swedes accused Olof of neglecting his sacrifices to the gods and believed that this was the cause of the famine.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Swedish settlers thus rebelled against Olof, surrounded his house on the shores of lake Vänern and burnt him inside it. Thus he was sacrificed to Odin, like his ancestor Domalde.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ynglingatal and Historia Norwegiae
2 CONT
2 CONT However, Historia Norwegiae says that Olof succeeded his father and ruled as the king of Sweden in peace until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ejus filius Olavus cognomento tretelgia diu et pacifice functus regno plenus dierum obiit in Swethia.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT His son, Olav, known as Tretelgje, accomplished a long and peaceful reign, and died in Sweden, replete in years.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT The lines of Ynglingatal appear to say that he was a Swedish prince (svía jöfri), and that he was burnt inside his hall and disappeared from Gamla Uppsala.
2 CONT
