2 CONT Archaelogy
2 CONT
2 CONT Along the lower parts of the river Byälven in Värmland, there are three large barrows, which legend attributes to Olof Trätälja. Moreover, there are many hillforts near this river and the northern shore of Lake Vänern testifying t
2 CONC o a violent period. Archaeological excavations from one of the hillforts, Villkorsberget, show that it was burnt in a period corresponding to Olof (510–680).
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT King of the Swedes Óláfr Trételgju Ingjaldsson was forced to leave Nerike, when the Swedes heard he was there, and he went on westward, through the forest, to a river which comes from the north and falls into the Venner lake, and 
2 CONC is called Klar river. There they sat themselves down, turned to and cleared the woods, burnt, and then settled there, and soon there were great districts, and altogether they were called Vermeland.
2 CONT
2 CONT Olafr was raised in the care of his foster-father Bove, in West Gotland, where he was brought up along with Saxe, Bove's son, who had the surname of Flette. He was given the name "Tree Feller" by the Swedes, who laughed at his pro
2 CONC ceedings, that he was clearing forests instead of ruling. He attracted a great many of his countrymen, who were fleeing Sweden on account of Ivar, and they settled in the bountiful Varmeland, but in so great a number that the land
2 CONC  could no longer support them, and they blamed him. He was is said to have sacrificed his own people in time of famine. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Sólveig Hálfdansdóttir of Solør, daughter of Hálfdan Guldtand Solvarsson of Solør.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Swedes took it amiss that Olaf was sparing in his sacrifices, and therefore gathered together troops, made an expedition against King Olaf, surrounded his house and burnt him in it, giving him to Odin as a sacrifice for good c
2 CONC rops. This happened at the Venner lake.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thus tells Thjodolf of it: 
2 CONT
2 CONT "The temple wolf, by the lake shores,
2 CONT
2 CONT The corpse of Olaf now devours.
2 CONT
2 CONT The clearer of the forests died
2 CONT
2 CONT At Odin's shrine by the lake side.
2 CONT
2 CONT The glowing flames stripped to the skin
2 CONT
2 CONT The royal robes from the Swedes' king.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thus Olaf, famed in days of yore,
2 CONT
2 CONT Vanished from earth at Venner's shore."1
2 CONT
