1 NOTE King of Sweden.
2 CONT Olof Trätälja, Olav tretelgja, "trehogger", dvs. skogrydder, iflg. Snorres Ynglingesaga konge som ryddet jord i Värmland
2 CONT
2 CONT . Olav konge av Sverige.
2 CONT
2 CONT Olof (Trätälja) INGJALDSSON Antavla
2 CONT Yrke: Småkung i Värmland
2 CONT Far: Ingjald (Illråde) AUDUNSSON (620 - 655)
2 CONT Mor: Göthild ALGOTSDOTTER (620 - )
2 CONT
2 CONT Händelse Datum Plats Källa
2 CONT Födelse omkr 650 Uppland, C Egen beräkning
2 CONT Död omkr 690 Värmland, S Egen beräkning
2 CONT
2 CONT Familj med Solveig HALVDANSDOTTER (650 - 695)
2 CONT Barn: Halvdan (Vitben) OLOFSSON (685 - 715)
2 CONT
2 CONT Noteringar
2 CONT Olav 'Trätälja' var gift med Solveig eller Sölve. Hon var dotter till Halfdan 'Guldtand' från Solön i väster. När Olav fick höra om sin fars död for han först till Närke. Eftersom svearna hade bestämt sig att fördriva
2 CONC  faderns ätt fortsatte Olav till Värmland och röjde skog, brände och byggde och blev därigenom kung i Värmland. Många svear strömmade till Värmland. Det blev därför svår hungersnöd och svält. De inflyttade svearna sk
2 CONC yllde detta på sin kung och påstod att denne inte blotat tillräcligt mycket. Därför samlade de en här och belägrade Olavs hus och brände honom inne. Detta skedde vid Vänern. Makarna hade två söner Ingjald och Halfdan. I
2 CONC ngjald blev kung i Värmland. (Källa: Ynglingasagan)
1 NOTE Ble jagd fra Svitjod med hele sin ætt og ryddet  seg land ved de ubebodde områdene ved  Klaraelvens utløp i Vänern. Olav giftet seg med  Solveig, datter til Halvdan Gulltann fra Solør,  to sønner; Ingjald og Halvdan. Olav var inge
2 CONC n  blotsmann og fikk skylden for uår og sult, ble  innebrent av misfornøyde svear.
1 NOTE Olaf ble ofret til Odin av de svenske nybyggerne i Värmland på grunn av uår, men da de senere oppdaget at uåret var grunnet i overbefolkning og ikke kongens handlinger valgte noen å krysse Eidskogen og slå seg ned i Solør. H
2 CONC er tok de Halvdan til konge siden han var sønn av deres gamle konge, Olaf. De svenskene som ble igjen i Värmland tok hans bror Ingjald til konge.
1 NOTE Was sacrificed to Odin after a season of bad harvests due to overpopulation in hopes of better harvests. 
2 CONT What a nice reward for working hard to make something work!!
1 NOTE Olof Trätälja, Olaf Tretelgju or Olaf Tree Feller was the son of the Swedish king Ingjald Ill-ruler of the House of Yngling according to Heimskringla. His mother was Gauthild, a princess of West Götaland, whose maternal grandfathe
2 CONC r was Olof the Sharp-sighted, the king of Nerike.
2 CONT
2 CONT His mother sent him to his foster-father Bove in West Götaland, where he grew up with his foster-brother Saxe who was surnamed Flette.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Olof heard of his father's death, he assembled the men who were willing to follow him and went to his kinsmen in Nerike, because after his father's atrocities, the Swedes had grown hostile towards the Ynglings.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the Swedes learnt that Olof and his kin had sought refuge in Nerike, they were were attacked and had to head west through deep and mountainous forests (Kilsbergen) to lake Vänern and the estuary of Klarälven (where Karlstad i
2 CONC s presently situated). Here, they settled and cleared land. Soon they had created a whole province called Värmland, where they could make good living.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the Swedes learnt that Olof was clearing land, they were amused and called him the Tree-feller. Olof married a woman named Solveig who was a daughter of Halfdan Guldtand of Soleyar. Olof and Solveig had two sons, Ingjald Olof
2 CONC sson and Halfdan Hvitbeinn, who were brought up in Soleyar in the house of his mother's uncle Sölve.
2 CONT
2 CONT Because of king Ivar Vidfamne and his harsh rule many Swedes emigrated to Värmland, and they became so numerous that the province could not sustain them. The land was afflicted by famine of which the Swedes accused the king. It wa
2 CONC s an old tradition in Sweden of holding the king responsible for the wealth of the land. The Swedes accused Olof of neglecting his sacrifices to the gods and believed that this was the cause of the famine.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Swedish settlers thus rebelled against Olof, surrounded his house on the shores of lake Vänern and burnt him inside it. Thus he was sacrificed to Odin, like his ancestor Domalde.
1 NOTE !SOURCES:
2 CONT     1. The Viking Age, Gen. Hst. 19, v. 1, p. 67
2 CONT     2. Attarskra Bjarni Thorsteinsson, Ice FH 1, p. 431
2 CONT     3. Hist. of the Anglo-Saxons, Eng. 36, v. 1, p. 242
2 CONT     4. Keiser und Koenig Hist., Gen. Hist. 25, pt 1, p. 137
1 NOTE As a boy, he was raised by Beowulf in Vestre-Gautland. He was Gauthild's foster-father. He fled Upsala upon his father's death. He established a kingdom in Värmland, Sweden, which until that time had been unpopulated. His nickname
2 CONC  "Trtalgja" meant tree-feller.
1 NOTE Olaf "the Woodcutter" Ingjaldsson, (the tree-hewer, or Tree-Feller).
2 CONT roi de Suède 620-630, King of Vermeland Varmland (Norway) 640, puis devient roi de Vestfold (Norvège) 630-685.
2 CONT b. ca. 567 in Sweden, (vers 690 Uppsala, Norvège - Varmland, Suède) 
2 CONT
2 CONT He was raised (fostered) by Bove in West Gotland along with Bove's son, Saxe.
2 CONT When Ingjald died Olaf succeeded him, but the Swedes, who had suffered under his unpopular father, drove him and his people out of Sweden.
2 CONT When Olaf, King Ingjald's son, heard of his father's end, he went with the men who chose to follow him to Nerike; for all the Swedish community rose with one accord to drive out Ingjald's family and all his friends. 
2 CONT Because of his father Ingjald's, evil deeds, Olaf was forced to leave Sweden and went west to the Klar River in Norway. 
2 CONT Now, when the Swedes got intelligence of him he could not remain there, but went on westwards, through the forest, to a river which comes from the north and falls into the Venner lake, and is called Klar river.
2 CONT He began clearing the forest and soon created several large districts, together called Vermeland. 
2 CONT The Swedes heard about Olaf's activities and laughed, calling him "tree-feller" or "Woodcutter".
2 CONT
2 CONT There were a great many people who fled the country from Sweden, on account of King Ivar; and when they heard that King Olaf had got good lands in Vermeland, so great a number came there to him that the land could not support them
2 CONC . Then there came dear times and famine, which they ascribed to their king; as the Swedes used always to reckon good or bad crops for or against their kings. 
2 CONT Later during his reign the crops failed. Some of the people held that the king was personally responsible for the fertility of the land by keeping up the sacrifices.
2 CONT Olaf was known to have been sparing in this duty, and people surrounded his house at Venner Lake 
2 CONT The Swedes took it amiss that Olaf was sparing in his sacrifices, and believed the dear times must proceed from this cause. 
2 CONT The Swedes therefore gathered together troops, made an expedition against King Olaf, 
2 CONT surrounded his house and burnt him in it and burned it down with him inside, "giving him to Odin as a sacrifice for good crops."
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ???? Ivar Vidfadme subdued all of Sweden and Denmark, much of Saxland, all the East country and one fifth of England.
