2 CONT Ingjald illråde or Ingjaldr hinn illráði (Ingold Ill-ruler or Ill-ready) was a legendary Swedish king of the House of Ynglings. Ingjald may have ruled in the 7th century, and he was the son of the former king Anund.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingjald is mentioned in the Ynglinga saga, Historia Norvegiæ, Hervarar saga, Upplendinga Konungum, Þorsteins saga Víkingssonar and 
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT From http://www.rpi.edu/~holmes/Hobbies/Genealogy/ps11/ps11_146.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
2 CONT
2 CONT Onund had a son called Ingjald, and at that time Yngvar was king
2 CONT
2 CONT of the district of Fjadryndaland. Yngvar had two sons by his
2 CONT
2 CONT wife -- the one called Alf, the other Agnar -- who were about the
2 CONT
2 CONT same age as Ingjald. Onund's district-kings were at that time
2 CONT
2 CONT spread widely over Sweden, and Svipdag the Blind ruled over
2 CONT
2 CONT Tiundaland, in which Upsal is situated, and where all the Swedish
2 CONT
2 CONT Things are held. There also were held the mid-winter sacrifices,
2 CONT
2 CONT at which many kings attended. One year at midwinter there was a
2 CONT
2 CONT great assembly of people at Upsal, and King Yngvar had also come
2 CONT
2 CONT there with his sons. Alf, King Yngvar's son, and Ingjald, King
2 CONT
2 CONT Onund's son, were there -- both about six years old. They amused
2 CONT
2 CONT themselves with child's play, in which each should be leading on
2 CONT
2 CONT his army. In their play Ingjald found himself not so strong as
2 CONT
2 CONT Alf, and was so vexed that he almost cried. His foster-brother
2 CONT
2 CONT Gautvid came up, led him to his foster-father Svipdag the Blind,
2 CONT
2 CONT and told him how ill it appeared that he was weaker and less
2 CONT
2 CONT manly than Alf, King Yngvar's son. Svipdag replied that it was a
2 CONT
2 CONT great shame. The day after Svipdag took the heart of a wolf,
2 CONT
2 CONT roasted it on the tongs, and gave it to the king's son Ingjald to
2 CONT
2 CONT eat, and from that time he became a most ferocious person, and of
2 CONT
2 CONT the worst disposition. When Ingjald was grown up, Onund applied
2 CONT
2 CONT for him to King Algaut for his daughter Gauthild. Algaut was a
2 CONT
2 CONT son of Gautrek the Mild, and grandson of Gaut; and from them
2 CONT
2 CONT Gotland (Gautland) took its name. King Algaut thought his
2 CONT
2 CONT daughter would be well married if she got King Onund's son, and
2 CONT
2 CONT if he had his father's disposition; so the girl was sent to
2 CONT
2 CONT Sweden, and King Ingjald celebrated his wedding with her in due
2 CONT
2 CONT time.
2 CONT
2 CONT 39. OF KING ONUND'S DEATH.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Onund one autumn, travelling between his mansion-houses,
2 CONT
2 CONT came over a road called Himmenheath, where there are some narrow
2 CONT
2 CONT mountain valleys, with high mountains on both sides. There was
2 CONT
2 CONT heavy rain at the time, and before there had been snow on the
2 CONT
2 CONT mountains. A landslip of clay and stones came down upon King
2 CONT
2 CONT Onund and his people, and there he met his death, and many with
2 CONT
2 CONT him. So says Thjodolf, namely: --
2 CONT
2 CONT "We all have heard how Jonkur's sons,
2 CONT
2 CONT Whom weapons could not touch, with stones
2 CONT
2 CONT Were stoned to death in open day,
2 CONT
2 CONT King Onund died in the same way.
2 CONT
2 CONT Or else perhaps the wood-grown land,
2 CONT
2 CONT Which long had felt his conquering hand,
2 CONT
2 CONT Uprose at length in deadly strife,
2 CONT
2 CONT And pressed out Onund's hated life." 
2 CONT
2 CONT 40. THE BURNING IN UPSAL.
2 CONT
2 CONT Then Ingjald, King Onund's son, came to the kingdom. The Upsal
2 CONT
2 CONT kings were the highest in Sweden among the many district-kings
2 CONT
2 CONT who had been since the time that Odin was chief. The kings who
2 CONT
2 CONT resided at Upsal had been the supreme chiefs over the whole
2 CONT
2 CONT Swedish dominions until the death of Agne, when, as before
2 CONT
2 CONT related, the kingdom came to be divided between brothers. After
2 CONT
2 CONT that time the dominions and kingly powers were spread among the
2 CONT
2 CONT branches of the family as these increased; but some kings cleared
2 CONT
2 CONT great tracts of forest-land, and settled them, and thereby
2 CONT
2 CONT increased their domains. Now when Ingjald took the dominions and
2 CONT
2 CONT the kingdom of his father, there were, as before said, many
2 CONT
2 CONT district-kings. King Ingjald ordered a great feast to be
2 CONT
2 CONT prepared in Upsal, and intended at that feast to enter on his
2 CONT
2 CONT heritage after King Onund his father. He had a large hall made
2 CONT
2 CONT ready for the occasion -- one not less, nor less sumptuous, than
2 CONT
2 CONT that of Upsal; and this hall was called the Seven Kings Hall, and
2 CONT
2 CONT in it were seven high seats for kings. Then King Ingjald sent
2 CONT
2 CONT men all through Sweden, and invited to his feast kings, earls,
2 CONT
2 CONT and other men of consequence. To this heirship-feast came King
2 CONT
2 CONT Algaut, his father-in-law; Yngvar king of Fjadryndaland, with his
2 CONT
2 CONT two sons, Alf and Agnar; King Sporsnjall of Nerike; King Sighvat
2 CONT
2 CONT of Aattundaland: but Granmar king of Sodermanland did not come.
2 CONT
2 CONT Six kings were placed in the seats in the new hall; but one of
2 CONT
2 CONT the high seats which Ingjald had prepared was empty. All the
2 CONT
2 CONT persons who had come got places in the new hall; but to his own
2 CONT
2 CONT court, and the rest of his people, he had appointed places at
2 CONT
2 CONT Upsal. It was the custom at that time that he who gave an
2 CONT
2 CONT heirship-feast after kings or earls, and entered upon the
2 CONT
2 CONT heritage, should sit upon the footstool in front of the high
2 CONT
2 CONT seat, until the full bowl, which was called the Brage-beaker, was
2 CONT
2 CONT brought in. Then he should stand up, take the Brage-beaker, make
2 CONT
2 CONT solemn vows to be afterwards fulfilled, and thereupon empty the
2 CONT
2 CONT beaker. Then he should ascend the high seat which his father had
2 CONT
2 CONT occupied; and thus he came to the full heritage after his father.
2 CONT
2 CONT Now it was done so on this occasion. When the full Brage-beaker
2 CONT
2 CONT came in, King Ingjald stood up, grasped a large bull's horn, and
2 CONT
2 CONT made a solemn vow to enlarge his dominions by one half, towards
2 CONT
2 CONT all the four corners of the world, or die; and thereupon pointed
2 CONT
2 CONT with the horn to the four quarters. Now when the guests had
2 CONT
2 CONT become drunk towards evening King Ingjald told Svipdag's sons,
2 CONT
2 CONT Gautvid and Hylvid, to arm themselves and their men, as had
2 CONT
2 CONT before been settled; and accordingly they went out, and came up
2 CONT
2 CONT to the new hall, and set fire to it. The hall was soon in a
2 CONT
2 CONT blaze, and the six kings, with all their people, were burned in
2 CONT
2 CONT it. Those who tried to come out were killed. Then King Ingjald
2 CONT
2 CONT laid all the dominions these kings had possessed under himself,
2 CONT
2 CONT and took scatt from them.
2 CONT
2 CONT 41. OF HJORVARD'S MARRIAGE.
2 CONT
2 CONT When King Granmar heard the news of this treachery, he thought
2 CONT
2 CONT the same lot awaited him if he did not take care. The same
2 CONT
2 CONT summer King Hjorvard, who was called Ylfing, came with his fleet
2 CONT
2 CONT to Sweden, and went into a fjord called Myrkva-fjord. When King
2 CONT
2 CONT Granmar heard this he sent a messenger to him to invite him and
2 CONT
2 CONT all his men to a feast. He accepted it willingly; for he had
2 CONT
2 CONT never committed waste in King Granmar's dominions. When he came
2 CONT
2 CONT to the feast he was gladly welcomed. In the evening, when the
2 CONT
2 CONT full bowls went round, as was the custom of kings when they were
2 CONT
2 CONT at home, or in the feasts they ordered to be made, they sat and
2 CONT
2 CONT drank together, a man and woman with each other in pairs, and the
2 CONT
2 CONT rest of the company sat and drank all together. But it was the
2 CONT
2 CONT law among the vikings that all who were at the entertainment
2 CONT
2 CONT should drink together in one company all round. King Hjorvard's
2 CONT
2 CONT high seat was placed right opposite to King Granmar's high seat,
2 CONT
2 CONT and on the same bench sat all his men. King Granmar told his
2 CONT
2 CONT daughter Hildigunn, who was a remarkably beautiful girl, to make
2 CONT
2 CONT ready to carry ale to the vikings. Thereupon she took a silver
2 CONT
2 CONT goblet, filled it, bowed before King Hjorvard, and said, "Success
2 CONT
2 CONT to all Ylfinger: this cup to the memory of Rolf Krake" -- drank
2 CONT
2 CONT out the half, and handed the cup to King Hjorvard. He took the
2 CONT
2 CONT cup, and took her hand, and said she must sit beside him. She
2 CONT
2 CONT says that is not viking fashion to drink two and two with women.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hjorvard replies that it were better for him to make a change,
2 CONT
2 CONT and leave the viking law, and drink in company with her. Then
2 CONT
2 CONT Hildigunn sat down beside him, and both drank together, and spoke
2 CONT
2 CONT a great deal with each other during the evening. The next day,
2 CONT
2 CONT when King Granmar and Hjorvard met, Hjorvard spoke of his
2 CONT
2 CONT courtship, and asked to have Hildigunn in marriage. King Granmar
2 CONT
2 CONT laid this proposal before his wife Hilda, and before people of
2 CONT
2 CONT consequence, saying they would have great help and trust in
2 CONT
2 CONT Hjorvard; and all approved of it highly, and thought it very
2 CONT
2 CONT advisable. And the end was, that Hildigunn was promised to
2 CONT
2 CONT Hjorvard, and the wedding followed soon after; and King Hjorvard
2 CONT
2 CONT stayed with King Granmar, who had no sons, to help him to defend
2 CONT
2 CONT his dominions.
2 CONT
2 CONT 42. WAR BETWEEN INGJALD AND GRANMAR AND HJORVARD.
2 CONT
2 CONT The same autumn King Ingjald collected a war-force, with which he
2 CONT
2 CONT intended to fall upon Granmar and Hjorvard; but when they heard
2 CONT
2 CONT it they also collected a force, and Hogne, who ruled over East
2 CONT
2 CONT Gotland, together with his son Hildur, came to their assistance.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hogne was father of Hilda, who was married to King Granmar. King
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingjald landed with his army, which was by far the most numerous.
2 CONT
2 CONT A battle began, which was very sharp; but after it had lasted a
2 CONT
2 CONT short time, the chiefs who ruled over Fjadryndaland, West
2 CONT
2 CONT Gotland, Nerike, and Aattundaland, took to flight with all the
2 CONT
2 CONT men from those countries, and hastened to their ships. This
2 CONT
2 CONT placed King Ingjald in great danger, and he received many wounds,
2 CONT
2 CONT but escaped by flight to his ships. Svipdag the Blind, Ingjald's
2 CONT
2 CONT foster-father, together with his sons, Gautvid and Hylvid, fell.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingjald returned to Upsal, very ill-satisfied with his
2 CONT
2 CONT expedition; and he thought the army levied from those countries
2 CONT
2 CONT he had acquired by conquest had been unfaithful to him. There
2 CONT
2 CONT was great hostility afterwards between King Ingjald and King
2 CONT
2 CONT Granmar, and his son-in-law King Hjorvard; and after this had
2 CONT
2 CONT continued a long time the friends of both parties brought about a
2 CONT
2 CONT reconciliation. The king appointed a meeting, and concluded a
2 CONT
2 CONT peace. This peace was to endure as long as the three kings
2 CONT
2 CONT lived, and this was confirmed by oath and promises of fidelity.
2 CONT
2 CONT The spring after, King Granmar went to Upsal to make offering, as
2 CONT
2 CONT usual, for a steady peace. Then the foreboding turned out for
2 CONT
2 CONT him so that it did not promise him long life, and he returned to
2 CONT
2 CONT his dominions. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 43. DEATH OF THE KINGS GRANMAR AND HJORVARD.
2 CONT
2 CONT The autumn after, King Granmar and his son-in-law Hjorvard went
2 CONT
2 CONT to a feast at one of their farms in the island Sile. When they
2 CONT
2 CONT were at the entertainment, King Ingjald came there in the night
2 CONT
2 CONT with his troops, surrounded the house, and burnt them in it, with
2 CONT
2 CONT all their men. Then he took to himself all the country these
2 CONT
2 CONT kings had possessed, and placed chiefs over it. King Hogne and
2 CONT
2 CONT his son Hildur often made inroads on horseback into the Swedish
2 CONT
2 CONT dominions, and killed King Ingjald's men, whom he had placed over
2 CONT
2 CONT the kingdom which had belonged to their relation Granmar. This
2 CONT
2 CONT strife between King Ingjald and King Hogne continued for a long
2 CONT
2 CONT time; but King Hogne defended his kingdom against King Ingjald to
2 CONT
2 CONT his dying day. King Ingjald had two children by his wife -- the
2 CONT
2 CONT eldest called Aasa, the other Olaf. Gauthild, the wife of
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingjald, sent the boy to his foster-father Bove, in West Gotland,
2 CONT
2 CONT where he was brought up along with Saxe, Bove's son, who had the
2 CONT
2 CONT surname of Flette. It was a common saying that King Ingjald had
2 CONT
2 CONT killed twelve kings, and deceived them all under pretence of
2 CONT
2 CONT peace; therefore he was called Ingjald the Evil-adviser. He was
2 CONT
2 CONT king over the greater part of Sweden. He married his daughter
2 CONT
2 CONT Aasa to Gudrod king of Scania; and she was like her father in
2 CONT
2 CONT disposition. Aasa brought it about that Gudrod killed his
2 CONT
2 CONT brother Halfdan, father of Ivar Vidfavne; and also she brought
2 CONT
2 CONT about the death of her husband Gudrod, and then fled to her
2 CONT
2 CONT father; and she thus got the name also of Aasa the Evil-adviser. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 44. OF INGJALD'S DEATH.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ivar Vidfavne came to Scania after the fall of his uncle Gudrod,
2 CONT
2 CONT and collected an army in all haste, and moved with it into
2 CONT
2 CONT Sweden. Aasa had gone to her father before. King Ingjald was at
2 CONT
2 CONT a feast in Raening, when he heard that King Ivar's army was in
2 CONT
2 CONT the neighbourhood. Ingjald thought he had not strength to go
2 CONT
2 CONT into battle against Ivar, and he saw well that if he betook
2 CONT
2 CONT himself to flight his enemies would swarm around him from all
2 CONT
2 CONT corners. He and Aasa took a resolution which has become
2 CONT
2 CONT celebrated. They drank until all their people were dead drunk,
2 CONT
2 CONT and then put fire to the hall; and it was consumed, with all who
2 CONT
2 CONT were in it, including themselves, King Ingjald, and Aasa. Thus
2 CONT
2 CONT says Thjodolf: --
2 CONT
2 CONT "With fiery feet devouring flame
2 CONT
2 CONT Has hunted down a royal game
2 CONT
2 CONT At Raening, where King Ingjald gave
2 CONT
2 CONT To all his men one glowing grave.
2 CONT
2 CONT On his own hearth the fire he raised,
2 CONT
2 CONT A deed his foemen even praised;
2 CONT
