2 CONT Ynglingatal and Historia Norwegiae
2 CONT
2 CONT It is interesting to note that the citation from Ynglingatal does not appear to describe Ingjald as an evil king. It calls his life a brave life frœknu fjörvi:
2 CONT
2 CONT    Ok Ingjald
2 CONT    í fjörvan trað
2 CONT    reyks rösuðr
2 CONT    á Ræningi,
2 CONT    þá er húsþjófr
2 CONT    hyrjar leistum
2 CONT    goðkonung
2 CONT    í gegnum steig.
2 CONT    Ok sá urðr
2 CONT    allri þjóðu
2 CONT    sjaldgætastr
2 CONT    með Svíum þótti,
2 CONT    er hann sjálfr
2 CONT    sínu fjörvi
2 CONT    frœknu fyrstr
2 CONT    um fara vildi.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT    With fiery feet devouring flame
2 CONT    Has hunted down a royal game
2 CONT    At Raening, where King Ingjald gave
2 CONT    To all his men one glowing grave.
2 CONT    On his own hearth the fire he raised,
2 CONT    A deed his foemen even praised;
2 CONT    By his own hand he perished so,
2 CONT    And life for freedom did forego."[2]
