1 NOTE RESEARCH NOTES:
2 CONT King of Sweden.  Resided @ Uppsala.  Killed 12 subkings by treachery.
1 NOTE Farfar til Halvdan Kvitebein, den Norske Kongsfar
1 NOTE Sources: RC 166; Kraentzler 1650; AF.  RC: Ingjald Braut-Onundsson "ill-ruler," King of Uppsala in Sweden. The last Fray-born pagan sacol "peace king" with human sacrifice in his own family. 
1 NOTE Enligt Snorres saga lät Ingjar "Illråde" under sin fader Bröt-Anunds gravöl
2 CONT bränna inne flera småkungar för att utvidga sitt eget rike.
2 CONT Han beseg rades själv av Ivar "Vidfamne",
2 CONT och för att slippa tas till fånga lät han sig bli innebränd.
1 NOTE Ingjaldr hinn illráði or Ingjald illråde ("ill-ruler") was a legendary Swedish king of the House of Ynglings. Ingjald may have ruled sometime during the 7th century, and he was the son of the former king Anund.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingjald is mentioned in the Ynglinga saga, Historia Norvegiæ, Hervarar saga, Upplendinga Konungum, Þorsteins saga Víkingssonar and Íslendingabók.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ynglinga saga
2 CONT Snorri Sturluson gave an extensive account on the life of Ingjald in the Ynglinga saga which is part of the Heimskringla.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Youth
2 CONT The Ynglinga saga, a part of the Heimskringla relates that the viceroy of Fjädrundaland was named Ingvar and he had two sons, Alf and Agnar, who were of the same age as Ingjald. Svipdag the Blind was the viceroy of Tiundaland, th
2 CONC e province of Uppsala where the Tings and the Yule (Midwinter) sacrifices were held (see the Temple at Uppsala).
2 CONT
2 CONT One Midwinter, when Ingjald and Alf were six years old, many people had assembled at Uppsala for the sacrifices. Alf and Ingjald played, but Ingjald found that he was the weaker boy and became so angry that he almost started to cr
2 CONC y. His foster-brother Gautvid led him to his foster-father Svigdag the Blind and told Svipdag about Ingjald's lack of manliness and strength. Svipdag said that it was a shame and the next day he gave Ingjald a roasted wolf's heart
2 CONC  to eat. From that day, Ingjald became a very ferocious person and had a bad disposition.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anund arranged a marriage for his son Ingjald with Gauthild, the daughter of the Geatish king Algaut, who was the son of Gautrek the Mild and the grandson of Gaut. Gautrek consented as he believed that Ingjald had inherited his fa
2 CONC ther's disposition. Gauthild's maternal grandfather was Olof the Sharp-sighted, the king of Närke.
2 CONT
2 CONT The deceit
2 CONT Snorri Sturluson relates that when his father Anund had died, Ingjald became the king of Sweden. The kings at Uppsala were the foremost among the kings of the various provinces since Odin ruled the country, and they were the supre
2 CONC me chiefs of the other kingdoms since the death of Agne and Sweden was divided between Erik and Alrik. The descendants of these two kings had spread, cleared land and settled new territories, until there were several petty kings.
2 CONT
2 CONT In honour of his own ascendance to the throne, Ingjald invited the kings, the jarls and other important men to a grand feast in a newly built hall, just as large and sumptuous as the one in Uppsala. It was called the hall of the s
2 CONC even kings and had seven high seats. Algaut the Geatish king of West Götaland, King Ingvar of Fjädrundaland with his two sons Agnar and Alf, King Sporsnjall of Nerike and King Sigvat of Attundaland came but not King Granmar of S
2 CONC ödermanland. The kings filled all seven seats but one. All the prominent people of Sweden had seats, except for Ingjald's own court whom he had sent to his old hall in Uppsala.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the custom of the time for those who inherited kings and jarls, Ingjald rested at the footstool until the Bragebeaker was brought in. Then he was supposed to stand up, take the beaker and make solemn vows, after which
2 CONC  he would ascend his father's high seat. However, when the beaker was brought in, he took a bull's horn and made the solemn vow that he would enlarge his own kingdom by half towards all the four quarters, towards which he pointed 
2 CONC his horn, or die.
2 CONT
2 CONT When all the prominent guests were drunk, he ordered Svipdag's sons, Gautvid and Hylvid, to arm themselves and their men and to leave the building. Outside, they set fire to the building which burnt down and those who tried to esc
2 CONC ape were killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thus Ingjald made himself the sole ruler of the domains of the murdered kings.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wars
2 CONT Granmar won allies in his son-in-law the sea-king Hjörvard of the Ylfings and his father-in-law Högne the Geatish king of East Götaland. They successfully withstood Ingjald's invasion where Ingjald realised that the men from th
2 CONC e provinces he had conquered were not loyal to him. After a long standstill there was peace for as long as the three kings lived. However, one night Ingjald and his men surrounded a farm where Granmar and Hjörvard were at a feast
2 CONC  and burnt the house down. He late disposed of five more kings, and he thus earned the name Illråde (ill-ruler) as he fulfilled his promise.
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorri Sturluson tells that it was a common saying that Ingjald killed twelve kings by deceiving them that he only wished for peace, and that he thus earned his cognomen Illråde (ill-ruler or ill-adviser).
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Downfall
2 CONT
2 CONT Ingjald and his daughter ÅsaIngjald had two children, a son Olof Trätälja and a daughter Åsa. His daughter had inherited her father's psychopathic disposition. She married king Guðröðr of Skåne. Before she murdered her husba
2 CONC nd she managed to make him kill his own brother Halfdan the Valiant, the father of the great Ivar Vidfamne.
2 CONT
2 CONT In order to avenge his father, Ivar Vidfamne gathered a vast host and departed for Sweden, where he found Ingjald at Ræning. When Ingjald and his daughter realized that it was futile to resist, they set the hall on fire and succum
2 CONC bed in the flames.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Ynglingatal and Historia Norwegiae
2 CONT It is interesting to note that the citation from Ynglingatal does not appear to describe Ingjald as an evil king. It calls his life a brave life frœknu fjörvi:
2 CONT
2 CONT Ok Ingjald 
2 CONT í fjörvan trað 
2 CONT reyks rösuðr 
2 CONT á Ræningi, 
2 CONT þá er húsþjófr 
2 CONT hyrjar leistum 
2 CONT goðkonung 
2 CONT í gegnum steig. 
2 CONT Ok sá urðr 
2 CONT allri þjóðu 
2 CONT sjaldgætastr 
2 CONT með Svíum þótti, 
2 CONT er hann sjálfr 
2 CONT sínu fjörvi 
2 CONT frœknu fyrstr 
2 CONT um fara vildi.[1]  With fiery feet devouring flame 
2 CONT Has hunted down a royal game 
2 CONT At Raening, where King Ingjald gave 
2 CONT To all his men one glowing grave. 
2 CONT On his own hearth the fire he raised, 
2 CONT A deed his foemen even praised; 
2 CONT By his own hand he perished so, 
2 CONT And life for freedom did forego."[2]   
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Norwegiæ presents a Latin summary of Ynglingatal, older than Snorri's quotation (continuing after Anund):
2 CONT
2 CONT Post istum filius suus Ingialdr in regem sublimatur, qui ultra modum timens Ivarum cognomine withfadm regem tunc temporis multis formidabilem se ipsum cum omni comitatu suo cenaculo inclusos igne cremavit. Ejus filius Olavus cogno
2 CONC mento tretelgia [...][2]
2 CONT  After him his son Ingjald ascended the throne. Being abnormally terrified of King Ivar Vidfadme, at that time an object of dread to many, he shut himself up in a dining-hall with his whole retinue and burnt all its inmates to dea
2 CONC th. His son, Olav, known as Tretelgje,[...][3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT ^ Hans Gillingstam (1973-1975), "Ingjald Illråde", Svenskt Biografiskt Lexikon, 20  
2 CONT ^ Storm, Gustav (editor) (1880). Monumenta historica Norwegiæ: Latinske kildeskrifter til Norges historie i middelalderen, Monumenta Historica Norwegiae (Kristiania: Brøgger), pp. 101-102. 
2 CONT ^ Ekrem, Inger (editor), Lars Boje Mortensen (editor) and Peter Fisher (translator) (2003). Historia Norwegie. Museum Tusculanum Press. ISBN 8772898135, p. 79. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Primary sources
2 CONT "The Burning at Upsal" in the Ynglinga saga at the Northvegr website. 
2 CONT N. Kershaw's English translation of the Hervarar saga 
2 CONT English translation at Northvegr "Of The Kings of the Uplands" 
2 CONT A translation in English of Þorsteins saga Víkingssonar 
