2 CONT =Accessing Ancient Ireland Historical Records=
2 CONT '''It’s always best to cite primary and secondary historical sources.''' 
2 CONT Several tertiary and quaternary sources cite the following ancient historical sources. These include encyclopedias in book form or online. These sources may have historically accurate and well-sourced articles or pages. However, t
2 CONC heir pages are too often academically insupportable. ('''Note:''' ''The worst websites to cite are online pedigrees or trees that are poorly sourced or provide no sources at all.'')
2 CONT '''''Wikipedia''''' may be a good place to find primary and secondary sources, but its webpages rate as a tertiary sources at best '''''if''''' author(s) are meticulous academic researchers and writers. 
2 CONT The best tertiary source website is The Foundation for Medieval Genealogy. I directly quotes passages from primary and secondary sources. For Ireland:  [http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/IRELAND.htm]
2 CONT An underused but valuable tertiary source is '''''Ireland’s National Biographical Dictionary.'''' [https://www.dib.ie]
2 CONT ==Top-Tier Sources==
2 CONT '''''Annals of Inisfallen''''' – [https://celt.ucc.ie/published/T100004.html] Enter numbers (years + decimals), the into browser search.
2 CONT '''''Annals of the Four Masters''''' – [https://celt.ucc.ie/published/T100005A/index.html] Click “CELT editions as a single file”, then enter the numbers (years + decimals), the into browser search.
2 CONT '''''Annals of Tigernach''''' – [https://celt.ucc.ie/published/T100002A/index.html] Click CELT editions as a single file, then enter the number(s), or year + decimal, the into browser search.
2 CONT '''''Annals of Ulster''''' – [https://celt.ucc.ie/published/T100001A.html] Enter the number(s), or year + decimal, into browser search.
2 CONT '''''Genealogies from Rawlinson B 502''''' – [https://www.ucc.ie/research/celt/published/G105003.html]
2 CONT Enter page number without a space after the period (i.e., p.144 not p. 144)
2 CONT '''''Irish Kings and High-Kings''''' by Francis John Byrne [https://archive.org/details/irishkingshighki00byrn]
