1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT ==Halfdan "the Old" Hringsson, King of Ringerike==
2 CONT
2 CONT * http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halfdan_the_Old
2 CONT
2 CONT The sources for Halfdan are the "Skaldaskaparmål", "Ættartalur" and "Hversum Noregr Byggdist".
2 CONT
2 CONT He is not to be confused with Halfdan Gamle, son of Sveidi, from the Orkney saga.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Halfdan Hringsson "the Old" In Ringerik - was born about 0450 in Ringerike, Buskerud, Norway. He is the son of Hring Raumsson.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Halfdan married Almveigu Eymundsdatter about 0479 in Ringerike, Buskerud, Norway. Almveigu was born about 0455, lived in Holmgarth, Novgorod, Russia. She is the daughter of King Eymund In Holmgard.
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the sagas, Halfdan had 18 sons, nine of them born at a single birth. Quoth Wikipedia:
2 CONT
2 CONT         the first, Thengil [Thengill], who was called Thengil of Men;
2 CONT
2 CONT         the second, Ræsir;
2 CONT
2 CONT         the third, Gram [Gramr];
2 CONT
2 CONT         the fourth, Gylfi;
2 CONT
2 CONT         the fifth, Hilmir;
2 CONT
2 CONT         the sixth, Jöfur [Jǫfurr];
2 CONT
2 CONT         the seventh, Tyggi;
2 CONT
2 CONT         the eighth, Skyli or Skúli;
2 CONT
2 CONT         the ninth, Harri or Herra.
2 CONT
2 CONT These nine brothers became so famous in foraying that, in all records since, their names are used as titles of rank, even as the name of King or that of Jarl. They had no children, and all fell in battle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Halfdan and his wife had nine other sons also; these were:
2 CONT
2 CONT *  Hildir, from whom the Hildings are come;
2 CONT
2 CONT *  Nefir, from whom the Niflungs sprang;
2 CONT
2 CONT *  Audi [Auði], from whom the Ödlings [Ǫðlingar] are come;
2 CONT
2 CONT * Yngvi, from whom the Ynglings are descended; (Skelfir in the "Ættartalur" from "Hversum Noregr Bygdist")
2 CONT
2 CONT * Dag [Dagr], from whom come the Döglings [Dǫglingar];
2 CONT
2 CONT * Bragi, from whom the Bragnings are sprung (that is the race of Halfdan the Generous (Hálfdanr inn mildi);
2 CONT
2 CONT *  Budli [Buðli], from whom the Budlungs [Buðlungar] are come (from the house of the Budlungs Atli and Brynhild [Brynhildr] descended);
2 CONT
2 CONT * the eighth was Lofdi [Lofði], who was a great war-king (that host who were called Lofdar [Lofðar] followed him; his kindred are called Lofdungs [Lofðungar], whence sprang Eylimi, Sigurd Fáfnir's-bane's mother's sire);
2 CONT
2 CONT * the ninth, Sigar [Sigarr], whence come the Siklings: that is the house of Siggeir [Siggeirr], who was son-in-law of Völsung [Vǫlsungr],—and the house of Sigar, who hanged Hagbard [Hagbarðr].
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Halfdan the Old (Old Norse Hálfdanr gamli and Hálfdanr inn gamli) was an ancient, legendary king from whom descended many of the most notable lineages of legend. A second Halfdan the Old is the purported great-grandfather of Ragnv
2 CONC ald Eysteinsson.
2 CONT
2 CONT Hyndluljód
2 CONT
2 CONT The eddic poem Hyndluljód states in verses 14–16:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Of old the noblest     of all was Áli,
2 CONT
2 CONT Before him Halfdan,     foremost of Skjöldungs [Skjǫldungar];
2 CONT
2 CONT Famed were the battles     the hero fought,
2 CONT
2 CONT To the corners of heaven     his deeds were carried.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Strengthened by Eymund [Eymundr],     the strongest of men,
2 CONT
2 CONT Sigtrygg [Sigtryggr] he slew     with the ice-cold sword;
2 CONT
2 CONT His bride was Álmveig [Álmveigr],     the best of women,
2 CONT
2 CONT And eighteen boys     did Álmveig bear him.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Hence come the Skjöldungs,     hence the Skilfings,
2 CONT
2 CONT Hence the Ödlings [Ǫðlingar],     hence the Ynglings,
2 CONT
2 CONT Hence come the free-born,     hence the high-born,
2 CONT
2 CONT The noblest of men     that in Midgard dwell:
2 CONT
2 CONT And all are thy kinsmen,     Óttar, thou fool!"
2 CONT
2 CONT Though Halfdan is himself called a Skjöldung in verse 14, in verse 16 the Skjöldungs are named instead as one of the families that sprang from Halfdan's marriage with Álmveig.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Ættartolur
2 CONT
2 CONT Halfdan and his sons
2 CONT
2 CONT The Ættartolur, the genealogies appended to the Hversu Noregr byggdist in the Flatey Book introduce Halfdan the Old as the ruler of Ringiríki (a territory including modern Ringerike and Valdres in Oppland). Halfdan is here the son
2 CONC  of King Hring (eponym of Ringeríki) by the daughter of a sea-king named Vífil (Vífill). Hring was son of Raum the Old (eponym of Raumaríki) by Hild (Hildr) the daughter Gudröd the Old (Guðrǫðr inn gamli). Raum the Old was son of 
2 CONC Nór (Nórr) (the eponym of Norway). See Nór for further details about Nór and his ancestry and descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his sacrifice Halfdan requested a lifetime of 300 years like that of his ancestor Snær. The form Tiggi appears instead of Tyggi in the list of the first nine sons. The list of the second nine sons has Skelfir instead of Yngvi a
2 CONC nd the form Næfil (Næfill) instead of Nefir. The order of the names is the same and it is explained that Hildir, Sigar, and Lofdi were war-kings; Audi, Budli, and Næfil were sea-kings, while Dag, Skelfir, and Bragi remained on the
2 CONC ir lands.
