1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==concerns==
2 CONT '''This Bröt-Anund is the same as https://www.geni.com/people/Br%C3%B6t-Anund-Ingvarsson-King-of-Uppsala/6000000106938812859.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==brief biography==
2 CONT Konge av Svitjod
2 CONT
2 CONT Småkung i Svealand
2 CONT
2 CONT Konge Svitjord 545-565
2 CONT
2 CONT ==comments==
2 CONT Date and place of birth have also been (erroneously?) reported to be:
2 CONT
2 CONT * circa 570 at an unspecified location
2 CONT * after 570 in Norway
2 CONT
2 CONT Date and place of death have also been (erroneously?) reported to be circa 615 at an unspecified location.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==content to clean up==
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Anund
2 CONT
2 CONT Anund, Swedish: Bröt-Anund meaning trail-blazer Anund or Anund the Land Clearer; alternate names Brøt-Anundr (Old East Norse) or Braut-Önundr (Old West Norse), was a legendary Swedish king of the House of Yngling who reigned in th
2 CONC e mid-seventh century. The name would have been Proto-Norse *Anuwinduz meaning "winning ancestor".[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT In his Ynglinga saga, Snorri Sturluson relates that Anund succeeded his father Ingvar on the Swedish throne, and after his father's wars against Danish Vikings and Estonian pirates, peace reigned over Sweden and there were good ha
2 CONC rvests. Anund was a popular king who became very rich, not only because of the peace and the good harvests but also because he avenged his father in Estonia. That country was ravaged far and wide and in the autumn Anund returned w
2 CONC ith great riches.
2 CONT
2 CONT In those days Sweden was dominated by vast and uninhabited forests, so Anund started making roads and clearing land and vast districts were settled by Swedes. Consequently he was named Bröt-Anund. He made a house for himself in ev
2 CONC ery district and used to stay as a guest in many homes.
2 CONT
2 CONT One autumn, King Anund was travelling between his halls (see Husbys) and came to a place called Himinheiðr (sky heath) between two mountains. He was surprised by a landslide which killed him.
2 CONT
2 CONT After presenting this story of Anund, Snorri Sturluson quotes Þjóðólfr of Hvinir's Ynglingatal:
2 CONT
2 CONT Varð Önundr Jónakrs bura harmi heptr und Himinfjöllum, ok ofvæg Eistra dólgi heipt hrísungs at hendi kom; ok sá frömuðr foldar beinum Högna hrörs um horfinn var.[1] We all have heard how Jonkur's sons, Whom weapons could not touch
2 CONC , with stones Were stoned to death in open day, King Onund died in the same way. Or else perhaps the wood-grown land, Which long had felt his conquering hand, Uprose at length in deadly strife, And pressed out Onund's hated life.[
2 CONC 2] The original text of Ynglingatal is hard to interpret, and it only says that Anund died und Himinfjöllum (under the sky mountains) and that stones were implied. According to Historia Norwegiæ, he was murded by his brother Sigva
2 CONC rd in Himinherthy (which the source says means "the fields of the sky", cœli campus. Such a place name is not known and Birger Nerman suggests that the original place of death was under the sky mountains, i.e. under the clouds (cf
2 CONC . the etymology of cloud). Consequently, he may have been killed outdoors, by his brother and with a stone. In the translation above, Laing has made the same interpretation as Nerman.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thorsteins saga Víkingssonar says that Anund was not the son of Ingvar, but the son of his grandfather Östen. It also relates that he had a brother named Olaf who was the king of Fjordane.
2 CONT
2 CONT All sources say that Anund was the father of the infamous Ingjald ill-ruler.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Notes===
2 CONT * Peterson, Lena (2007). "Lexikon över urnordiska personnamn" (PDF). Swedish Institute for Language and Folklore.(Lexicon of nordic personal names before the 8th century)
2 CONT * Storm informs that he has corrected the name to Himinheithi (sky fields) in his edition, instead of the original Himinherthy.
2 CONT * Storm, Gustav (editor) (1880). Monumenta historica Norwegiæ: Latinske kildeskrifter til Norges historie i middelalderen, Monumenta Historica Norwegiae (Kristiania: Brøgger), p. 101.
2 CONT * Ekrem, Inger (editor), Lars Boje Mortensen (editor) and Peter Fisher (translator) (2003). Historia Norwegie. Museum Tusculanum Press. ISBN 87-7289-813-5, p. 79.
2 CONT ===Primary sources===
2 CONT *Ynglingatal
2 CONT *Ynglinga saga (part of the Heimskringla)
2 CONT *Historia Norwegiæ
2 CONT *Thorsteins saga Víkingssonar
2 CONT ===Secondary sources===
2 CONT *Nerman, B. Det svenska rikets uppkomst. Stockholm, 1925.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT ==ONUND THE LAND-CLEARER.==
2 CONT
2 CONT Onund was the name of Yngvar's son who succeeded him. In his days there was peace in Sweden, and he became rich in valuable goods. King Onund went with his army to Estland to avenge his father, and landed and ravaged the country r
2 CONC ound far and wide, and returned with a great booty in autumn to Sweden. In his time there were fruitful seasons in Sweden, so that he was one of the most popular of kings. Sweden is a great forest land, and there are such great un
2 CONC inhabited forests in it that it is a journey of many days to cross them. Onund bestowed great diligence and expense on opening the woods and cultivating the cleared land. He made roads through the desert forests; and thus cleared 
2 CONC land is found all through the forest country, and great districts are settled. In this way extensive tracts of land were brought into cultivation, for there were country people enough to cultivate the land. Onund had roads made th
2 CONC rough all Sweden, both through forests and morasses, and also over mountains; and he was therefore called Onund Roadmaker. He had a house built for himself in every district of Sweden, and went over the whole country in guest-quar
2 CONC ters.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Events in the life of Braut-Önundr Yngvarsson===
2 CONT
2 CONT '''event 1 .''' succeeded his father to the kingdom at Uppsala 
2 CONT '''† death 1 .''' ·King Onund one autumn, travelling between his mansion-houses, came over a road called Himmenheath, where there are some narrow mountain valleys, with high mountains on both sides. There was heavy rain at the tim
2 CONC e, and before there had been snow on the mountains. A landslip of clay and stones came down upon King Onund and his people, and there he met his death, and many with him. So says Thjodolf, namely: -- "We all have heard how Jonkur'
2 CONC s sons, Whom weapons could not touch, with stones Were stoned to death in open day, King Onund died in the same way. Or else perhaps the wood-grown land, Which long had felt his conquering hand, Uprose at length in deadly strife, 
2 CONC And pressed out Onund's hated life." event 1 . ·applied for his son, Ingjald, to King Algaut, of Gotland, for his daughter Gauthild  '''event 1 .''' ·went with his army to Estland to avenge his father, and landed and ravaged the c
2 CONC ountry round far and wide, and returned with a great booty in autumn to Sweden  '''event 1 .''' ·bestowed great diligence and expense on opening the woods and cultivating the cleared land. He made roads through the desert forests;
2 CONC  and thus cleared land is found all through the forest country, and great districts are settled. In this way extensive tracts of land were brought into cultivation, for there were country people enough to cultivate the land. Onund
2 CONC  had roads made through all Sweden, both through forests and morasses, and also over mountains; and he was therefore called Onund Roadmaker
2 CONT
2 CONT Title: Royal Families of Medieval Scandinavia, Flanders, and Kiev, by Rupert Alen & Anna Dahlquist, 1997, King's River Publ.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.	Note: Page: 4 King Onund one autumn, travelling between his mansion-houses,came over a road called Himmenheath, where there are some narrow mountain valleys, with high mountains on both sides. There was heavy rain at the time, 
2 CONC and before there had been snow on the mountains. A landslip of clay and stones came down upon King Onund and his people, and there he met his death, and many with him. The Cultivator's ancestry is provided elsewhere on this tree. 
2 CONC The Cultivator's father was Yngvar (The Tall) Eysteinsson and his mother was <Unknown>. His paternal grandparents were Eystein Adilsson and <Unknown>. He had a brother named Skirta. He was the younger of the two children.
2 CONT
2 CONT  <nowiki>--------------------</nowiki> 
2 CONT
2 CONT Peaceful & prosperous rein. Very popular. Cleared the great forests of Sweden to make roads, areas for cultivation & a house for himself in every section of Sweden, thusly became known asOnund Roadmaker'. One autumn, traveling bet
2 CONC ween his mansion-houses, came over a road called Himmenheath, where there are some narrow mountain valleys with high mountains on both sides. There was heavy rain at the time & before there had been snow on the mountains a landsli
2 CONC p of clay & stones came down upon Onund & his people where he met his death (& many with him).
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT  From http://www.rpi.edu/~holmes/Hobbies/Genealogy/ps18/ps18_353.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT -------- 
2 CONT
2 CONT Braut Onund Ingvarsson (b. 636)Braut Onund Ingvarsson (son of Ingvar Eysteinsson) was born 636 in Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes for Braut Onund Ingvarsson:
2 CONT
2 CONT In his days there was peace in Sweden, and he became rich in valuable goods. King Onund went with his army to Estland to avenge his father,landed with his army and ravaged the country round far and wide, took a great booty and ret
2 CONC urned in autumn to Sweden. In his time there were fruitful seasons in Sweden. Onund was the most popular of kings. Sweden is a great forest land, and there are such great uninhabited forests in it that it is a journey of many days
2 CONC  to cross them. Onund bestowed great diligence and expense on opening the woods and cultivating the cleared land. He also made roads through the desert forests; and thus cleared land is found all through the forest country, and gr
2 CONC eat districts are settled. In this way extensive tracts of land were brought into cultivation, for there were country people enough to cultivate the land. Onund had roads made through all Sweden,both through forests and morasses, 
2 CONC and also over mountains; and he was therefore called Braut-Onund (Onund Land-clearer). King Onund had a house built for himself in every district of Sweden, and went over the whole country in guest-quarters.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Braut Onund Ingvarsson are:
2 CONT
2 CONT +Ingjald Braut-Onundson.
2 CONT
