1 NOTE Begrav i ett bergskred
1 NOTE Brøt-Anundr (Old East Norse) or Braut-Önundr (Old West Norse) (meaning trail-blazer Anund or Anund the land-clearer) was a legendary Swedish king of the House of Yngling who reigned in the mid-seventh century. The name would have
2 CONC  been Proto-Norse *Anuwinduz [1], meaning "winning ancestor".
2 CONT
2 CONT In his Ynglinga saga, Snorri Sturluson relates that Anund succeeded his father Ingvar on the Swedish throne, and after his father's wars against Danish vikings and Estonian pirates, peace reigned over Sweden and there were good ha
2 CONC rvests. Anund was a popular king who became very rich, not only because of the peace and the good harvests but also because he avenged his father in Estonia. That country was ravaged far and wide and in the autumn Anund returned w
2 CONC ith great riches.
2 CONT
2 CONT In those days Sweden was dominated by vast and uninhabited forests, so Anund started making roads and clearing land and vast districts were settled by Swedes. Consequently he was named Bröt-Anund. He made a house for himself in e
2 CONC very district and used to stay as a guest in many homes.
2 CONT
2 CONT One autumn, King Anund was travelling between his halls (see Husbys) and came to a place called Himinheiðr (sky heath) between two mountains. He was surprised by a landslide which killed him.
2 CONT
2 CONT After presenting this story of Anund, Snorri Sturluson quotes Þjóðólfr of Hvinir's Ynglingatal:
2 CONT
2 CONT Varð Önundr 
2 CONT Jónakrs bura 
2 CONT harmi heptr 
2 CONT und Himinfjöllum, 
2 CONT ok ofvæg 
2 CONT Eistra dólgi 
2 CONT heipt hrísungs 
2 CONT at hendi kom; 
2 CONT ok sá frömuðr 
2 CONT foldar beinum 
2 CONT Högna hrörs 
2 CONT um horfinn var.[2]  We all have heard how Jonkur's sons, 
2 CONT Whom weapons could not touch, with stones 
2 CONT Were stoned to death in open day, 
2 CONT King Onund died in the same way. 
2 CONT Or else perhaps the wood-grown land, 
2 CONT Which long had felt his conquering hand, 
2 CONT Uprose at length in deadly strife, 
2 CONT And pressed out Onund's hated life.[3]   
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Norwegiæ presents a Latin summary of Ynglingatal, older than Snorri's quotation (continuing after Ingvar):
2 CONT
2 CONT Iste ergo genuit Broutonund, quem Sigwardus frater suus occidit in Himinheithi[1], quod loci vocabulum interpretatur coeli campus. Post istum filius suus Ingialdr [...][2].
2 CONT  Yngvar bred Braut-Ånund, whose brother, Sigurd, laid him low in Himinheid, a place-name which means 'field of heaven'. After him his son Ingjald [...][3]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The original text of Ynglingatal is hard to interprete, and it only says that Anund died und Himinfjöllum (under the sky mountains) and that stones were implied. According to Historia Norwegiae, he was murded by his brother Sigva
2 CONC rd in Himinherthy (which the source says means "the fields of the sky", cœli campus. Such a place name is not known and Birger Nerman suggests that the original place of death was under the sky mountains, i.e. under the clouds (cf
2 CONC . the etymology of cloud). Consequently, he may have been killed outdoors, by his brother and with a stone. In the translation above, Laing has made the same interpretation as Nerman.
2 CONT
2 CONT Thorsteins saga Víkingssonar says that Anund was not the son of Ingvar, but the son of his grandfather Östen. It also relates that he had a brother named Olaf who was the king of Fjordane.
2 CONT
2 CONT All sources say that Anund was the father of the infamous Ingjald ill-ruler.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Notes
2 CONT ^ Storm informs that he has corrected the name to Himinheithi (sky fields) in his edition, instead of the original Himinherthy. 
2 CONT ^ Storm, Gustav (editor) (1880). Monumenta historica Norwegiæ: Latinske kildeskrifter til Norges historie i middelalderen, Monumenta Historica Norwegiae (Kristiania: Brøgger), p. 101. 
2 CONT ^ Ekrem, Inger (editor), Lars Boje Mortensen (editor) and Peter Fisher (translator) (2003). Historia Norwegie. Museum Tusculanum Press. ISBN 8772898135, p. 79. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Primary sources
2 CONT Ynglingatal 
2 CONT Ynglinga saga (part of the Heimskringla) 
2 CONT Historia Norwegiae 
2 CONT Thorsteins saga Víkingssonar 
2 CONT
2 CONT Secondary sources
2 CONT Nerman, B. Det svenska rikets uppkomst. Stockholm, 1925.
1 NOTE "Braut-Anund" Ingvarsson, roi de Uppland de Varmland, ( 645 - ?) King of Upsal, roi de Suède 545-565 ?
2 CONT
2 CONT La dynastie des rois de Uppland est suivie jusqu'en 420.... 
2 CONT
2 CONT Onund plundered and ravaged in Estland (Estonia) to avenge his father. 
2 CONT He became very rich and popular with the people. He expanded his kingdom by clearing the forest and cultivating the land.
2 CONT He ordered to built roads to connect the settled districts and was known as "Onund the Road Maker".
2 CONT He died in a landslide.
2 CONT
2 CONT La dynastie des rois de Uppland est suivie jusqu'en 420....
