1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Olav den klarsynte (Ólaf hinn skyggna)
2 CONT
2 CONT (In English: Olaf the Sharp-sighted)
2 CONT
2 CONT King in Närike (Sweden)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://lind.no/nor/index.asp?lang=gb&emne=nor&person=Olav%20den%20klarsynte
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Olof the Sharp-sighted (Óláfr inn skyggni) whose granddaughter married Ingjald ill-ruler of the House of Yngling according to the Heimskringla. According to Gautreks saga and Upplendinga Konungum, he aided the Norwegian king Vikar
2 CONC  in a war together with Starkad. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sporsnjall, a form of Fornsnjallr, (in Heimskringla) who was invited by Ingjald to a banquet only to be burnt to death inside Ingjald's hall with a number of other petty kings. Sporsjall appears as Sporsnäll in later legends from 
2 CONC Närke. 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT  Närke (help·info) is a traditional Swedish province or landskap in middle Sweden. It borders Västergötland, Värmland, Västmanland, Södermanland and Östergötland. On Närke's 4,100 km² surface area, 193,857 people live.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Contents [hide]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1 Name
2 CONT
2 CONT 2 Administration
2 CONT
2 CONT 3 Heraldry
2 CONT
2 CONT 4 Geography
2 CONT
2 CONT 5 History 
2 CONT
2 CONT 5.1 Dukes of Närke
2 CONT
2 CONT 6 Culture
2 CONT
2 CONT 7 Historical subdivisions
2 CONT
2 CONT 8 References 
2 CONT
2 CONT 8.1 Footnotes
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Name
2 CONT
2 CONT The name of the province (Neeric 1165-81) comes from an old word när (narrow) which refers to the narrow ridge where the church of Norrbyås (Nerboahs 1275) is situated. What the rest of the name means is not clear[2]. In English s
2 CONC ometimes also Nerike (an archaic spelling of the province) and Nericia (the Latin name) is used for the province.
2 CONT
2 CONT Riseberga Monastery Ruin in Lekeberg 
2 CONT
2 CONT View over the surroundings of Örebro, Närke[edit] Administration
2 CONT
2 CONT The traditional provinces of Sweden serve no administrative or political purposes, but are historical and cultural entities. In the case of Närke, the province makes up the southern part of Örebro County.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Heraldry
2 CONT
2 CONT Arms granted in 1560, and revised a decade or so later with the current one. The arms is represented with a ducal coronet. At the centre there are two crossbow darts was - the crossbow being the favourite weapon of the peasants. B
2 CONC lazon: "Gules between four Roses Argent two Arrows in saltire Or points upward pointed Argent." The Närke arms is one of the components of the coat of arms for Örebro County, granted in 1944.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Geography
2 CONT
2 CONT Highest mountain: Kilsbergen
2 CONT
2 CONT Largest lake: Vättern
2 CONT
2 CONT National parks: Garphyttan
2 CONT
2 CONT The oldest city in Närke was Örebro, receiving its privileges around 1200. After that, Askersund was chartered in 1643 and Kumla in 1942. City status in Sweden was discontinued as of 1971, so these are historical titles.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] History
2 CONT
2 CONT Main article: History of Närke
2 CONT
2 CONT Norse mythology mentions three kings of Närke, Nidud, Olof the Sharp-sighted and Sporsnjall. Nidud was a villainous king in the lay of Wayland the smith, Olaf aided the Norwegian king Vikar in battle, and Sporsnjall was burnt to d
2 CONC eath by Ingjald Ill-ruler together with five other petty kings.
2 CONT
2 CONT The exiled Norwegian King Olaf the Holy, stayed in Närke for some time, 1028-1029, with Sigtrygg, before departing for Russia and its ruler Yaroslav I the Wise. Olaf may have initiated the Christianization of the province (see als
2 CONC o Culture of Närke).
2 CONT
2 CONT In the year 1170, Närke was incorporated into the diocese of Strängnäs.
2 CONT
2 CONT In around 1200 A.D., the Castle of Örebro was built to protect the bridge crossing the ford (öre means "sand bank" and bro means "bridge"), as well as the borough on the south side of the bridge. It was to withstand many sieges, a
2 CONC nd acquired a reputation for being impregnable.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1316 the future Saint Birgitta of Sweden was married to Ulf Gudmarson, lord of Närke, to whom she bore eight children, one of whom was afterwards honoured as St Catherine of Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1435, Engelbrekt Engelbrektsson, the leader of a popular uprising against the Danish bailiiffs (fogdar), was given the castle of Örebro as a fief. In the following year he was assassinated.
2 CONT
2 CONT From 1525 to 1554, it was a fiefdom belonging to Lars Siggesson Sparre. Later in belonged to Duke Charles from 1560 to 1598 and to his son Duke Charles Philip from 1611 to 1622.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the 16th and 17th centuries iron bars were manufactured in the territory, which was under a number of fiefs. As the farming activities yielded very little, the iron manufacture became even more attractive to the farmers, as
2 CONC  well as trade with oxen. The oxen were sold in Bergslagen as well as Dalarna, where the skin of the oxen were important items in the mines.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1810 It was decided in Örebro castle that Jean Baptiste Bernadotte (Karl XIV Johan) should be the next king of Sweden
2 CONT
2 CONT The surplus of oxen was also one of the reasons why the shoe-making business took shape and became a most important industry in Närke up until the middle of the 20th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Närke was renowned for its warlike peasants and when coat-of-arms for the province was granted for the funeral of Gustav Vasa in 1560 two crossbow darts was the central symbol - the crossbow being the favourite weapon of the peasa
2 CONC nts.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Närke there are also a number of medieval castles. The most important and best preserved one is located in Tarsta at Sköllersta.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Dukes of Närke
2 CONT
2 CONT Since 1772, Swedish Princes have been created Dukes of various provinces. This is solely a nominal title.
2 CONT
2 CONT Prince Eugén (1865-1947)
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Culture
2 CONT
2 CONT Main article: Culture of Närke
2 CONT
2 CONT The province of Närke has 5,200 ancient remains.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Historical subdivisions
2 CONT
2 CONT The old subdivisions of provinces were the Hundreds of Sweden. In Närke, the hundreds were:
2 CONT
2 CONT Asker Hundred
2 CONT
2 CONT Edsberg Hundred
2 CONT
2 CONT Glanshammar Hundred
2 CONT
2 CONT Grimsten Hundred
2 CONT
2 CONT Hardemo Hundred
2 CONT
2 CONT Kumla Hundred
2 CONT
2 CONT Noraskoga Mountain District (transferred to Västmanland in 1617)
2 CONT
2 CONT Skyllersta Hundred
2 CONT
2 CONT Sundbo Hundred
2 CONT
2 CONT Örebro Hundred
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] References
2 CONT
