1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ==concerns==
2 CONT '''This Eystein is the same as https://www.geni.com/people/Eystein-kr%C3%A1ku-Konungurinn-Adilsson-King-of-Uppsala/6000000106941281836.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==brief biography==
2 CONT King of Uppsala
2 CONT
2 CONT ==comments==
2 CONT Date and place of birth have also been (erroneously?) reported to be circa 510 at Västerås, Västmanland, Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT Date and place of death have also been (erroneously?) reported to be circa 600 at an unspecified location.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==content to clean up==
2 CONT Eysteinn (d. ca 600) was the son of Eadgils and Yrsa of Saxony. He was the father of Ingvar.
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorri Sturluson relates that Eysteinn ruled Sweden at the time when Hrólf Kraki died in Lejre. It was a troubled time when many seakings ravaged the Swedish shores. One of those kings was named Sölve and he was from Jutland (but 
2 CONC according to Historia Norwegiae he was Geatish, see below). At this time Sölve was pillaging in the Baltic Sea and so he arrived in Lofond (probably the island of '''Lovön''' or the Lagunda Hundred), where Eysteinn was at a feast.
2 CONC  It was night-time and Sölve and his men surrounded the house and set it on fire burning everyone inside to death. Then Sölve arrived at Sigtuna (Old Sigtuna) and ordered the Swedes to accept him as king. The Swedes refused and ga
2 CONC thered an army that fought against Sölve and his men, but they lost after eleven days. The Swedes had to accept him as king for a while until they rebelled and killed him.
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://maps.google.se/maps?q=Lov%C3%B6n+,eker%C3%B6+,+stockholm&hl=sv&ie=UTF8&ll=59.322856,17.849693&spn=0.058942,0.209255&sll=47.502235,16.783631&sspn=0.078046,0.209255&t=h&hnear=Lov%C3%B6n&z=13 Lovön, Ekerö, Stockholm] 
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorri then quotes a stanza from Þjóðólfr of Hvinir's Ynglingatal:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Veit ek Eysteins
2 CONT     enda fólginn
2 CONT     lokins lífs
2 CONT     á Lofundi;
2 CONT     ok sikling
2 CONT     með Svíum kváðu
2 CONT     Jótska menn
2 CONT     inni brenna.
2 CONT     Ok bitsótt
2 CONT     í brandnói
2 CONT     hlíðar þangs
2 CONT     á hilmi rann,
2 CONT     þá er timbrfastr
2 CONT     toptar nökkvi,
2 CONT     flotna fullr
2 CONT     um fylki brann.[1]	
2 CONT
2 CONT     For a long time none could tell
2 CONT     How Eystein died – but now I know
2 CONT     That at Lofond the hero fell;
2 CONT     The branch of Odin was laid low,
2 CONT     Was burnt by Solve's Jutland men.
2 CONT     The raging tree-devourer fire
2 CONT     Rushed on the monarch in its ire;
2 CONT     First fell the castle timbers, then
2 CONT     The roof-beams – Eystein's funeral pyre.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Norwegiæ presents a Latin summary of Ynglingatal, older than Snorri's quotation (continuing after Eadgils, called Adils or Athisl):
2 CONT
2 CONT Hic [Adils vel Athisl] genuit Eustein, quem Gautones in domo quadam obtrusum cum suis vivum incenderunt. Hujus filius Ynguar [...][1]. 	
2 CONT He [Adils] became sire to Øystein, whom the Götar thrust into a house and incinerated alive there with his men. His son Yngvar, [...][2] 	
2 CONT Thorsteins saga Víkingssonar makes Eysteinn the father of Anund and grandfather of Ingjald and consequently skips Ingvar's generation. It adds a second son to Eysteinn named Olaf, who was the king of Fjordane in Norway.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT 34. ROLF KRAKE'S DEATH.
2 CONT
2 CONT Eystein, King Adils' son, ruled next over Sweden, and in his lifetime Rolf Krake of Leidre fell. In those days many kings, both Danes and Northmen, ravaged the Swedish dominions; for there were many sea-kings who ruled over many p
2 CONC eople, but had no lands, and he might well be called a sea-king who never slept beneath sooty roof-timbers.
2 CONT
2 CONT 35. OF EYSTEIN AND THE JUTLAND KING SOLVE.
2 CONT
2 CONT There was a sea-king called Solve, a son of Hogne of Njardo, who at that time plundered in the Baltic, but had his dominion in Jutland. He came with his forces to Sweden, just as King Eystein was at a feast in a district called Lo
2 CONC fond. Solve came unexpectedly in the night on Eystein, surrounded the house in which the king was, and burned him and all his court. Then Solve went to Sigtun, and desired that the Swedes should receive him, and give him the title
2 CONC  of king; but they collected an army, and tried to defend the country against him, on which there was a great battle, that lasted, according to report, eleven days. There King Solve was victorious, and was afterwards king of the S
2 CONC wedish dominions for a long time, until at last the Swedes betrayed him, and he was killed. Thjodolf tells of it thus: --
2 CONT
2 CONT "For a long time none could tell How Eystein died -- but now I know That at Lofond the hero fell; The branch of Odin was laid low, Was burnt by Solve's Jutland men. The raging tree-devourer fire Rushed on the monarch in its ire; F
2 CONC irst fell the castle timbers, then The roof-beams -- Eystein's funeral pyre."
2 CONT
2 CONT *********************** Events in the life of Eysteinn Adilsson
2 CONT
2 CONT † death 1 . ·There was a sea-king called Solve, a son of Hogne of Njardo, who at that time plundered in the Baltic, but had his dominion in Jutland. He came with his forces to Sweden, just as King Eystein was at a feast in a distr
2 CONC ict called Lofond. Solve came unexpectedly in the night on Eystein, surrounded the house in which the king was, and burned him and all his court. event 1 . ·succeeded his father to the kingdom of Uppsala
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Øystein. Ved denne Tid brugte Kongerne, forat underholde sine Hofmænd, at reise omkring til de Rigeste i Landet paa Gjæsterie eller Veitslo. Da Øystein saaledes opholdt sig paa Løvøen, blev han overfalden og fældet af den norske S
2 CONC jøkonge Sølve. Svenskerne ikke nøjde hermed, holdt mod ham et Slag, som varede i elleve Dage. Sølve seirede dog, og herskede en Tid i Upsala, men ihjelsloges omsider af Øysteins Søn. 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Øystein Adilsson var ein konge av Ynglingeætta, skildra hjå Snorre Sturlason og i Den eldste Noregshistoria. Øystein skal ha levd på den tida Rolv Krake fall. Han var son av kong Adils, som er nemnd i fleire kjelder.
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorre fortel korleis Øystein rådde i ei tid med mykje ufred. Mellom anna hadde Øystein vanskar med ein kong Solve frå Nærøy, som hadde jord på Jylland og i Sverige. Solve kringsette halla til Øystein og brende han inne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tjodolv frå Kvine segjer:
2 CONT
2 CONT Øystein veit eg  enden fann  på live i Lovund,  so lagnaden vilde.  Og juten, kvad dei,  inne brende  Stor-kongen  med svia-menn.  Den som skogen  øyder av  på hovdingen fauk  i heimen hans eigin  då den trauste  timber-stogo  av 
2 CONC kjempur full  yvi kongen brann.  Yngvar Øysteinsson hemna far sin, seier Snorre.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Den eldste Noregshistoria [endre] Etter denne framstillinga var det gautane som brende Øystein inne. "Juten" i kvadet over er mistydd av Snorre. Dette fell inn i ei problemstilling som og er nemnd i Béowulf, der det er strid mell
2 CONC om svear og gautar.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henta frå «http://nn.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%98ystein_Adilsson»
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Noteringar  Sveakonung i G:a Uppsala under senare hälften av 500-talet. Son till Adils den store och Yrsa av Venden. Under hans tid härjade både danskarna och norrmännen i Svea rike. Även sjökkonungar utan land kom hit och plundra
2 CONC de. En sådan, Sölve son till Högne på Njardö, härjade i österled. Han hade ett eget rike i Jutland. Kung Östen var ute på gästning i Lovund när Sölve kom med sin flotta och överföll honom mitt i natten. Kung Östen blev innebränd m
2 CONC ed hela sin hird. Sedan drog Sölve till Sigtuna och krävde att få bli kung. Kung Östen höglades i Lovund, tätt intill nuvarande Enköpingsvägen.  
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Eysteinn (d. ca 600), Swedish: Östen, was the son of Eadgils  and Yrsa of Saxony. He was the father of Ingvar. The Eysteinn tumulus (Östens hög) in Västerås  near Östanbro has been linked to King Eysteinn by some popular historian
2 CONC s. The term Hög is derived from the Old Norse word haugr meaning mound or barrow.
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorri Sturluson relates that Eysteinn ruled Sweden at the time when Hrólf Kraki died in Lejre. It was a troubled time when many sea kings ravaged the Swedish shores. One of those kings was named Sölve and he was from Jutland (but
2 CONC  according to Historia Norwegiae he was Geatish, see below). At this time Sölve was pillaging in the Baltic Sea and so he arrived in Lofond (probably the island of Lovön or the Lagunda Hundred), where Eysteinn was at a feast. It w
2 CONC as night-time and Sölve and his men surrounded the house and set it on fire burning everyone inside to death. Then Sölve arrived at Sigtuna (Old Sigtuna) and ordered the Swedes to accept him as king. The Swedes refused and gathere
2 CONC d an army that fought against Sölve and his men, but they lost after eleven days. The Swedes had to accept him as king for a while until they rebelled and killed him
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%96sten
2 CONT --------------------
