2 CONT
2 CONT Note: There was a sea-king called Solve, a son of Hogne of Njardo, who at that time plundered in the Baltic, but had his dominion in Jutland. He came with his forces to Sweden, just as King Eystein was at a feast in a district cal
2 CONC led Lofond. Solve came unexpectedly in the night on Eystein, surrounded the house in which the king was, and burned him and all his court. Then Solve went to Sigtun, and desired that the Swedes should receive him, and give him the
2 CONC  title of king; but they collected an army, and tried to defend the country against him, on which there was a great battle, that lasted, according to report, eleven days. There King Solve was victorious, and was afterwards king of
2 CONC  the Swedish dominions for a long time, until at last the Swedes betrayed him, and he was killed. Thjodolf tells of it thus: -- "For a long time none could tell How Eystein died -- but now I know That at Lofond the hero fell; The 
2 CONC branch of Odin was laid low, Was burnt by Solve's Jutland men. The raging tree-devourer fire Rushed on the monarch in its ire; First fell the castle timbers, then The roof-beams -- Eystein's funeral pyre.[Ayres.FBC.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------- Eysteinn (Swedish: Östen; died ca 600) was the son of Eadgils and Yrsa of Saxony. He was the father of Ingvar. The Eysteinn tumulus (Östens hög) in Västerås near Östanbro has been linked to King Eysteinn by so
2 CONC me popular historians. The term Hög is derived from the Old Norse word haugr meaning mound or barrow.
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorri Sturluson relates that Eysteinn ruled Sweden at the time when Hrólf Kraki died in Lejre. It was a troubled time when many sea kings ravaged the Swedish shores. One of those kings was named Sölve and he was from Jutland (but
2 CONC  according to Historia Norwegiae he was Geatish, see below). At this time Sölve was pillaging in the Baltic Sea and so he arrived in Lofond (probably the island of Lovön or the Lagunda Hundred), where Eysteinn was at a feast. It w
2 CONC as night-time and Sölve and his men surrounded the house and set it on fire burning everyone inside to death. Then Sölve arrived at Sigtuna (Old Sigtuna) and ordered the Swedes to accept him as king. The Swedes refused and gathere
2 CONC d an army that fought against Sölve and his men, but they lost after eleven days. The Swedes had to accept him as king for a while until they rebelled and killed him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorri then quotes a stanza from Þjóðólfr of Hvinir's Ynglingatal:
2 CONT
2 CONT Veit ek Eysteins
2 CONT
2 CONT enda fólginn
2 CONT
2 CONT lokins lífs
2 CONT
2 CONT á Lofundi;
2 CONT
2 CONT ok sikling
2 CONT
2 CONT með Svíum kváðu
2 CONT
2 CONT Jótska menn
2 CONT
2 CONT inni brenna.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ok bitsótt
2 CONT
2 CONT í brandnói
2 CONT
2 CONT hlíðar þangs
2 CONT
2 CONT á hilmi rann,
2 CONT
2 CONT þá er timbrfastr
2 CONT
2 CONT toptar nökkvi,
2 CONT
2 CONT flotna fullr
2 CONT
2 CONT um fylki brann.[1] For a long time none could tell
2 CONT
2 CONT How Eystein died – but now I know
2 CONT
2 CONT That at Lofond the hero fell;
2 CONT
2 CONT The branch of Odin was laid low,
2 CONT
2 CONT Was burnt by Solve's Jutland men.
2 CONT
2 CONT The raging tree-devourer fire
2 CONT
2 CONT Rushed on the monarch in its ire;
2 CONT
2 CONT First fell the castle timbers, then
2 CONT
2 CONT The roof-beams – Eystein's funeral pyre.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Norwegiæ presents a Latin summary of Ynglingatal, older than Snorri's quotation (continuing after Eadgils, called Adils or Athisl):
2 CONT
2 CONT Hic [Adils vel Athisl] genuit Eustein, quem Gautones in domo quadam obtrusum cum suis vivum incenderunt. Hujus filius Ynguar [...][1].
2 CONT
2 CONT He [Adils] became sire to Øystein, whom the Götar thrust into a house and incinerated alive there with his men. His son Yngvar, [...][2] Thorsteins saga Víkingssonar makes Eysteinn the father of Anund and grandfather of Ingjald an
2 CONC d consequently skips Ingvar's generation. It adds a second son to Eysteinn named Olaf, who was the king of Fjordane in Norway.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.^ Storm, Gustav (editor) (1880). Monumenta historica Norwegiæ: Latinske kildeskrifter til Norges historie i middelalderen, Monumenta Historica Norwegiae (Kristiania: Brøgger), p. 101.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.^ Ekrem, Inger (editor), Lars Boje Mortensen (editor) and Peter Fisher (translator) (2003). Historia Norwegie. Museum Tusculanum Press. ISBN 8772898135, p. 79.
2 CONT
