2 CONT [edit] Beowulf
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Old English poem Beowulf the name of Ohthere only appears in constructions referring to his father Ongenþeow (fæder Ohtheres),[3] mother (Onelan modor and Ohtheres),[4] and his sons Eadgils (suna Ohteres,[5] sunu Ohteres[6]
2 CONC ) and Eanmund (suna Ohteres).[7]
2 CONT
2 CONT When Ohthere and his actions are concerned, he is referred to as Ongenþeow's offspring together with his brother Onela. The section deals with Ohthere and Onela pillaging the Geats at the death of their king Hreðel, restarting the
2 CONC  Swedish-Geatish wars:
2 CONT
2 CONT Þa wæs synn and sacu Sweona and Geata,
2 CONT
2 CONT ofer wid wæter wroht gemæne,
2 CONT
2 CONT here-nið hearda, syððan Hreðel swealt,
2 CONT
2 CONT oððe him Ongenþeowes eaferan wæran
2 CONT
2 CONT frome fyrd-hwate, freode ne woldon
2 CONT
2 CONT ofer heafo healdan, ac ymb Hreosna-beorh
2 CONT
2 CONT eatolne inwit-scear oft gefremedon.[8] There was strife and struggle 'twixt Swede and Geat
2 CONT
2 CONT o'er the width of waters; war arose,
2 CONT
2 CONT hard battle-horror, when Hrethel died,
2 CONT
2 CONT and Ongentheow's offspring grew
2 CONT
2 CONT strife-keen, bold, nor brooked o'er the seas
2 CONT
2 CONT pact of peace, but pushed their hosts
2 CONT
2 CONT to harass in hatred by Hreosnabeorh.[9]  
2 CONT
2 CONT Later, it is implied in the poem that Ohthere has died, because his brother Onela is king. Ohthere's sons Eadgils and Eanmund fled to the Geats and the wars began anew.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Scandinavian sources
2 CONT
2 CONT Ynglingatal, Ynglinga saga, Íslendingabók and Historia Norvegiae all present Óttarr as the son of Egill (called Ongenþeow in Beowulf) and as the father of Aðísl/Aðils/athils/Adils (Eadgils).
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the latest source, Ynglinga saga, Óttarr refused to pay tribute to the Danish king Fróði for the help that his father had received. Then Fróði sent two men to collect the tribute, but Óttarr answered that the Swedes h
2 CONC ad never paid tribute to the Daner and would not begin with him. Fróði then gathered a vast host and looted in Sweden, but the next summer he pillaged in the east. When Óttarr learnt that Fróði was gone, he sailed to Denmark to pl
2 CONC under in return and went into the Limfjord where he pillaged in Vendsyssel. Fróði's jarls Vott and Faste attacked Óttarr in the fjord. The battle was even and many men fell, but the Daner were reinforced by the people in the neigh
2 CONC bourhood and so the Swedes lost (a version apparently borrowed from the death of Óttarr's predecessor Jorund). The Daner put Óttarr's dead corpse on a mound to be devoured by wild beasts, and made a wooden crow that they sent to S
2 CONC weden with the message that the wooden crow was all that Óttarr was worth. After this, Óttarr was called Vendelcrow.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is only Snorri who uses the epithet Vendelcrow, whereas the older sources Historia Norvegiae and Íslendingabók use it for his father Egill. Moreover, it is only in Snorri's work that story of Óttarr's death in Vendsyssel appear
2 CONC s, and it is probably his own invention.[1] Ynglingatal only mentions that Óttarr was killed by the Danish jarls Vott and Faste in a place named Vendel (Laing has been influenced by Snorri's version in his translation):
2 CONT
2 CONT Féll Óttarr
2 CONT
2 CONT und ara greipar
2 CONT
2 CONT dugandligr
2 CONT
2 CONT fyrir Dana vápnum,
2 CONT
2 CONT þann hergammr
2 CONT
2 CONT hrægum fœti
2 CONT
2 CONT viti borinn
2 CONT
2 CONT á Vendli sparn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Þau frá ek verk
2 CONT
2 CONT Vötts ok Fasta
2 CONT
2 CONT sœnskri þjóð
2 CONT
2 CONT at sögum verða;
2 CONT
2 CONT at eylands
2 CONT
2 CONT jarlar Fróða
2 CONT
2 CONT vígframað
2 CONT
2 CONT um veginn höfðu.[10] By Danish arms the hero bold,
2 CONT
2 CONT Ottar the Brave, lies stiff and cold.
2 CONT
2 CONT To Vendel's plain the corpse was borne;
2 CONT
2 CONT By eagles' claws the corpse is torn,
2 CONT
2 CONT Spattered by ravens' bloody feet,
2 CONT
2 CONT The wild bird's prey, the wild wolf's meat.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Swedes have vowed revenge to take
2 CONT
2 CONT On Frode's earls, for Ottar's sake;
2 CONT
2 CONT Like dogs to kill them in their land,
2 CONT
2 CONT In their own homes, by Swedish hand.[11]  
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Norwegiæ presents a Latin summary of Ynglingatal, older than Snorri's quotation (continuing after Egil):
2 CONT
2 CONT Cui successit in regnum filius suus Ottarus, qui a suo æquivoco Ottaro Danorum comite et fratre ejus Fasta in una provinciarum Daniæ, scilicet Wendli, interemptus est. Cujus filius Adils [...][12]
2 CONT
2 CONT  The successor to the throne was his son Ottar, who was assassinated in Vendel, a law province of Denmark, by his namesake, a Danish jarl, and this man's brother, Fasta. His son Adils [...][13]
2 CONT
2 CONT Historia Norvegiæ only informs that Ohthere was killed by the Danish brothers Ottar [sic.] and Faste in a Danish province called Vendel.
2 CONT
2 CONT [edit] Ohthere's Barrow
2 CONT
2 CONT Ohthere's barrow (Swedish: Ottarshögen) (60°08′N 17°34′E﻿ / ﻿60.133°N 17.567°E﻿ / 60.133; 17.567) is located in Vendel parish, Uppland, Sweden. The barrow is 5 metres high and 40 metres wide. In the 17th century the barrow was kno
2 CONC wn locally as Ottarshögen. The term Hög is derived from the Old Norse word haugr meaning mound or barrow. [14]
2 CONT
2 CONT The barrow was excavated in the period 1914-1916.[14] It showed the remains of both a man and a woman, and the finds were worthy of a king.[15] The Swedish archaeologist Sune Lindqvist[16] reported that in its centre there was a w
2 CONC ooden vessel with ashes. There were few finds but they were well-preserved. There were some decorative panels similar to those found in the other Vendel era graves nearby. A comb with a case was found, as well as a golden Roman co
2 CONC in, a solidus, dated to be no later than 477. It had been perforated and was probably used as decoration, but it showed signs of wear and tear and had probably been worn for a longer time. Lindquist stated that the identification 
2 CONC of the barrow as that of Ohthere could not receive more archaeological confirmation than those provided by the excavation.
2 CONT
