1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halga
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Halga, Helgi, Helghe or Helgo was a legendary Danish king living in the early 6th century.
2 CONT
2 CONT Scholars generally agree that he appears in both Anglo-Saxon (Beowulf) and Scandinavian tradition (Norse sagas and Danish chronicles). In both traditions, he was a Scylding, the son of Healfdene and the brother of Hroðgar. In Beow
2 CONC ulf, his relationship to Hroðulf is not explained, but if he was not his father, as in the Scandinavian tradition, he was at least his uncle. Both traditions also mention his family's feud with Froda and Ingeld. Whereas, not much 
2 CONC is said about Halga in Anglo-Saxon sources, much more is said in Scandinavian ones, all of them containing a version of the story of his incestuous relationship with his own daughter Yrsa. This liaison resulted in Halga's son Hroð
2 CONC ulf.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Hrólfr Kraki's saga, Halfdan (Healfdene) had three children, the sons Helgi and Hróarr (Hroðgar) and the daughter Signý. The sister was the eldest and married to Sævil Jarl, with whom she had the son Hrókr. Halfdan was murdered
2 CONC  by his own brother Fróði (Froda) and the two brothers had to seek refuge with a man called Vivil on an island, until they could avenge their father and kill Fróði. Hróarr moved to Northumbria and married king Norðri's daughter Ög
2 CONC n, whereas Helgi became the new king of Denmark, but stayed unmarried. Wanting a spouse, Helgi went to the Saxons wanting to woo their warlike queen Oluf. She was, however, not interested and humiliated Helgi by shaving his head a
2 CONC nd covering him with tar, while he was asleep, and sending him back to his ship. Some time later, Helgi returned and through a ruse, he kidnapped the queen for a while during which time he made her pregnant. Having returned to her
2 CONC  kingdom, the queen bore a child, a girl which she named Yrsa after her dog. Yrsa was set to live as a shepherd, until she was 12 years old, when she met her father Helgi who fell in love with her, not knowing it was his daughter.
2 CONC  Oluf kept quiet about the parentage and saw it as her revenge that Helgi would wed his own daughter. Helgi and Yrsa had the son Hrólfr Kraki (Hroðulf).
2 CONT
2 CONT For his share of the kingdom Hróarr had been given a precious ring, an heirloom. There was, however, a second relative who wanted his share, their nephew Hrókr. Since Hrókr was neither given a part of the kingdom nor the ring, he 
2 CONC went to Northumbria, killed Hróarr and threw the ring into the water (later retrieved by Hróarr's son Agnar). Helgi avenged his brother by cutting off Hrókr's arms and legs.
2 CONT
2 CONT Learning that Helgi and Yrsa lived happily together, queen Oluf travelled to Denmark to tell her daughter the truth. Yrsa was shocked and although Helgi wanted their relationship to remain as it was, Yrsa insisted on leaving him t
2 CONC o live alone. She was later taken by the Swedish king Aðils (Eadgils) as his queen, which made Helgi even more unhappy. Later, one Yule, Helgi was visited by an ugly being while he was in his hunting house. No person in the entire
2 CONC  kingdom allowed the being to enter the House, except Helgi. Later, the thing asked him to slep in his bed. Unwillingly he agreed, and as the thing got into the bed, it turned into an elvish woman, who was clad in silk and who was
2 CONC  the most beautiful woman he had ever seen. He raped her, and made her pregnant with a daughter named Skuld. Helgi forgot the woman and a couple of days after the date had passed, he was visited by the woman, who had Skuld on her 
2 CONC arms. The daughter would later marry Hjörvarðr (Heoroweard), Hrólfr Kraki's killer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Missing Yrsa, Helgi went to Uppsala to fetch her, but was killed by Aðils in battle. He was succeeded by his son Hrólfr.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Halga
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://freepages.genealogy.rootsweb.com/~greenefamily/lape/pafg111.htm#16796
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Halga, Helgi, Helghe or Helgo was a legendary Danish king living in the early 6th century[1]. His name would in his own language (Proto-Norse) have been *Hailaga[2] (dedicated to the gods).
2 CONT
2 CONT Scholars generally agree that he appears in both Anglo-Saxon (Beowulf) and Scandinavian tradition (Norse sagas and Danish chronicles)[3]. In both traditions, he was a Scylding, the son of Healfdene and the brother of Hroðgar. In B
2 CONC eowulf, his relationship to Hroðulf is not explained, but if he was not his father, as in the Scandinavian tradition, he was at least his uncle. Both traditions also mention his family's feud with Froda and Ingeld.
2 CONT
2 CONT Whereas, not much is said about Halga in Anglo-Saxon sources, much more is said in Scandinavian ones, all of them containing a version of the story of his incestuous relationship with his own daughter Yrsa. This liaison resulted i
2 CONC n Halga's son Hroðulf.
2 CONT
