1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Swedish king Egil Vendelcrow  aka Ongentheow
2 CONT Ongentheow (Old English: Ongenþeow, Ongenþio, Ongendþeow; Old Norse: Angantýr) (died ca. 515) was the name of a semi-legendary Swedish king of the house of Scylfings, who appears in Old English sources.
2 CONT
2 CONT He is generally identified with the Swedish king Egil Vendelcrow mentioned in Ynglingatal, Historia Norwegiae and in Ynglinga saga.[1][2][3][4] The reason why they are thought to have been the same is that each has the same positi
2 CONC on in the line of Swedish kings and is described as the father of Ohthere and grandfather of Eadgils.[5] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ongentheow
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ongen%C3%BEeow Ongentheow, (Anglo-Saxon Ongenþeow, Ongenþio, Ongendþeow; Swedish Angantyr) (– ca 515) was the name of a semi-legendary Swedish king of the house of Scylfings, who appears in Anglo-Saxon
2 CONC  sources. He is generally identified with the Swedish king Egil (also Swedish Egill, Eigil) who appears in Ynglingatal, Historia Norwegiae and in Ynglinga saga.[1][2][3][4]
2 CONT
2 CONT The names are different and have little etymological connection. Ongenþeow would in Proto-Norse have been *Anganaþewaz, whereas Egil would have been *Agilaz. The reason why they are thought to have been the same is that they have 
2 CONC the same position in the line of Swedish kings and are described as the fathers of Ohthere and grandfathers of Eadgils. As will be shown below, it can be argued that they are based on the same person and the same events, but it sh
2 CONC ould be noted that not every scholar is open to the historicity of the characters in Beowulf, and in the Norse sagas.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Anglosaxon sources'''
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Anglo-Saxon epic Beowulf Ongentheow is described as a fearsome warrior and it took two warriors Eofor and Wulf Wonreding to take him down.
2 CONT
2 CONT The epic tells that the Geats under their new king Hæþcyn captured the Swedish queen, but old king Ongenþeow saved her, at a hill fort called Hrefnesholt, although they lost her gold[5]. Ongentheow killed Hæþcyn[6], and besieged t
2 CONC he Geats at Hrefnesholt[7]. The Geats were, however, rescued by Hygelac, Hæþcyn's brother[8], who arrived the next day with reinforcements[9]. Having lost the battle, but rescued his queen, Ongenþeow and his warriors returned home
2 CONC [10].
2 CONT
2 CONT However, the war was not over. Hygelac, the new king of the Geats, attacked the Swedes[11]. The Geatish warriors Eofor and Wulf fought together against the hoary king Ongenþeow[12]. Wulf hit Ongentheow's head with his sword so tha
2 CONC t the old king bled over his hair, but the king hit back and wounded Wulf[13]. Then, Eofor retaliated by cutting through the Swedish king's shield and through his helmet[14], giving Ongentheow a death-blow[15]. Eofor took the Swed
2 CONC ish king's helmet, sword and breastplate and carried them to Hygelac[16]. When they came home, Eofor and Wulf were richly awarded[17], and Eofor was given Hygelac's daughter[18]. Because of this battle, Hygelac is referred to as O
2 CONC ngentheow's slayer[19].
2 CONT
2 CONT Ongentheow is also mentioned in passing by the earlier poem Widsit
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Beowulf'' gives [https://www.geni.com/people/Hygelac-of-G%C3%B6taland/6000000008177334846?through=6000000008177343225 Hygelac]'s genealogy: according to the poem, he was the son of [https://www.geni.com/people/Hrethel-of-G%C3%B6
2 CONC taland-Legendary/6000000008177334872 Hrethel] and had two brothers [https://www.geni.com/people/Herebeald-of-G%C3%B6taland/6000000008177334863 Herebeald] and [https://www.geni.com/people/H%C3%A4thcyn-of-G%C3%B6taland-Legendary/600
2 CONC 0000008177334866 Hæþcyn], as well as an [https://www.geni.com/people/N-N-Hygelacsdatter-Legendary/6000000008177343225?through=6000000196285753853 unnamed sister] who was married to [https://www.geni.com/people/Ecgtheow-Legendary/6
2 CONC 000000196285753853 Ecgtheow] and was the mother of the hero [https://www.geni.com/people/Beowulf-Legendary/6000000196285818823 Beowulf]. [https://www.geni.com/people/Hygelac-of-G%C3%B6taland/6000000008177334846?through=60000000081
2 CONC 77343225 Hygelac] was married to [https://www.geni.com/people/Hygd-H%C3%A6rethsdatter/6000000008177334843 Hygd], and they had a son [https://www.geni.com/people/Heardred-of-G%C3%B6taland/6000000008177334840?through=600000000817733
2 CONC 4843 Heardred] and an [https://www.geni.com/people/N-N-Hygelacsdatter-Legendary/6000000008177343225?through=6000000008177334840 unnamed daughter] who married [https://www.geni.com/people/Eofor-Wonredsson-Legendary/6000000008248212
2 CONC 946 Eofor]. When [https://www.geni.com/people/Hygelac-of-G%C3%B6taland/6000000008177334846?through=6000000008177343225 Hygelac]'s brother [https://www.geni.com/people/H%C3%A4thcyn-of-G%C3%B6taland-Legendary/6000000008177334866  Hæ
2 CONC þcyn] was fighting with the Swedes, [https://www.geni.com/people/Hygelac-of-G%C3%B6taland/6000000008177334846?through=6000000008177343225 Hygelac] arrived at Hrefnesholt one day too late to save his brother [https://www.geni.com/p
2 CONC eople/H%C3%A4thcyn-of-G%C3%B6taland-Legendary/6000000008177334866 Hæþcyn], but he managed to rescue the surviving Geatish warriors, who were besieged by the Swedish king [https://www.geni.com/people/Ongentheow/6000000071760922920 
2 CONC Ongentheow] and his three sons. The Swedes found refuge at a hill fort but were assaulted by the Geats. In the battle, the Swedish king was slain by [https://www.geni.com/people/Eofor-Wonredsson-Legendary/6000000008248212946 Eofor
2 CONC ]. After the death of his brother [https://www.geni.com/people/Herebeald-of-G%C3%B6taland/6000000008177334863?through=6000000008177334846 Herebeald], [https://www.geni.com/people/Hygelac-of-G%C3%B6taland/6000000008177334846?throug
2 CONC h=6000000008177343225 Hygelac] ascended the Geatish throne. After he was killed during a raid on Frisia (by a grandson of Clovis I), [https://www.geni.com/people/Hygelac-of-G%C3%B6taland/6000000008177334846?through=600000000817734
2 CONC 3225 Hygelac] was succeeded by [https://www.geni.com/people/Heardred-of-G%C3%B6taland-Legendary/6000000008177334840?through=6000000008177334846 Heardred], according to Beowulf.
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hygelac
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT https://sv.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Egil_Tunnadolg
2 CONT Egil Tunnadolg (även Angantyr) var enligt Heimskringla en sveakung av Ynglingaätten. Han omnämns även i Historia Norvegiæ, men har där tillnamnet Egil Vendelkråka.
2 CONT
2 CONT Enligt Snorre Sturlasson var Egil son till Ane och liksom denne ingen krigisk kung. Hans fars skattväktare trälen Tunne gjorde uppror genom att köpa sig krigare med skatter han gömt undan. Efter åtta slag tvingas Egil fly till kun
2 CONC g Frode av Danmark. Han ger Egil en här för att besegra Tunne mot att Egil betalar honom skatt. Egil vinner visserligen, men istället för att betala skatt skickar han varje halvår stora gåvor till danerna, och vänskapen består. Eg
2 CONC il ska ha blivit dödad av en folkilsken tjur under en jakt, och efterträtts av sin son Ottar Vendelkråka.
2 CONT
2 CONT I egenskap av far till Ottar Vendelkråka har han i äldre historieforskning identifierats med Ongentheow (Angantyr) i det engelska eposet Beowulf. I Ynglingatal benämns Egil dessutom med tillnamnet Týs öttungr vilket anses vara en 
2 CONC omtolkning av ett äldre Angan Týs. I Beowulf berättas att Ongentheows hustru blev tillfångatagen av den geatiske kungen Haethcyn (Håkan) som vedergällning för att Ongentheows söner Onela och Ohthere hade härjat i Geatland. Ongenth
2 CONC eow hann dock upp Haethcyn och omringade geaterna. Dessa får emellertid bistånd i sista stund då Ongentheows broder Hygelac kommer med hjälptrupper, varvid svearna besegras och Ongentheow dödas.
2 CONT Källa: 
2 CONT Snorre Sturlasson (1991). Nordiska kungasagor. 1, Från Ynglingasagan till Olav Tryggvasons saga. Stockholm: Fabel. sid. 49-50. Libris 1266026. ISBN 91-7842-122-5
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ongen%C3%BEeow Ongentheow, (Anglo-Saxon Ongenþeow, Ongenþio, Ongendþeow; Swedish Angantyr) (– ca 515) was the name of a semi-legendary Swedish king of the house of Scylfings, who appears in Anglo-Saxon
2 CONC  sources. He is generally identified with the Swedish king Egil (also Swedish Egill, Eigil) who appears in Ynglingatal, Historia Norwegiae and in Ynglinga saga.[1][2][3][4]
2 CONT
2 CONT The names are different and have little etymological connection. Ongenþeow would in Proto-Norse have been *Anganaþewaz, whereas Egil would have been *Agilaz. The reason why they are thought to have been the same is that they have 
2 CONC the same position in the line of Swedish kings and are described as the fathers of Ohthere and grandfathers of Eadgils. As will be shown below, it can be argued that they are based on the same person and the same events, but it sh
2 CONC ould be noted that not every scholar is open to the historicity of the characters in Beowulf, and in the Norse sagas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anglosaxon sources
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Anglo-Saxon epic Beowulf Ongentheow is described as a fearsome warrior and it took two warriors Eofor and Wulf Wonreding to take him down.
2 CONT
2 CONT The epic tells that the Geats under their new king Hæþcyn captured the Swedish queen, but old king Ongenþeow saved her, at a hill fort called Hrefnesholt, although they lost her gold[5]. Ongentheow killed Hæþcyn[6], and besieged t
2 CONC he Geats at Hrefnesholt[7]. The Geats were, however, rescued by Hygelac, Hæþcyn's brother[8], who arrived the next day with reinforcements[9]. Having lost the battle, but rescued his queen, Ongenþeow and his warriors returned home
2 CONC [10].
2 CONT
2 CONT However, the war was not over. Hygelac, the new king of the Geats, attacked the Swedes[11]. The Geatish warriors Eofor and Wulf fought together against the hoary king Ongenþeow[12]. Wulf hit Ongentheow's head with his sword so tha
2 CONC t the old king bled over his hair, but the king hit back and wounded Wulf[13]. Then, Eofor retaliated by cutting through the Swedish king's shield and through his helmet[14], giving Ongentheow a death-blow[15]. Eofor took the Swed
2 CONC ish king's helmet, sword and breastplate and carried them to Hygelac[16]. When they came home, Eofor and Wulf were richly awarded[17], and Eofor was given Hygelac's daughter[18]. Because of this battle, Hygelac is referred to as O
2 CONC ngentheow's slayer[19].
2 CONT
2 CONT Ongentheow is also mentioned in passing by the earlier poem Widsith as the king of Sweden: lines 30–33: Wald Woingum, Wod þyringum, 	Wald [ruled] the Woings, Wod the Thuringians, Sæferð Sycgum, Sweom Ongendþeow, 	Saeferth the Sycg
2 CONC s, the Swedes Ongendtheow, Sceafthere Ymbrum, Sceafa Longbeardum 	Sceafthere the Ymbers, Sceafa the Lombards,
2 CONT
2 CONT Egil
2 CONT
2 CONT In Ari Þorgilsson's Íslendingabók and in Historia Norwegiae, he was called Egil Vendelcrow (Vendilcraca/Vendilkráka, a name traditionally given to those living at the royal estate of Vendel in Sweden). Snorri Sturluson, however, g
2 CONC ave the name Vendelcrow to Egil's son Ottar (Ohthere). In these sources, Egil was the son of Aun the Old, and like him, not very warlike. After he had made the thrall Tunni (or Tonne) responsible for the treasury, Tunni rebelled a
2 CONC gainst Egil. They fought eight battles after which Egil fled to Denmark, according to the Ynglinga saga (Ynglingatal does not mention where he fled and Historia Norwegiae does not mention any escape at all). Snorri wrote that Fróð
2 CONC i, the Danish king, aided Egil in defeating Tunni, and made Egil a tributary to the Danish king.
2 CONT
2 CONT Egil was killed by a bull during the sacrifices at the Temple at Uppsala.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Ok lofsæll
2 CONT     ór landi fló
2 CONT     Týs óttungr
2 CONT     Tunna ríki,
2 CONT     en flæming
2 CONT     farra trjónu
2 CONT     jötuns eykr
2 CONT     á Agli rauð.
2 CONT     Sás of austr
2 CONT     áðan hafði
2 CONT     brúna hörg
2 CONT     of borinn lengi,
2 CONT     en skíðlauss
2 CONT     Skilfinga nið
2 CONT     hœfis hjörr
2 CONT     til hjarta stóð.[20][21]
2 CONT
2 CONT     The fair-haired son of Odin's race,
2 CONT     Who fled before fierce Tunne's face,
2 CONT     Has perished by the demon-beast
2 CONT     Who roams the forests of the East.
2 CONT     The hero's breast met the full brunt
2 CONT     Of the wild bull's shaggy front;
2 CONT     The hero's heart's asunder torn
2 CONT     By the fell Jotun's spear-like horn.[22][23]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Norwegiæ presents a Latin summary of Ynglingatal, older than Snorri's quotation:
2 CONT
2 CONT Auchun vero genuit Eigil cognomento Vendilcraco, quem proprius servus nomine Tonne regno privavit, et cum domino pedisseqvus VIII civilia bella commisit, in omnibus victoria potitus, in nono tandem devictus occubuit; sed paulo pos
2 CONC t ipsum regem truculentus taurus confodiens trucidavit. Cui successit in regnum filius suus Ottarus [...][24] 	
2 CONT Aukun's son was Egil Vendelkråke, whose own bondman, Tunne, drove him from his kingdom; and though a mere servant he joined in eight civil combats with his master and won supremacy in all of them, but in a ninth he was finally def
2 CONC eated and killed. Shortly afterwards however the monarch was gored and slaughtered by a ferocious bull. The successor to the throne was his son Ottar, [...][25] 	
2 CONT The even earlier source Íslendingabók also cites the line of descent in Ynglingatal and it also gives Egil as the successor of Aunn and the predecessor of Óttarr: xvi Aun inn gamli. xvii Egill Vendilkráka. xviii Óttarr[26].
2 CONT
2 CONT Comments
2 CONT
2 CONT The two versions seem contradictory, but it has been shown that the two stories may very well describe the same event (Schück H. 1907, Nerman B. 1925), and that Ynglingatal was probably misinterpreted by Snorri due to a different 
2 CONC dialectal meaning of the word farra. In Ynglingatal, it says
2 CONT
2 CONT     en flæming
2 CONT     farra trjónu
2 CONT     jötuns eykr
2 CONT     á Agli rauð.
2 CONT
2 CONT If there is any authenticity behind the traditions, the origin of Ynglingatal was most probably a Swedish poem which has not survived (see also Sundquist 2004). In Old Swedish, farra did not mean "bull" but it meant "boar" (cf. En
2 CONC glish farrow meaning "young pig"). Moreover, in Old Norse Trjóna normally meant a pig's snout (modern Scandinavian tryne). Flæmingr meant "sword" (originally a Flemish sword imported by Vikings).
2 CONT
2 CONT Moreover, the sword of the snout can hardly refer to the horns of a bull, but it is more natural to interpret it as the tusks of a boar. In English, the lines can be translated as but the giant beast coloured its tusk red on Egil.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Anglo-Saxon, the name eofor meant "boar" and consequently Ynglingatal could very well relate of Eofor (the boar) killing Egil with kennings for boars. These kennings, sung originally by Swedes, were later misinterpreted by Norw
2 CONC egians and Icelanders as literal expressions due to the different dialectal meanings of farra.
2 CONT
2 CONT Moreover, according to Schück, the name Tunni which has no meaning in Old Norse should in Proto-Norse have been *Tunþa and derived from *Tunþuz. Consequently, it would have been the same word as the Gothic Tunþus which meant "toot
2 CONC h". This would mean that the name of Egil's enemy, actually meant "tooth" and Tunni and the bull/boar would consequently have been the same enemy, i.e. Eofor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some scholars have suggested that the name Ongentheow is connected to the Danish king Ongendus, (fl. c. 700) who appears in one sentence of Alcuin's life of Willibrord.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Ongentheow, (Old Englsh: Ongenþeow, Ongenþio, Ongendþeow; Swedish: Angantyr) (– ca 515) was the name of a semi-legendary Swedish king of the house of Scylfings, who appears in Old English sources. He is generally identified with t
2 CONC he Swedish king Egil (also Swedish Egill, Eigil) who appears in Ynglingatal, Historia Norwegiae and in Ynglinga saga.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ongentheow
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Egil Vendilkråke Frå Wikipedia – det frie oppslagsverket Gå til: navigering, søk Egil Vendilkråke var ein konge av Ynglingeætta, far til Ottar Vendilkråke og son av Aun den gamle. Namnet "Vendilkråke" er sett på Egil av Are Frode,
2 CONC  medan Ynglingesoga berre kjenner til at sonen Ottar har bore namnet. Etter Are skulle Vendil vise til ein stad i Uppland i Sverige, der Ynglingekongane er hauglagde.
2 CONT
2 CONT Det angelsaksiske kvædet Béowulf har ein hovding ved namn Ongentheow på same stad som Egil i ættelina over skilvingane/ynglingane. Dette har ført til ei drøfting av om desse to i røynda er same mannen, sjølv om grunnforteljingane 
2 CONC er noko ulike. Prova for likskap finst i tolkinga av Ynglingatal, som moglegvis er tolka gale av nordmenn og islendingar, på grunnlag av austnordisk (svensk) norrøn dikting. Egil er den einaste i diktet som har nemninga "Skilving"
2 CONC  knytt til seg.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Ynglingesoga [endre] Etter islandske kjelder laut Egil vinne attende makta frå trælen Tunne eller Tonne, som hadde rådd i staden til faren Aun, som døydde medan Egil framleis var liten. Tunne hadde vore skattmeister hjå Aun, og r
2 CONC ømde unna med ei mengd skattar og lausøyre. Då Egil vart konge, vart Tunne sett mellom trælane, og dette lika han dårleg, rømde, og vart teken til hovding. Tunne og mennane hans dreiv med røvarferder etter dette, og herja bygdene.
2 CONC  Egil laut gå mot Tunne i ei rekkje slag, men det tok tid før han vann. Snorre fortel at dei heldt åtte slag seg imellom, og Tunne vann dei alle. Då rømde Egil til Danmark, og bad om hjelp hjå kong Frode den frøkne. Saman greidde 
2 CONC desse å vinne over Tunne, men sidan sende Egil mange gåver til Frode kvart år. Egil vart drepen av ein gra-okse som vart mannevond, og då Egil ville veide han, sette stuten horna i han og drap han. Det vart banen hans.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tjodolv frå Kvine fortel det slik:
2 CONT
2 CONT Og den lovsæle laut  or lande røma,  son hans Ty,  for Tunne den mektuge.  Men jotuns øyken  på Egil farga  blanke panne-sverd  av blode raudt,  uksen, som hadde  aust i skogen  hovude lengi  høgt bori;  men hovud-sverde  slire-la
2 CONC ust  skilvings-sonen  stod til hjarta. 
2 CONT  Ongentheow [endre] Ongentheow er far til Onela (Åle) og Ohthere (Ottar). Hædcyn, kongen over gautane, gjer hemntog mot sveane, og dronninga til Ongentheow vert teken til fange. Ongentheow reiser ut og vinn dronniga att. Hædcyn ve
2 CONC rt drepen. Så held han gautane innestengde i Hræfnawuwu (ramnholtet), og trugar dei på livet. Bror av Hædcyn, Hygelac, kjem til med ein annan her, og gautane kjem unna. Sveane dreg seg unna, og Ongentheow vert såra av Wulf, son av
2 CONC  Wonred, men Wulf vert sjølv slegen ned. Eofor, bror av Wulf, hemner han, drep Ongentheow og tek herbunad og våpen attende til Hygelac. Wulf overlever åtaket, og Eofor vert gift med dotter til Hygelac.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Samanstilling [endre] Ordet farra, som finst i den norrøne originalteksta, tykkjest vera tolka ulikt på svensk og vestnordisk. På gamal austnordisk tydde farra villsvin, ikkje okse. Dette høver godt med namnet Eofor, som og tyder
2 CONC  villsvin på gamalengelsk. At Egil/Ongentheow då er drepen av "hoggtennene til svinet" (en flæming farra trjónu), i staden for horna til oksen, kan vera ein språkleg bilete på korleis Eofor drap Ongentheow. Trjonu tyder tryne, og 
2 CONC flæming kan tyde eit sverd, altså: "Sverdet frå svinetrynet". Dei språklege bileta i kvadet er sidan mistydd av norske og islandske skaldar på grunn av dialektale skilnader (engelsk Wikipedia).
2 CONT
2 CONT Henta frå «http://nn.wikipedia.org/wiki/Egil_Vendilkr%C3%A5ke»
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT 30. OF EGIL AND TUNNE.
2 CONT
2 CONT Egil was the name of On the Old's son, who succeeded as king in Sweden after his father's death. He was no warrior, but sat quietly at home. Tunne was the name of a slave who had been the counsellor and treasurer of On the Old; an
2 CONC d when On died Tunne took much treasure and buried it in the earth. Now when Egil became king he put Tunne among the other slaves, which he took very ill and ran away with others of the slaves. They dug up the treasures which Tunn
2 CONC e had concealed, and he gave them to his men, and was made their chief. Afterwards many malefactors flocked to him; and they lay out in the woods, but sometimes fell upon the domains, pillaging and killing the people. When King Eg
2 CONC il heard this he went out with his forces to pursue them; but one night when he had taken up his night quarters, Tunne came there with his men, fell on the king's men unexpectedly, and killed many of them. As soon as King Egil per
2 CONC ceived the tumult, he prepared for defence, and set up his banner; but many people deserted him, because Tunne and his men attacked them so boldly, and King Egil saw that nothing was left but to fly. Tunne pursued the fugitives in
2 CONC to the forest, and then returned to the inhabited land, ravaging and plundering without resistance. All the goods that fell into Tunne's hands he gave to his people, and thus became popular and strong in men. King Egil assembledúa
2 CONC n army again, and hastened to give battle to Tunne. But Tunne was again victorious, and King Egil fled with the loss of many people. Egil and Tunne had eight battles with each other, and Tunne always gained the victory. Then King 
2 CONC Egil fled out of the country, and went to Sealand in Denmark, to Frode the Bold, and promised him a scatt from the Swedes to obtain help. Frode gave him an army, and also his champions, with which force King Egil repaired to Swede
2 CONC n. When Tunne heard this he came out to meet him; and there was a great battle, in which Tunne fell, and King Egil recovered his kingdom, and the Danes returned home. King Egil sent King Frode great and good presents every year, b
2 CONC ut he paid no scatt to the Danes; but notwithstanding, the friendship between Egil and Frode continued without interruption. After Tunne's fall, Egil ruled the kingdom for three years. It happened in Sweden that an old bull, which
2 CONC  was destined for sacrifice, was fed so high that he became dangerous to people; and when they were going to lay hold of him he escaped into the woods, became furious, and was long in the forest committing great damage to the coun
2 CONC try. King Egil was a great hunter, and often rode into the forest to chase wild animals. Once he rode out with his men to hunt in the forest. The king had traced an animal a long while, and followed it in the forest, separated fro
2 CONC m all his men. He observed at last that it was the bull, and rode up to it to kill it. The bull turned round suddenly, and the king struck him with his spear; but it tore itself out of the wound. The bull now struck his horn in th
2 CONC e side of the horse, so that he instantly fell flat on the earth with the king. The king sprang up, and was drawing his sword, when the bull struck his horns right into the king's breast. The king's men then came up and killed the
2 CONC  bull. The king lived but a short time, and was buried in a mound at Upsal. Thjodolf sings of it thus: --
2 CONT
2 CONT "The fair-haired son of Odin's race, Who fled before fierce Tunne's face, Has perished by the demon-beast Who roams the forests of the East. The hero's breast met the full brunt Of the wild bull's shaggy front; The hero's heart's 
2 CONC asunder torn By the fell Jotun's spear-like horn."
2 CONT
