2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Ongentheow, (Anglo-Saxon Ongenþeow, Ongenþio, Ongendþeow; Swedish Angantyr) (– ca 515) was the name of a semi-legendary Swedish king of the house of Scylfings, who appears in Anglo-Saxon sources. He is generally identified with th
2 CONC e Swedish king Egil (also Swedish Egill, Eigil) who appears in Ynglingatal, Historia Norwegiae and in Ynglinga saga.
2 CONT
2 CONT The names are different and have little etymological connection. Ongenþeow would in Proto-Norse have been *Anganaþewaz, whereas Egil would have been *Agilaz. The reason why they are thought to have been the same is that they have 
2 CONC the same position in the line of Swedish kings and are described as the fathers of Ohthere and grandfathers of Eadgils. As will be shown below, it can be argued that they are based on the same person and the same events, but it sh
2 CONC ould be noted that not every scholar is open to the historicity of the characters in Beowulf, and in the Norse sagas.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Anglo-Saxon epic Beowulf Ongentheow is described as a fearsome warrior and it took two warriors Eofor and Wulf Wonreding to take him down.
2 CONT
2 CONT The epic tells that the Geats under their new king Hæþcyn captured the Swedish queen, but old king Ongenþeow saved her, at a hill fort called Hrefnesholt, although they lost her gold. Ongentheow killed Hæþcyn, and besieged the Gea
2 CONC ts at Hrefnesholt. The Geats were, however, rescued by Hygelac, Hæþcyn's brother, who arrived the next day with reinforcements. Having lost the battle, but rescued his queen, Ongenþeow and his warriors returned home.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, the war was not over. Hygelac, the new king of the Geats, attacked the Swedes. The Geatish warriors Eofor and Wulf fought together against the hoary king Ongenþeow. Wulf hit Ongentheow's head with his sword so that the ol
2 CONC d king bled over his hair, but the king hit back and wounded Wulf. Then, Eofor retaliated by cutting through the Swedish king's shield and through his helmet, giving Ongentheow a death-blow. Eofor took the Swedish king's helmet, s
2 CONC word and breastplate and carried them to Hygelac. When they came home, Eofor and Wulf were richly awarded, and Eofor was given Hygelac's daughter. Because of this battle, Hygelac is referred to as Ongentheow's slayer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ongentheow is also mentioned in passing by the earlier poem Widsith as the king of Sweden:
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ongentheow
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Roi de Uppland
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Ongentheow (Old Englsh: Ongenþeow, Ongenþio, Ongendþeow; Swedish: Angantyr) (died ca 515) was the name of a semi-legendary Swedish king of the house of Scylfings, who appears in Old English sources. He is generally identified with
2 CONC  the Swedish king Egil (also Swedish Egill, Eigil) who appears in Ynglingatal, Historia Norwegiae and in Ynglinga saga.[1][2][3][4]
2 CONT
2 CONT The names are different and have little etymological connection. Ongenþeow would in Proto-Norse have been *Anganaþewaz, whereas Egil would have been *Agilaz. The reason why they are thought to have been the same is that they have 
2 CONC the same position in the line of Swedish kings and are described as the fathers of Ohthere and grandfathers of Eadgils. As will be shown below, it can be argued that they are based on the same person and the same events, but not e
2 CONC very scholar is open to the historicity of the characters in Beowulf, and in the Norse sagas.
2 CONT
2 CONT Old English sources
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Old English epic Beowulf Ongentheow is described as a fearsome warrior and it took two warriors Eofor and Wulf Wonreding to take him down.
2 CONT
2 CONT The epic tells that the Geats under their new king Hæþcyn captured the Swedish queen, but old king Ongenþeow saved her, at a hill fort called Hrefnesholt, although they lost her gold.[5] Ongentheow killed Hæþcyn,[6] and besieged t
2 CONC he Geats at Hrefnesholt.[7] The Geats were, however, rescued by Hygelac, Hæþcyn's brother,[8] who arrived the next day with reinforcements.[9] Having lost the battle, but rescued his queen, Ongenþeow and his warriors returned home
2 CONC .[10]
2 CONT
2 CONT However, the war was not over. Hygelac, the new king of the Geats, attacked the Swedes.[11] The Geatish warriors Eofor and Wulf fought together against the hoary king Ongenþeow.[12] Wulf hit Ongentheow's head with his sword so tha
2 CONC t the old king bled over his hair, but the king hit back and wounded Wulf.[13] Then, Eofor retaliated by cutting through the Swedish king's shield and through his helmet,[14] giving Ongentheow a death-blow.[15] Eofor took the Swed
2 CONC ish king's helmet, sword and breastplate and carried them to Hygelac.[16] When they came home, Eofor and Wulf were richly awarded,[17] and Eofor was given Hygelac's daughter.[18] Because of this battle, Hygelac is referred to as O
2 CONC ngentheow's slayer.[19]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ongentheow is also mentioned in passing by the earlier poem Widsith as the king of Sweden: lines 30–33: Wald Woingum, Wod þyringum, 	Wald [ruled] the Woings, Wod the Thuringians, Sæferð Sycgum, Sweom Ongendþeow, 	Saeferth the Sycg
2 CONC s, the Swedes Ongendtheow, Sceafthere Ymbrum, Sceafa Longbeardum 	Sceafthere the Ymbers, Sceafa the Lombards,
2 CONT
2 CONT Egil
2 CONT
2 CONT In Ari Þorgilsson's Íslendingabók and in Historia Norwegiae, he was called Egil Vendelcrow (Vendilcraca/Vendilkráka, a name traditionally given to those living at the royal estate of Vendel in Sweden). Snorri Sturluson, however, g
2 CONC ave the name Vendelcrow to Egil's son Ottar (Ohthere). In these sources, Egil was the son of Aun the Old, and like him, not very warlike. After he had made the thrall Tunni (or Tonne) responsible for the treasury, Tunni rebelled a
2 CONC gainst Egil. They fought eight battles after which Egil fled to Denmark, according to the Ynglinga saga (Ynglingatal does not mention where he fled and Historia Norwegiae does not mention any escape at all). Snorri wrote that Fróð
2 CONC i, the Danish king, aided Egil in defeating Tunni, and made Egil a tributary to the Danish king.
2 CONT
2 CONT Egil was killed by a bull during the sacrifices at Gamla Uppsala.
2 CONT
2 CONT     Ok lofsæll
2 CONT     ór landi fló
2 CONT     Týs óttungr
2 CONT     Tunna ríki,
2 CONT     en flæming
2 CONT     farra trjónu
2 CONT     jötuns eykr
2 CONT     á Agli rauð.
2 CONT     Sás of austr
2 CONT     áðan hafði
2 CONT     brúna hörg
2 CONT     of borinn lengi,
2 CONT     en skíðlauss
2 CONT     Skilfinga nið
2 CONT     hœfis hjörr
2 CONT     til hjarta stóð.[20][21]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT     The fair-haired son of Odin's race,
2 CONT     Who fled before fierce Tunne's face,
2 CONT     Has perished by the demon-beast
2 CONT     Who roams the forests of the East.
2 CONT     The hero's breast met the full brunt
2 CONT     Of the wild bull's shaggy front;
2 CONT     The hero's heart's asunder torn
2 CONT     By the fell Jotun's spear-like horn.[22][23]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Norwegiæ presents a Latin summary of Ynglingatal, older than Snorri's quotation:
2 CONT
2 CONT Auchun vero genuit Eigil cognomento Vendilcraco, quem proprius servus nomine Tonne regno privavit, et cum domino pedisseqvus VIII civilia bella commisit, in omnibus victoria potitus, in nono tandem devictus occubuit; sed paulo pos
2 CONC t ipsum regem truculentus taurus confodiens trucidavit. Cui successit in regnum filius suus Ottarus [...][24] 	
2 CONT
2 CONT Aukun's son was Egil Vendelkråke, whose own bondman, Tunne, drove him from his kingdom; and though a mere servant he joined in eight civil combats with his master and won supremacy in all of them, but in a ninth he was finally def
2 CONC eated and killed. Shortly afterwards however the monarch was gored and slaughtered by a ferocious bull. The successor to the throne was his son Ottar, [...][25] 	
2 CONT
2 CONT The even earlier source Íslendingabók also cites the line of descent in Ynglingatal and it also gives Egil as the successor of Aunn and the predecessor of Óttarr: xvi Aun inn gamli. xvii Egill Vendilkráka. xviii Óttarr.[26]
2 CONT
2 CONT Comments
2 CONT
2 CONT The two versions seem contradictory, but it has been shown that the two stories may very well describe the same event (Schück H. 1907, Nerman B. 1925), and that Ynglingatal was probably misinterpreted by Snorri due to a different 
2 CONC dialectal meaning of the word farra. In Ynglingatal, it says
2 CONT
2 CONT     en flæming
2 CONT     farra trjónu
2 CONT     jötuns eykr
2 CONT     á Agli rauð.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT If there is any authenticity behind the traditions, the origin of Ynglingatal was most probably a Swedish poem which has not survived (see also Sundquist 2004). In Old Swedish, farra did not mean "bull" but it meant "boar" (cf. En
2 CONC glish farrow meaning "young pig"). Moreover, in Old Norse Trjóna normally meant a pig's snout (modern Scandinavian tryne). Flæmingr meant "sword" (originally a Flemish sword imported by Vikings).
2 CONT
2 CONT Moreover, the sword of the snout can hardly refer to the horns of a bull, but it is more natural to interpret it as the tusks of a boar. In English, the lines can be translated as but the giant beast coloured its tusk red on Egil.
2 CONT
2 CONT In Old English, the name eofor meant "boar" and consequently Ynglingatal could very well relate of Eofor (the boar) killing Egil with kennings for boars. These kennings, sung originally by Swedes, were later misinterpreted by Norw
2 CONC egians and Icelanders as literal expressions due to the different dialectal meanings of farra.
2 CONT
2 CONT Moreover, according to Schück, the name Tunni which has no meaning in Old Norse should in Proto-Norse have been *Tunþa and derived from *Tunþuz. Consequently, it would have been the same word as the Gothic Tunþus which meant "toot
2 CONC h". This would mean that the name of Egil's enemy, actually meant "tooth" and Tunni and the bull/boar would consequently have been the same enemy, i.e. Eofor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some scholars have suggested that the name Ongentheow is connected to the Danish king Ongendus, (fl. c. 700) who appears in one sentence of Alcuin's life of Willibrord.[27][28]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ongen%C3%BEeow Ongentheow, (Anglo-Saxon Ongenþeow, Ongenþio, Ongendþeow; Swedish Angantyr) (– ca 515) was the name of a semi-legendary Swedish king of the house of Scylfings, who appears in Anglo-Saxon
2 CONC  sources. He is generally identified with the Swedish king Egil (also Swedish Egill, Eigil) who appears in Ynglingatal, Historia Norwegiae and in Ynglinga saga.[1][2][3][4]
2 CONT
2 CONT The names are different and have little etymological connection. Ongenþeow would in Proto-Norse have been *Anganaþewaz, whereas Egil would have been *Agilaz. The reason why they are thought to have been the same is that they have 
2 CONC the same position in the line of Swedish kings and are described as the fathers of Ohthere and grandfathers of Eadgils. As will be shown below, it can be argued that they are based on the same person and the same events, but it sh
2 CONC ould be noted that not every scholar is open to the historicity of the characters in Beowulf, and in the Norse sagas.
