1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aun Ane, On, One, Auchun or Aun the Old (Audhun, the same name as the A-S name Edwin) was the son of Jorund and one of the Swedish kings of the House of Yngling, the ancestors of Norway
2 CONC 's first king, Harald Fairhair.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aun was a wise king who sacrificed greatly to the gods, but he was not a warlike king and preferred to live in peace. Consequently, he was attacked by the Danish prince Halfdan (the son of Fróði, the son of Dan the Arrogant, the f
2 CONC ounder of Denmark). Aun lost the battles and fled to the Geats in Västergötland, where he stayed for 25 years until Halfdan died in his bed in Uppsala and was buried in a mound.
2 CONT
2 CONT King Aun could return to Uppsala, but he was 60 years old. In order to live longer he sacrificed his own son to Odin who promised him that he could live for another 60 years. However, after 25 years, Aun was attacked by Halfdan's 
2 CONC cousin Ale the Strong. Aun lost several battles and had to flee a second time to Västergötland. Ale the Strong ruled in Uppsala for 25 years until he was killed by Starkad the old.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Ale the Strong's death, Aun could return to Uppsala. Once again, Aun sacrificed a son to Odin, but this time Odin said that he would live as long as he sacrificed a son every ten years and that he had to name one of the Swed
2 CONC ish provinces after the number of sons he sacrificed.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Aun had sacrificed a son for the seventh time, he was so old that he could not walk but had to be carried on a chair. When he had sacrificed a son for the eighth time, he could no longer get out of his bed. When he had sacrif
2 CONC iced his ninth son, he was so old that he had to feed by suckling a horn like a little child.
2 CONT
2 CONT After ten years he wanted to sacrifice his tenth and last son and name the province of Uppsala the ten lands. However, the Swedes refused to allow him this sacrifice and so he died. He was buried in a mound at Uppsala and succeede
2 CONC d by his last son Egil. From that day, dying in bed of old age was called Aun's sickness among the Scandinavians. <pre>
2 CONT
2 CONT     Knátti endr
2 CONT     at Upsölum
2 CONT     ána-sótt
2 CONT     Aun of standa,
2 CONT     ok þrálífr
2 CONT     þiggja skyldi
2 CONT     jóðs alað
2 CONT     öðru sinni.
2 CONT     Ok sveiðurs
2 CONT     at sér hverfði
2 CONT     mækis hlut
2 CONT     enn mjávara,
2 CONT     es okhreins
2 CONT     óttunga hrjóðr
2 CONT     lögðis odd
2 CONT     liggjandi drakk;
2 CONT     máttit hárr
2 CONT     hjarðar mæki
2 CONT     austrkonungr
2 CONT     upp of halda.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT     In Upsal's town the cruel king
2 CONT     Slaughtered his sons at Odin's shrine --
2 CONT     Slaughtered his sons with cruel knife,
2 CONT     To get from Odin length of life.
2 CONT     He lived until he had to turn
2 CONT     His toothless mouth to the deer's horn;
2 CONT     And he who shed his children's blood
2 CONT     Sucked through the ox's horn his food.
2 CONT     At length fell Death has tracked him down,
2 CONT     Slowly, but sure, in Upsal's town.[3][4] </pre>
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Norwegiæ presents a Latin summary of Ynglingatal, older than Snorri's quotation (continuing after Jorund): <blockquote> Iste genuit Auchun, qui longo vetustatis senio IX annis ante obitum suum densæ usum alimoniæ post
2 CONC ponens lac tantum de cornu ut infans suxisse fertur. Auchun vero genuit Eigil cognomento Vendilcraco [...] 	
2 CONT He became the father of Aukun, who, in the feebleness of a protracted old age, during the nine years before his death is said to have abandoned the consumption of solid food and only sucked milk from a horn, like a babe-in-arms. A
2 CONC ukun's son was Egil Vendelkråke, [...] </blockquote>
2 CONT
2 CONT The even earlier source Íslendingabók also cites the line of descent in Ynglingatal and it also gives Aun as the successor of Jörundr and the predecessor of Egil Vendelcrow: xv Jörundr. xvi Aun inn gamli. xvii Egill Vendilkráka
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Aun den gamle , Åne den gamle eller Auchun , var en kung av Ynglingeätten som måste ha varit fruktansvärt gammal. Han är far till Egil Vendilkråke och son till Jorund, en av Yngvesönerna . :
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Aun den gamle teikna av Gerhard Munthe
2 CONT Snorri Sturlason berättar hur Aun kom i konflikt med sköldungarna , och särskilt sönerna till Frode den fredlige , Halvdan och Fridleiv. Aun lät dem fly och fann skydd i Västergötland , efter att ha varit kung i Uppsala i 25 vintr
2 CONC ar. Han satt i Gautland i 25 vintrar till, medan Halvdan regerade för Uppsala.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sedan drevs han ut ur Uppsala av en kung Åle frun, son till Fridleiv. Han fick då ännu en gång fly till Gautland, och bodde där i 25 vintrar (han hade då levt 60 år under tiden). Åle dödades av Starkad den gamle , och Aun återvänd
2 CONC e till Uppsala och regerade där 25 nya vintrar.
2 CONT
2 CONT Aun ansågs mer vara en man än en krigare, och efter att han kom tillbaka, klädde han av sina söner för ett långt liv och vann tio nya år för varje son. Med tiden blev han så gammal att han inte kunde gå, och så småningom låg han i
2 CONC  en säng och drack som ett spädbarn. Då gjorde svenskarna uppror, och den yngste av hans tio söner blev skonad. Det här var Egil Vendilkråke . Aun dog av ålderdom , och fram till dess hade detta varit föga känt. Skaldarna kallade 
2 CONC då ålderdomen "Ånesott", säger Snorre.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tjodolv från Kvine vinner i Ynglingatal :
2 CONT
2 CONT Äntligen en kund
2 CONT i Uppsalir
2 CONT Åne-sott
