1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jorund Jorund eller Eorund var en sveakung av Ynglingaätten, enligt Heimskringla. Han var son till Yngve och hämnades sin far genom att dräpa Hake och återvann så den svenska tronen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jorund tillbringade somrarna med att plundra i grannländerna. En sommar befann han sig i Danmark och hade slagit läger i Oddasund. Han blev dock upptäckt av den norske kungen Gylaug vars far Jorund och hans bror Erik hade hängt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Under striden upptäckte danerna vad som var på gång och anslöt sig från alla håll. Jorund blev tillfångatagen och hängd av Gylaug.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jorund Jorund or Jörundr (5th century) was a Swedish king of the House of Yngling. He was the son of Yngvi, and he had reclaimed the throne of Sweden for his dynasty from Haki (the brother of Hagbard, 
2 CONC the hero of the legend of Hagbard and Signy, and it should be noted that Snorri cites two kennings from this legend Sigar's steed and Hagard's fell noose, when telling of Jorund).
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorri Sturluson relates that when Jorund was young he used to travel the seas and plunder with his brother Erik, and they were great warriors. One summer they plundered in Denmark where they met another pillager, King Gudlög of H
2 CONC ålogaland (a province in Norway) with whom they fought. They took him prisoner and carried him ashore at Stromones where they hanged him. Gudlaug's surviving companions raised a mound over him there.
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorri then cites the poem Háleygjatal by a Norwegian skald named Eyvindr skáldaspillir:
2 CONT
2 CONT     En Guðlaugr
2 CONT     grimman tamdi
2 CONT     við ofrkapp
2 CONT     austrkonunga
2 CONT     Sigars jó,
2 CONT     er synir Yngva
2 CONT     menglötuð
2 CONT     við meið reiddu.
2 CONT     Og náreiðr
2 CONT     á nesi drúpir
2 CONT     vingameiðr,
2 CONT     þar er víkur deilir,
2 CONT     þar er fjölkunnt
2 CONT     um fylkis hreyr
2 CONT     steini merkt,
2 CONT     Straumeyjarnes.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT     By the fierce East-kings' cruel pride,
2 CONT     Gudlog must on the wild horse ride --
2 CONT     The wildest horse you e'er did see:
2 CONT     'Tis Sigur's steed – the gallows tree.
2 CONT     At Stromones the tree did grow,
2 CONT     Where Gudlog's corpse waves on the bough.
2 CONT     A high stone stands on Stromo's heath,
2 CONT     To tell the gallant hero's death.[3][4]
2 CONT
2 CONT This act rendered the Swedish princes, Eric and Jorund, even more famous and they were thought of as even greater men. When they learnt that King Haki no longer had his forces around him, they decided to take care of their enemy. 
2 CONC They assembled a large force that was joined by Swedes as they approached. They entered Mälaren (a bay at the time) and steered towards Uppsala. They left their ships at the Fyris Wolds and were met by Haki who had less men. Haki 
2 CONC was a brutal fighter and managed to turn the tide of the battle. He slew Erik who held the banner and Jorund retreated with his men. Luckily, Haki had been seriously wounded and died.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jorund then ruled Sweden at Uppsala, but he usually spent the summers pillaging. One summer, he plundered in Jutland and entered Limfjorden, where he continued the pillaging. They anchored in Oddesund (before a storm in 1825, it w
2 CONC as near the innermost part of the fjord and almost 200 km from its mouth) but were discovered by the Norwegian pirate Gylaug of Hålogaland, the son of Gudlaug. Gylaug and his men attacked them and were joined by local forces who w
2 CONC anted revenge. As Jorund was vastly outnumbered (and had to run an almost 200 km long gauntlet to get out of the fjord), he lost the battle, and Gylaug had him hanged.
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorri illustrates this event with the stanza from Ynglingatal:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Varð Jörundr
2 CONT     hinn er endr of dó,
2 CONT     lífs of lattr
2 CONT     í Limafirði,
2 CONT     þá er hábrjóstr
2 CONT     hörva Sleipnir
2 CONT     bana Goðlaugs
2 CONT     of bera skyldi;
2 CONT     ok Hagbarðs
2 CONT     hersa valdi
2 CONT     höðnu leif
2 CONT     at halsi gekk.[5][6]
2 CONT
2 CONT     Jorund has travelled far and wide,
2 CONT     But the same horse he must bestride
2 CONT     On which he made brave Gudlog ride.
2 CONT     He too must for a necklace wear
2 CONT     Hagbert's fell noose in middle air.
2 CONT     The army leader thus must ride
2 CONT     On Horva's horse, at Lymfjord's side.[7][8]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Norwegiæ presents a Latin summary of Ynglingatal, older than Snorri's quotation, continuing after Yngvi (called Ingialdr):
2 CONT
2 CONT Post hunc filius ejus Jorundr, qui cum Danos debellasset, ab eisdem suspensus in loco Oddasund in sinu quodam Daciæ, quem Limafiorth indiginæ appellant, male vitam finivit. Iste genuit Auchun (i.e. Aun) [...][9] 	
2 CONT After him his son Jorund ruled, who ended his days unhappily once he had fought a war against the Danes, who hanged him at Oddesund, on an arm of the sea in Denmark which the natives call Limfjorden. He became the father of Aukun,
2 CONC  [...][10] 	
2 CONT The even earlier source Íslendingabók also cites the line of descent in Ynglingatal and it gives the same line of succession: xiiii Yngvi. xv Jörundr. xvi Aun inn gamli[11].
2 CONT
2 CONT The Skjöldunga saga and the Bjarkarímur tell that Jorund was defeated by the Danish king Fróði (corresponds to the Heaðobard Froda in Beowulf), who made him a tributary and took his daughter. The daughter gave birth to Halfdan, bu
2 CONC t another woman became Fróði's legitimate wife and gave him an heir named Ingjaldr (corresponds to the Heaðobard Ingeld in Beowulf). Together with one of his earls, Swerting, Jorund conspired against Fróði and killed him during th
2 CONC e blót.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Konge i Uppsala (Sverige)
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Jorund or Jörundr (5th century) was a Swedish king of the House of Yngling. He was the son of Yngvi, and he had reclaimed the throne of Sweden for his dynasty from Haki (the brother of Hagbard, the hero of the legend of Hagbard an
2 CONC d Signy, and Snorri cites two kennings  from this legend Sigar's steed and Hagard's fell noose, when telling of Jorund).
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorri Sturluson relates that when Jorund was young he used to travel the seas and plunder with his brother Erik, and they were great warriors. One summer they plundered in Denmark where they met another pillager, King Gudlög of H
2 CONC ålogaland (a province in Norway) with whom they fought. They took him prisoner and carried him ashore at Stromones where they hanged him. Gudlaug's surviving companions raised a mound over him there
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jorund
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT 28. JORUND, YNGVE'S SON.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jorund, King Yngve's son, remained king at Upsal. He ruled the country; but was often in summer out on war expeditions. One summer he went with his forces to Denmark; and having plundered all around in Jutland, he went into Lymfjo
2 CONC rd in autumn, and marauded there also. While he was thus lying in Oddesund with his people, King Gylog of Halogaland, a son of King Gudlog, of whom mention is made before, came up with a great force, and gave battle to Jorund. Whe
2 CONC n the country people saw this they swarmed from all parts towards the battle, in great ships and small; and Jorund was overpowered by the multitude, and his ships cleared of their men. He sprang overboard, but was made prisoner an
2 CONC d carried to the land. Gylog ordered a gallows to be erected, led Jorund to it, and had him hanged there. So ended his life. Thjodolf talks of this event thus: --
2 CONT
2 CONT "Jorund has travelled far and wide, But the same horse he must bestride On which he made brave Gudlog ride. He too must for a necklace wear Hagbert's fell noose in middle air. The army leader thus must ride On Horva's horse, at Ly
2 CONC mfjord's side."
2 CONT
2 CONT ******************** Events in the life of Jörundr Yngvesson
2 CONT
2 CONT event 1 . ·a great warrior, like his brother Eric, and they bided their time in their warships during the reign of the usurper, Hake, who'd killed their cousin Hugleik, event 1 . ·became more celebrated by this deed, he and his br
2 CONC other, this killing of King Gudlog from Halogaland, and they appeared to be much greater men than before, and they steered for home, Sweden, and gathered together a strong force, for as soon as the Swedes heard that the Yngling br
2 CONC others were come to them, they flocked to them in multitudes † death 1 . ·Though ruler of a country; he was often in the summer out on war expeditions. One summer he went with his forces to Denmark; and having plundered all around
2 CONC  in Jutland, he went into Lymfjord in autumn, and marauded there also. While he was thus lying in Oddesund with his people, King Gylog of Halogaland, a son of King Gudlog, of whom mention is made before, came up with a great force
2 CONC , and gave battle to Jorund. When the country people saw this they swarmed from all parts towards the battle, in great ships and small; and Jorund was overpowered by the multitude, and his ships cleared of their men. He sprang ove
2 CONC rboard, but was made prisoner and carried to the land. Gylog ordered a gallows to be erected, led Jorund toit, and had him hanged there. So ended his life. Thjodolf talks of this event thus: -- "Jorund has travelled far and wide, 
2 CONC But the same horse he must bestride On which he made brave Gudlog ride. He too must for a necklace wear Hagbert's fell noose in middle air. The army leader thus must ride On Horva's horse, at Lymfjord's side." event 1 . ·recovered
2 CONC  the lands of his father from Hake, the usurper,
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Jørund and his brother, Eric were very young when their father was killed, so their uncle Alf's son Hugleik ruled, reputed as not being a warrior and being quite greedy. Two sea king brothers, Hake and Hagbard, invaded Sweden and 
2 CONC killed Hugleik, Hake ruled the Swedes. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Meanwhile, Yngvi's sons, Jørund and Eric, invaded Denmark, taking and hanging the king, Gudlog at Stromones. They went after Hake next, who killed Eric and cut the brother's banner in two at a great battle on the Fyrisvoid near Up
2 CONC psala, Hake was wounded enough to have set his boat free with all his men and burned it, falling upon the flames to die. Jorund becmae the king at Uppsala. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jørund would leave on expeditions to Denmark and Jutland. One year he was marauding at Lymfjord in the autumn when Gudlog's son, King Gylog of Halogaland, attacked, imprisoned and hanged Jørund.
2 CONT
2 CONT Heimskringla, Ynglinga Saga, Section 25-28 
2 CONT
2 CONT Jørund or Eorund was a Swedish king of the House of Yngling. He was the son of Yngvi, and he had reclaimed the throne of Sweden for his dynasty from Haki (the brother of Hagbard, the hero of the legend of Hagbard and Signy, and it
2 CONC  should be noted that Snorri cites two kennings from this legend Sigar's steed and Hagard's fell noose, when telling of Jorund). 
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorri Sturluson relates that when Jørund was young he used to travel the seas and plunder with his brother Erik, and they were great warriors. One summer they plundered in Denmark where they met another pillager, King Gudlög of H
2 CONC ålogaland (a province in Norway) with whom they fought. They took him prisoner and carried him ashore at Stromones where they hanged him. Gudlaug's surviving companions raised a mound over him there. 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jorund 
2 CONT
