2 CONC rboard, but was made prisoner and carried to the land. Gylog ordered a gallows to be erected, led Jorund toit, and had him hanged there. So ended his life. Thjodolf talks of this event thus: -- "Jorund has travelled far and wide, 
2 CONC But the same horse he must bestride On which he made brave Gudlog ride. He too must for a necklace wear Hagbert's fell noose in middle air. The army leader thus must ride On Horva's horse, at Lymfjord's side." event 1 . ·recovered
2 CONC  the lands of his father from Hake, the usurper, <nowiki>--------------------</nowiki> Notes for Kong Jørund Yngveson Ble hengt av Kong Gylaug.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jorund, som erholdt Styret over Sverige efterat hans Broder Erik var falden mod Hake, blev paa et af sine idelige Sjøtog angreben af den danske Kong Guløg og slagen i Jylland ved Uddesund i Liimfjorden, hvorved han selv blev fange
2 CONC n og hængt. <nowiki>--------------------</nowiki> Yngvesønene Jorund/Jørund og Eirik var kongar av Ynglingeætta, søner av Yngve, den eine av Alrekssønene i følgje Snorre Sturlason. Dei var det tredje dømet på samkongar eller kongs
2 CONC brør i Ynglingesoga.
2 CONT
2 CONT Brørne var berre små då faren Yngve og bror hans drap kvarandre, og det var søskenbarnet Hugleik som rådde for Svitjod medan dei vaks opp. Soga fortel at brørne reiste tidleg på herferd og vart store sjøkongar, og herja mellom ann
2 CONC a i Danmark. Her råka dei på ein Gudlaug, konge over håløygene, og dei tok på han og vann. Sidan vende dei seg til Svitjod, der ein dansk hovding, Hake, hadde teke makta og drepe Hugleik. Jorund og Eirik gjekk til slag mot Hake på
2 CONC  Fyrisvollane, og Eirik vart drepen der. Hake fekk og banesår, og vart førd ut på sjøen i langskip, som sidan vart brend.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jorund var sidan konge i Uppsala, og var på herferd om somrane, er det gjete. Han miste livet på Jylland, då kong Gylaug av Hålogaland, son av Gudlaug, vann over han, og Jorund vart hengd der. Jorund var far til Aun den gamle, som
2 CONC  vart konge etter han.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tjodolv frå Kvine fortel i Ynglingatal:
2 CONT
2 CONT Då Jørund var feig i forne tid live han let i Limfjorden, då stry-hesten høg-bringa banen hans Gudlaug bera skulde og det harde reipe som Hagbard kvævde herse-hovdingen um halsen gjekk. Etter soga er Hake bror av Hagbard. I kvada 
2 CONC som omtalar kong Jorund, er det mange tilvisingar til forteljinga om Hagbard og Signe (Hagbard vart og hengd).
2 CONT
2 CONT Den eldste Noregshistoria [endre]
2 CONT
2 CONT Historia Norvegiæ omtalar berre Jorund, ikkje Eirik. Her er dei søner av ein Ingjald (visseleg eit anna namn på Yngve). Denne framstillinga veit berre av at Jorund miste livet i kamp med danene, og vart hengd der. At det var ein h
2 CONC åløyg-konge som valda dette, finst berre attgjeve hjå Snorre (som kan ha hatt andre kjelder).
2 CONT
2 CONT Historisk fastsetjing [endre]
2 CONT
2 CONT Forteljinga om Hugleik, Eirik og Jorund er knytt både til Hagbard og Signe-forteljinga, og til forteljinga om Starkad den gamle, ein av kjempene hans Hake. Samstundes er det mogleg at Hake er den same som Hoc Healfdene, nemnd i kv
2 CONC ædet Béowulf. Har dette noko føre seg, kan ein datere hendingane i livet hans Jorund til første halvdel av 400-talet, ei tid då danene var på frammarsj og la under seg landa aust og vest for øyane dei opphavleg budde på, og slik k
2 CONC om i trette med sveane og jydane. Kjeldene er elles uklåre på kongsrekkja før Jorund, og mykje tyder på at Jorund kan reknast som byrjinga på historisk tid, sidan han og er son av ein Yngve (som er opphavsnamnet til heile slekta).
2 CONC  Kongane før Jorund (unnateke sonen Aun), er skildra i meir mytisk/magiske vendingar.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jorund og Eirik er og omtalt hjå Øyvind Skaldespillar. Dei er soleis måteleg namngjetne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henta frå «http://nn.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yngves%C3%B8nene»
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------- Noteringar Sveakonung under senare hälften av 400-talet med säte i G.a Uppsala. Son till Yngve. Om somrarna var han ute och härjade i grannländerna. En sommar var han och skövlade i Danmark. Han låg med sitt m
2 CONC anskap i Oddasundet då Gylaug Hålögakonung kom med en väldig flotta. Jornund hade tidigare tillsammans med sin bror Erik hängt Gylaugs far Gudlaug. Det blev en stor strid och när landets folk fick reda på vem man stred emot kom de
2 CONC  strömmande från alla hörn. Jorund blev infångad och ledd upp på land där kung Gylaug lät hänga honom. Troligen höglagd vid Limfjorden.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT  Jorund and brother Eirik killed King Guthlaug (Gudlagur) of Halogaland. He also killed King Haki of Sweden. Later he was captured by King Gyllaug of Halogaland and hanged.
2 CONT
2 CONT    He and his brother Eric, the sons of Yngve, became famous by conquering the King Gudlaug of the Haleygians in Norway, whom they had met in Demnark. Met King Hake and his army af the Fyrisvols. In the battle, Eric was killed and
2 CONC  Jorund fled to his ship. King Hake was himself so grievously wounded that he ordered a warship to be loaded with his dead men and their weapons, and himself placed on it. The sails were hoisted and the ship set on fire, and out i
2 CONC t flew, with the dying king on board. Jorund now became king in Upsala. When he was maurauding in Jutland one summer, he met a son of King Gudlaug in battle and was overpowered, captured and hanged. [WBH - Sweden]
2 CONT
2 CONT    FOSTER, MINOR, BURR, WAITE, NEWLIN LINES
2 CONT
2 CONT    When Hake had ruled as king of Upsala for three years, Jorund and Eric, the sons of Yngve, returned with warships and warriors. They had grown up and become famous by conquering the king Gudlaug, of the Haleygians in Norway, wh
2 CONC om they had met in Denmark. Now they met King Hake and his army at the Fyrisvols. In the battle Eric was killed and Jorund fled to his ship. But King Hake was himself so grievously wounded that he ordered a warship to be loaded wi
2 CONC th his dead men and their weapons, and himself to be placed upon it. The sails were hoisted and the ship set on fire, and out it flew, with the dying king on board, between the skerries to the sea. Jorund now became king in Upsala
2 CONC . When he was one summer marauding in Jutland, he met a son of King Gudlaug, in the battle with whom he was overpowered, captured and hanged. [History of Sweden, pp. 36-7]
2 CONT
2 CONT     Reference Number: G6SZ-Q5
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT     Note: Heimskringla or The Chronicle of the Kings of Norway
2 CONT     Note: The Ynglinga Saga, or The Story of the Yngling Family from Odin to Halfdan the Black
2 CONT     Note: 28. JORUND, YNGVE'S SON.
2 CONT
2 CONT    Jorund, King Yngve's son, remained king at Upsal. He ruled the country; but was often in summer out on war expeditions. One summer he went with his forces to Denmark; and having plundered all around in Jutland, he went into Lym
2 CONC fjord in autumn, and marauded there also. While he was thus lying in Oddesund with his people, King Gylog of Halogaland, a son of King Gudlog, of whom mention is made before, cameup with a great force, and gave battle to Jorund. W
2 CONC hen the country people saw this they swarmed from all parts towards the battle, in great ships and small; and Jorund was overpowered by the multitude, and his ships cleared of their men. He sprang overboard, but was made prisoner 
2 CONC and carried to the land. Gylog ordered a gallows to be erected, led Jorund to it, and had him hanged there. So ended his life. Thjodolf talks of this event thus: --
2 CONT
2 CONT "Jorund has travelled far and wide, But the same horse he must bestride On which he made brave Gudlog ride. He too must for a necklace wear Hagbert's fell noose in middle air. The army leader thus must ride On Horva's horse, at Ly
2 CONC mfjord's side." <nowiki>--------------------</nowiki> Jorund or Jörundr (5th century) was a Swedish king of the House of Yngling. He was the son of Yngvi, and he had reclaimed the throne of Sweden for his dynasty from Haki (the br
2 CONC other of Hagbard, the hero of the legend of Hagbard and Signy, and it should be noted that Snorri cites two kennings from this legend Sigar's steed and Hagard's fell noose, when telling of Jorund).
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorri Sturluson relates that when Jorund was young he used to travel the seas and plunder with his brother Erik, and they were great warriors. One summer they plundered in Denmark where they met another pillager, King Gudlög of H
2 CONC ålogaland (a province in Norway) with whom they fought. They took him prisoner and carried him ashore at Stromones where they hanged him. Gudlaug's surviving companions raised a mound over him there.
2 CONT
2 CONT This act rendered the Swedish princes, Eric and Jorund, even more famous and they were thought of as even greater men. When they learnt that King Haki no longer had his forces around him, they decided to take care of their enemy. 
2 CONC They assembled a large force that was joined by Swedes as they approached. They entered Mälaren (a bay at the time) and steered towards Uppsala. They left their ships at the Fyris Wolds and were met by Haki who had less men. Haki 
2 CONC was a brutal fighter and managed to turn the tide of the battle. He slew Erik who held the banner and Jorund retreated with his men. Luckily, Haki had been seriously wounded and died.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jorund then ruled Sweden at Uppsala, but he usually spent the summers pillaging. One summer, he plundered in Jutland and entered Limfjorden, where he continued the pillaging. They anchored in Oddesund (before a storm in 1825, it w
2 CONC as near the innermost part of the fjord and almost 200 km from its mouth) but were discovered by the Norwegian pirate Gylaug of Hålogaland, the son of Gudlaug. Gylaug and his men attacked them and were joined by local forces who w
2 CONC anted revenge. As Jorund was vastly outnumbered (and had to run an almost 200 km long gauntlet to get out of the fjord), he lost the battle, and Gylaug had him hanged
2 CONT
2 CONT After him his son Jorund ruled, who ended his days unhappily once he had fought a war against the Danes, who hanged him at Oddesund, on an arm of the sea in Denmark which the natives call Limfjorden. He became the father of Aukun 
2 CONC <nowiki>--------------------</nowiki> http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jorund
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Jorund or Jörundr (5th century) was a Swedish king of the House of Yngling. He was the son of Yngvi, and he had reclaimed the throne of Sweden for his dynasty from Haki (the brother of Hagbard, the hero of the legend of Hagbard an
2 CONC d Signy, and Snorri cites two kennings from this legend Sigar's steed and Hagard's fell noose, when telling of Jorund).
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorri Sturluson relates that when Jorund was young he used to travel the seas and plunder with his brother Erik, and they were great warriors. One summer they plundered in Denmark where they met another pillager, King Gudlög of H
2 CONC ålogaland (a province in Norway) with whom they fought. They took him prisoner and carried him ashore at Stromones where they hanged him. Gudlaug's surviving companions raised a mound over him there.
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorri then cites the poem Háleygjatal by a Norwegian skald named Eyvindr skáldaspillir:
2 CONT
2 CONT     En Guðlaugr
2 CONT     grimman tamdi
2 CONT     við ofrkapp
2 CONT     austrkonunga
2 CONT     Sigars jó,
2 CONT     er synir Yngva
2 CONT     menglötuð
2 CONT     við meið reiddu.
2 CONT     Og náreiðr
2 CONT     á nesi drúpir
2 CONT     vingameiðr,
2 CONT     þar er víkur deilir,
2 CONT     þar er fjölkunnt
2 CONT     um fylkis hreyr
2 CONT     steini merkt,
2 CONT     Straumeyjarnes.[1][2]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT     By the fierce East-kings' cruel pride,
2 CONT     Gudlog must on the wild horse ride --
2 CONT     The wildest horse you e'er did see:
2 CONT     'Tis Sigur's steed – the gallows tree.
2 CONT     At Stromones the tree did grow,
2 CONT     Where Gudlog's corpse waves on the bough.
2 CONT     A high stone stands on Stromo's heath,
2 CONT     To tell the gallant hero's death.[3][4]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT This act rendered the Swedish princes, Eric and Jorund, even more famous and they were thought of as even greater men. When they learnt that King Haki no longer had his forces around him, they decided to take care of their enemy. 
2 CONC They assembled a large force that was joined by Swedes as they approached. They entered Mälaren (a bay at the time) and steered towards Uppsala. They left their ships at the Fyris Wolds and were met by Haki who had less men. Haki 
2 CONC was a brutal fighter and managed to turn the tide of the battle. He slew Erik who held the banner and Jorund retreated with his men. Luckily, Haki had been seriously wounded and died.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jorund then ruled Sweden at Uppsala, but he usually spent the summers pillaging. One summer, he plundered in Jutland and entered Limfjorden, where he continued the pillaging. They anchored in Oddesund (before a storm in 1825, it w
2 CONC as near the innermost part of the fjord and almost 200 km from its mouth) but were discovered by the Norwegian pirate Gylaug of Hålogaland, the son of Gudlaug. Gylaug and his men attacked them and were joined by local forces who w
2 CONC anted revenge. As Jorund was vastly outnumbered (and had to run an almost 200 km long gauntlet to get out of the fjord), he lost the battle, and Gylaug had him hanged.
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorri illustrates this event with the stanza from Ynglingatal:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Varð Jörundr
2 CONT     hinn er endr of dó,
2 CONT     lífs of lattr
2 CONT     í Limafirði,
2 CONT     þá er hábrjóstr
2 CONT     hörva Sleipnir
2 CONT     bana Goðlaugs
2 CONT     of bera skyldi;
2 CONT     ok Hagbarðs
2 CONT     hersa valdi
2 CONT     höðnu leif
2 CONT     at halsi gekk.[2][5]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT     Jorund has travelled far and wide,
2 CONT     But the same horse he must bestride
2 CONT     On which he made brave Gudlog ride.
2 CONT     He too must for a necklace wear
2 CONT     Hagbert's fell noose in middle air.
2 CONT     The army leader thus must ride
2 CONT     On Horva's horse, at Lymfjord's side.[3][4]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Norwegiæ presents a Latin summary of Ynglingatal, older than Snorri's quotation, continuing after Yngvi (called Ingialdr):
2 CONT
2 CONT Post hunc filius ejus Jorundr, qui cum Danos debellasset, ab eisdem suspensus in loco Oddasund in sinu quodam Daciæ, quem Limafiorth indiginæ appellant, male vitam finivit. Iste genuit Auchun (i.e. Aun) [...][6] 	
2 CONT
2 CONT After him his son Jorund ruled, who ended his days unhappily once he had fought a war against the Danes, who hanged him at Oddesund, on an arm of the sea in Denmark which the natives call Limfjorden. He became the father of Aukun,
2 CONC  [...][7] 	
2 CONT
2 CONT The even earlier source Íslendingabók also cites the line of descent in Ynglingatal and it gives the same line of succession: xiiii Yngvi. xv Jörundr. xvi Aun inn gamli.[8]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Skjöldunga saga and the Bjarkarímur tell that Jorund was defeated by the Danish king Fróði (corresponds to the Heaðobard Froda in Beowulf), who made him a tributary and took his daughter. The daughter gave birth to Halfdan, bu
2 CONC t another woman became Fróði's legitimate wife and gave him an heir named Ingjaldr (corresponds to the Heaðobard Ingeld in Beowulf). Together with one of his earls, Swerting, Jorund conspired against Fróði and killed him during th
2 CONC e blót.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT 13. JORUND - King in Uppsala, Sweden from 302 until he was hanged in 312 A.D. He went on many expeditions, which suddenly ended when he was captured and hanged in 312. His son was:
2 CONT
2 CONT 14. AUN (ON OR ANE) THE OLD - King of Sweden until 380 A.D. 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT BIOGRAFI:
2 CONT
2 CONT Jorund eller Eorund var en sveakung av Ynglingaätten, enligt Heimskringla. Han var son till Yngve och hämnades sin far genom att dräpa Hake och återvann så den svenska tronen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jorund tillbringade somrarna med att plundra i grannländerna. En sommar befann han sig i Danmark och hade slagit läger i Oddasund. Han blev dock upptäckt av den norske kungen Gylaug vars far Jorund och hans bror Erik hade hängt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Under striden upptäckte danerna vad som var på gång och anslöt sig från alla håll. Jorund blev tillfångatagen och hängd av Gylaug.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Levde på 430-50 talet
