1 OCCU Kung av Uppsala, konge av Sverne, Swedish King of House of Ynglinga, Roi d'Uppsala, Konge i Sverige., King in Uppsala, Kung
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Dyggvi or Dyggve was a mythological Swedish king of the House of Ynglings. He died in bed and never reached Valhalla. Instead he went to Hel, Loki's daughter, who acquired a husband of royal blood. He was succeeded
2 CONC  by his son Dag the Wise.
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorri Sturluson wrote of Dygvvi's father Domar in his Ynglinga saga (1225):
2 CONT
2 CONT Dyggvi hét son hans, er þar næst réð löndum, ok er frá honum ekki sagt annat, en hann varð sóttdauðr.[1] 	
2 CONT
2 CONT Dygve was the name of his son, who succeeded him in ruling the land; and about him nothing is said but that he died in his bed.[2][3] 	
2 CONT
2 CONT About Dyggvi's mother Snorri had more to say: Dygve's mother was Drott, a daughter of King Danp, the son of Rig, who was first called "king" in the Danish tongue. His descendants always afterwards considered the title of king the 
2 CONC title of highest dignity. Dygve was the first of his family to be called king, for his predecessors had been called "Drottnar", and their wives "Drottningar", and their court "Drott". Each of their race was called Yngve, or Yngune
2 CONC , and the whole race together Ynglinger. The Queen Drott was a sister of King Dan Mikillati, from whom Denmark took its name.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dyggvi
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Dyggve Domarsson (Dyggvi Domarsson) (In English: Dygve Domarsson) King in Uppsala (Sweden)
2 CONT
2 CONT http://lind.no/nor/index.asp?lang=gb&emne=nor&person=Dyggve%20Domarsson
2 CONT
2 CONT Dyggvi or Dyggve was a mythological Swedish king of the House of Ynglings. He died in bed and never reached Valhalla. Instead he went to Hel, Loki's daughter, who acquired a husband of royal blood. He was succeeded by his son Dag 
2 CONC the Wise.
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorri Sturluson wrote of Domar in his Ynglinga saga (1225):
2 CONT
2 CONT Dygve was the name of his son, who succeeded him in ruling the land; and about him nothing is said but that he died in his bed.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dyggve Dyggve var kung av Svitjod i Ynglingaätten. Hans styre var enligt Ynglingatal fullt av välstånd och han dog sotdöden.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dyggvi Dyggvi or Dyggve was a mythological Swedish king of the House of Ynglings. He died in bed and never reached Valhalla. Instead he went to Hel, Loki's daughter, who acquired a husband of royal blo
2 CONC od. He was succeeded by his son Dag the Wise.
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorri Sturluson wrote of Domar in his Ynglinga saga (1225):
2 CONT
2 CONT Dyggvi hét son hans, er þar næst réð löndum, ok er frá honum ekki sagt annat, en hann varð sóttdauðr.[1] 	
2 CONT Dygve was the name of his son, who succeeded him in ruling the land; and about him nothing is said but that he died in his bed.[2][3]	
2 CONT
2 CONT About Dyggvi's mother Snorri had more to say:
2 CONT
2 CONT Móðir Dyggva var Drótt, dóttir Danps konungs, sonar Rígs, er fyrstr var konungr kallaðr á danska tungu; hans ættmenn höfðu ávalt síðan konungsnafn fyrir hit œzta tignarnafn. Dyggvi var fyrstr konungr kallaðr sinna ættmanna; en áðr
2 CONC  váru þeir dróttnar kallaðir, en konur þeirra dróttningar, en drótt hirðsveitin. En Yngvi eða Ynguni var kallaðr hverr þeirra ættmanna alla ævi, en Ynglingar allir saman. Drótt dróttning var systir Dans konungs hins mikilláta, er 
2 CONC Danmörk er við kend.[4] 	
2 CONT Dygve's mother was Drott, a daughter of King Danp, the son of Rig, who was first called "king" in the Danish tongue. His descendants always afterwards considered the title of king the title of highest dignity. Dygve was the first 
2 CONC of his family to be called king, for his predecessors had been called "Drottnar", and their wives "Drottningar", and their court "Drott". Each of their race was called Yngve, or Yngune, and the whole race together Ynglinger. The Q
2 CONC ueen Drott was a sister of King Dan Mikillati, from whom Denmark a took its name.[5][6] 	
2 CONT In his Ynglinga saga, Snorri Sturluson included a piece from Ynglingatal composed in the 9th century:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Kveðkat dul,
2 CONT     nema Dyggva hrör
2 CONT     Glitnis gná
2 CONT     at gamni hefr,
2 CONT     því at jódis
2 CONT     Ulfs ok Narfa
2 CONT     Konungmann
2 CONT     kjósa skyldi;
2 CONT     ok allvald
2 CONT     Yngva þjóðar
2 CONT     Loka mær
2 CONT     of leikinn hefr.[7][8]	
2 CONT
2 CONT     Dygve the Brave, the mighty king,
2 CONT     It is no hidden secret thing,
2 CONT     Has gone to meet a royal mate,
2 CONT     Riding upon the horse of Fate.
2 CONT     For Loke's daughter in her house
2 CONT     Of Yngve's race would have a spouse;
2 CONT     Therefore the fell-one snatched away
2 CONT     Brave Dygve from the light of day.[9][10]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Norwegiæ presents a Latin summary of Ynglingatal, older than Snorri's quotation:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Hujus [Domar] filius Dyggui item in eadem regione vitæ metam invenit. Cui successit in regnum filius ejus Dagr [...][11]
2 CONT
2 CONT Likewise Dyggve, his [Domar's] son, reached the limit of his life in that same region [Sweden]. His son Dag [...][12] 	
2 CONT The even earlier source Íslendingabók also cites the line of descent in Ynglingatal and it also gives Dyggvi as the successor of Dómarr and the predecessor of Dagr: ix Dómarr. x Dyggvi. xi Dagr[13].
