2 CONT    The forest foe, red fire, night thief,
2 CONT    Fell brother of the raging sea,
2 CONT    Against your father and your chief.[6][7]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Norwegiæ presents a Latin summary of Ynglingatal, older than Snorri's quotation:
2 CONT
2 CONT Hic [Wanlanda] genuit Wisbur, quem filii sui cum omni curia sua, ut citius hærenditarentur, vivum incenderunt. Cujus filium Domald [...][8]
2 CONT
2 CONT He [Vanlande] was the father of Visbur, whose sons burnt him alive with all his hirdsmen, so that they might attain their inheritance more swiftly. His son Domalde [...][9]
2 CONT
2 CONT The even earlier source Íslendingabók cites the line of descent in Ynglingatal and also gives Visburr as the successor of Vanlandi and the predecessor of Dómaldr: vi Vanlandi. vii Visburr. viii Dómaldr <nowiki>--------------------
2 CONC </nowiki> Konge i Uppsala (Sverige) <nowiki>--------------------</nowiki> Visbur tog arv efter sin far Vanlande. Han gifte sig med Aude den mäktiges dotter och gav henne tre storgårdar och ett guldsmycke. De fick två söner som het
2 CONC te Gisl och Önder. Visbur lämnade henne ensam och skaffade sig en annan kvinna. Hans första hustru for till sin far och tog med sig sönerna. Med sin nya kvinna fick han sonen Domalde. Den första hustrun lät "sejda" olycka över Vis
2 CONC bur. När hans två söner var 12 till 13 vintrar for de till sin far för att hämta guldsmycket som var deras mors brudgåva, men han ville inte lämna det ifrån sig. Då sade de att smycket skulle bli döden för den bäste mannens i hans
2 CONC  ätt. Sedan sejdade de åter på ett sådant sätt de skulle kunna döda sin far. Sejdkvinnan Huld sade till dem att hon skulle sejda så att släktdråp därefter skulle begås av Ynglingaätten. De gick med på detta. Därefter samlade de ma
2 CONC nskap och smög sig på Visbur under natten och brände honom inne. Guldsmycket användes sedan när kung Agne dödades.
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------- From Wikipedia: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Visbur
2 CONT
2 CONT 'Visbur or Wisbur (Old Norse "Certain/Undoubted Son"[1]) in Scandinavian mythology was a king of the House of Ynglings and the son of Vanlandi. He was burned to death inside his hall by the arson of two of his own sons in revenge 
2 CONC for rejecting their mother and denying them their heritage. He was succeeded by his son Dómaldi.' <nowiki>--------------------</nowiki> In Scandinavian mythology, Visbur was a king of the House of Ynglings and the son of Vanlandi.
2 CONC  He was burned to death inside his hall by the arson of two of his own sons in revenge for rejecting their mother and denying them their heritage. He was succeeded by his son Dómaldi.
2 CONT
2 CONT Visbur succeeded his father Vanlande. He married the daughter of Aude the Rich, and gave her as her bride-gift three large farms, and a gold ornament. They had two sons, Gisle and Ond; but Visbur left her and took another wife, wh
2 CONC ereupon she went home to her father with her two sons. Visbur had a son who was called Domald, and his stepmother used witchcraft to give him ill-luck. Now, when Visbur's sons were the one twelve and the other thirteen years of ag
2 CONC e, they went to their father's place, and desired to have their mother's dower; but he would not deliver it to them. Then they said that the gold ornament should be the death of the best man in all his race, and they returned home
2 CONC . Then they began again with enchantments and witchcraft, to try if they could destroy their father. The sorceress Huld said that by witchcraft she could bring it about by this means, that a murderer of his own kin should never be
2 CONC  wanting in the Yngling race; and they agreed to have it so. Thereafter they collected men, came unexpectedly in the night on Visbur, and burned him in his house. <nowiki>--------------------</nowiki>
2 CONT
