2 CONC fjölkyngi Finna í farfýsi hans. Þá gerðist honum svefnhöfugt, ok lagðist hann till svefns. En er hann hafði lítt sofnat, kallaði hann ok sagði, at mara trað hann. Menn hans fóru til ok vildu hjálpa honum; en er þeir tóku uppi til 
2 CONC höfuðsins, þá trað hon fótleggina, svá at nær brotnuðu; þá tóku þeir til fótanna, þá kafði hon höfuðit, svá at þar dó hann. Svíar tóku lík hans, ok var hann brendr við á þá er Skúta heitir. Þar váru settir bautasteinar hans.[1] Va
2 CONC nlande, Swegde's son, succeeded his father, and ruled over the Upsal domain. He was a great warrior, and went far around in different lands. Once he took up his winter abode in Finland with Snae the Old, and got his daughter Driva
2 CONC  in marriage; but in spring he set out leaving Driva behind, and although he had promised to return within three years he did not come back for ten. Then Driva sent a message to the witch Huld; and sent Visbur, her son by Vanlande
2 CONC , to Sweden. Driva bribed the witch- wife Huld, either that she should bewitch Vanlande to return to Finland, or kill him. When this witch-work was going on Vanlande was at Upsal, and a great desire came over him to go to Finland;
2 CONC  but his friends and counsellors advised him against it, and said the witchcraft of the Finn people showed itself in this desire of his to go there. He then became very drowsy, and laid himself down to sleep; but when he had slept
2 CONC  but a little while he cried out, saying that the Mara was treading upon him. His men hastened to him to help him; but when they took hold of his head she trod on his legs, and when they laid hold of his legs she pressed upon his 
2 CONC head; and it was his death. The Swedes took his body and burnt it at a river called Skytaa, where a standing stone was raised over him.[2][3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorri also quoted some lines from Ynglingatal composed in the 9th century:
2 CONT
2 CONT    En á vit
2 CONT    Vilja bróður
2 CONT    vitta véttr
2 CONT    Vanlanda kom,
2 CONT    þá er trollkund
2 CONT    of troða skyldi
2 CONT    liðs grímhildr
2 CONT    ljóna bága;
2 CONT    ok sá brann á beði Skútu
2 CONT    menglötuðr,
2 CONT    er mara kvalði.[4][5]
2 CONT
2 CONT    And Vanlande, in a fatal hour,
2 CONT    Was dragg'd by Grimhild's daughter's power,
2 CONT    The witch-wife's, to the dwelling-place
2 CONT    Where men meet Odin face to face.
2 CONT    Trampled to death, to Skytaa's shore
2 CONT    The corpse his faithful followers bore;
2 CONT    And there they burnt, with heavy hearts,
2 CONT    The good chief killed by witchcraft's arts.[6][7]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Norwegiæ presents a Latin summary of Ynglingatal, older than Snorri's quotation:
2 CONT
2 CONT Iste [Swegthir] genuit Wanlanda, qui in somno a dæmone suffocatus interiit, quod genus dæmoniorum norwegico sermone mara vocatur. Hic genuit Wisbur [...][8] He [Sveigde] sired Vanlande, who died in his sleep, suffocated by a gobli
2 CONC n, one of the demonic species known in Norwegian as 'mare'. He was the father of Visbur, [...][9] The even earlier source Íslendingabók cites the line of descent in Ynglingatal and also gives Vanlandi as the successor of Svegðir a
2 CONC nd the predecessor of Visbur: v Svegðir. vi Vanlandi. vii Visburr. viii Dómaldr[10].
2 CONT
2 CONT Geography
