1 OCCU господар в Ноатун, Konge, Konge i Uppsala, King of Sweden, Swedish King of the House of Ynking of Uppsala, Svitjord
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Foto from Old Uppsala Museum;
2 CONT
2 CONT http://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fjolner
2 CONT
2 CONT Fjölner eller Fjolner var i den nordiska asatron Frejs och jättinnan Gerds son, och ska enligt sagorna ha varit kung över svearna. Enligt Grottesången var han samtida med kejsar Augustus, och lär i så fall ha levat någon gång unde
2 CONC r första århundradet f.Kr och i början av första århundradet e.Kr..
2 CONT
2 CONT Fjölner skall enligt Ynglingasagans regentlängd, vars första kungar enbart har mytiska namn, ha varit kung över svearna. Legenden förtäljer att han under ett besök hos den danske kungen Frode på Själland ska ha druckit sig så beru
2 CONC sad att han i sömnyra drunknat i ett mjödkar hos densamme. Om detta skaldades det sedan att Fjölner hade drunknat i "vindlös våg". Han ska också ha sålt trälinnorna Fenja och Menja till kung Frode, som sedan lät dem mala guld i kv
2 CONC arnen Grotte.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fjolnir
2 CONT
2 CONT Fjölnir, Fjölner, Fjolner or Fjolne (1st century BC - early 1st century AD) was a Swedish king of the House of Yngling, at Gamla Uppsala. He appears in a semi-mythological context as the son of Freyr and Gerd.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fjölnir drowned in a vat of mead visiting Peace-Fróði, an equally mythological king of Zealand, where Denmark later appeared. Fjölnir was then succeeded by his son Sveigðir.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grottasöngr
2 CONT
2 CONT Grottasöngr informs that Fjölnir was the contemporary of Caesar Augustus (63 BC, AD 14). He was a mighty king and the crops were bountiful and peace was maintained. At his time, king Fróði, the son of Friðleifr, ruled in Lejre in 
2 CONC Zealand. Grottasöngr relates that when Fróði once visited Uppsala he bought two giantesses, Fenja and Menja:
2 CONT
2 CONT  Fróði konungr sótti heimboð í Svíþjóð til þess konungs, er Fjölnir er nefndr. Þá keypti hann ambáttir tvær, er hétu Fenja ok Menja. Þær váru miklar ok sterkar.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT However, the two giantesses were to be his undoing (see Grottasöngr).
2 CONT
2 CONT Ynglinga saga
2 CONT
2 CONT The Ynglinga saga tells that Fjölnir was the son of Freyr himself and the giantess Gerd, but he was the first of his house who was not to be deified.
2 CONT
2 CONT Freyr tók þá ríki eptir Njörð; var hann kallaðr dróttinn yfir Svíum ok tók skattgjafir af þeim; hann var vinsæll ok ársæll sem faðir hans. Freyr reisti at Uppsölum hof mikit, ok setti þar höfuðstað sinn; lagði þar til allar skyldi
2 CONC r sínar, lönd ok lausa aura; þá hófst Uppsala auðr, ok hefir haldizt æ síðan. Á hans dögum hófst Fróða friðr, þá var ok ár um öll lönd; kendu Svíar þat Frey. Var hann því meir dýrkaðr en önnur goðin, sem á hans dögum varð landsfól
2 CONC kit auðgara en fyrr af friðinum ok ári. Gerðr Gýmis dóttir hét kona hans; sonr þeirra hét Fjölnir.[2][3]
2 CONT
2 CONT Frey took the kingdom after Njord, and was called drot by the Swedes, and they paid taxes to him. He was, like his father, fortunate in friends and in good seasons. Frey built a great temple at Upsal, made it his chief seat, and g
2 CONC ave it all his taxes, his land, and goods. Then began the Upsal domains, which have remained ever since. Then began in his days the Frode- peace; and then there were good seasons, in all the land, which the Swedes ascribed to Frey
2 CONC , so that he was more worshipped than the other gods, as the people became much richer in his days by reason of the peace and good seasons. His wife was called Gerd, daughter of Gymis, and their son was called Fjolne.[4][5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Then Snorri tells that after Freyr's death, Fjölnir became the king of Sweden. However, he drowned in a vat of mead visiting Peace-Fróði (Friðfróði), the king of Zealand.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fjölnir, son Yngvifreys, réð þá fyrir Svíum ok Uppsala auð; hann var ríkr ok ársæll ok friðsæll. Þá var Friðfróði at Hleiðru; þeirra í millum var heimboð ok vingan. Þá er Fjölnir fór til Fróða á Selund, þá var þar fyrir búin veizl
2 CONC a mikil ok boðit til víða um lönd. Fróði átti mikinn húsabœ; þar var gert ker mikit margra alna hátt, ok okat með stórum timbrstokkum; þat stóð í undirskemmu, en lopt var yfir uppi, ok opit gólfþilit, svá at þar var niðr hellt leg
2 CONC inum, en kerit blandit fult mjaðar; þar var drykkr furðu sterkr. Um kveldit var Fjölni fylgt til herbergis í hit næsta lopt, ok hans sveit með honum. Um nóttina gékk hann út í svalir at leita sér staðar, var hann svefnœrr ok dauða
2 CONC drukkinn. En er hann snerist aptr til herbergis, þá gékk hann fram eptir svölunum ok til annarra loptdura ok þar inn, missti þá fótum ok féll í mjaðarkerit, ok týndist þar.[6][7]
2 CONT
2 CONT Fjolne, Yngve Frey's son, ruled thereafter over the Swedes and the Upsal domains. He was powerful, and lucky in seasons and in holding the peace. Fredfrode ruled then in Leidre, and between them there was great friendship and visi
2 CONC ting. Once when Fjolne went to Frode in Sealand, a great feast was prepared for him, and invitations to it were sent all over the country. Frode had a large house, in which there was a great vessel many ells high, and put together
2 CONC  of great pieces of timber; and this vessel stood in a lower room. Above it was a loft, in the floor of which was an opening through which liquor was poured into this vessel. The vessel was full of mead, which was excessively stro
2 CONC ng. In the evening Fjolne, with his attendants, was taken into the adjoining loft to sleep. In the night he went out to the gallery to seek a certain place, and he was very sleepy and exceedingly drunk. As he came back to his room
2 CONC  he went along the gallery to the door of another left, went into it, and his foot slipping, he fell into the vessel of mead and was drowned.[8][9]
2 CONT
2 CONT Ynglingatal
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorri also quoted some lines of Ynglingatal, composed in the 9th century:
2 CONT
2 CONT     Varð framgengt,
2 CONT
2 CONT     þars Fróði bjó,
2 CONT
2 CONT     feigðarorð,
2 CONT
2 CONT     es at Fjölni kom;
2 CONT
2 CONT     ok sikling
2 CONT
2 CONT     svigðis geira
2 CONT
2 CONT     vágr vindlauss
2 CONT
2 CONT     of viða skyldi.[10][11]
2 CONT
2 CONT     In Frode's hall the fearful word,
2 CONT
2 CONT     The death-foreboding sound was heard:
2 CONT
2 CONT     The cry of fey denouncing doom,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Was heard at night in Frode's home.
2 CONT
2 CONT     And when brave Frode came, he found
2 CONT
2 CONT     Swithiod's dark chief, Fjolne, drowned.
2 CONT
2 CONT     In Frode's mansion drowned was he,
2 CONT
2 CONT     Drowned in a waveless, windless sea.[12][13]
2 CONT
2 CONT The Historia Norwegiæ provides a Latin summary of Ynglingatal, which precedes Snorri's quotation. It also informs that Fjölnir was the son of Freyr, the father of Svegder and that he drowned in a vat of mead:
2 CONT
2 CONT Froyr vero genuit Fiolni, qui in dolio medonis dimersus est, cujus filius Swegthir [...][14]
2 CONT
2 CONT Frøy engendered Fjolne, who was drowned in a tun of mead. His son, Sveigde, [...][15]
2 CONT
2 CONT The even earlier source Íslendingabók cites the line of descent in Ynglingatal and also gives Fjölnir as the successor of Freyr and the predecessor of Svegðir. In addition to this it summarizes that Fjölnir died at Friðfróði's (i.
2 CONC e. Peace-Fróði): iii Freyr. iiii Fjölnir. sá er dó at Friðfróða. v Svegðir:
2 CONT
2 CONT Gesta Danorum
2 CONT
2 CONT In Gesta Danorum, Book 1, Frodi corresponds to Hadingus and Fjölnir to Hundingus, but the story is a little different. It relates how King Hundingus of Sweden believed a rumor that King Hadingus of Denmark had died and held his ob
2 CONC sequies with ceremony, including an enormous vat of ale. Hundingus himself served the ale, but accidentally stumbled and fell into the vat, choked, and drowned. When word of this came to King Hadingus of this unfortunate death, Ki
2 CONC ng Hadingus publicly hanged himself (see Freyr).
2 CONT
2 CONT Ballad of Veraldur
2 CONT
2 CONT Dumézil (1973, Appendix I) cites a Faroese ballad recorded in 1840 about Odin and his son Veraldur. It is believed that this Veraldur is related to Fjölnir and Freyr, as per Snorri's statement that Freyr was veraldar goð ("god of 
2 CONC the world").
2 CONT
2 CONT In this ballad Veraldur sets off to Zealand to seek the king's daughter in marriage despite Odin's warnings. The king of Zealand mislikes Veraldur and tricks him into falling into a brewing vat in a "hall of stone" where Veraldur 
2 CONC drowns. When Odin hears the news, he decides to die and go to Asgard where his followers will be also be welcomed after death.
2 CONT
2 CONT The tale is similar to that of the death of Fjölnir, son of Freyr, who accidentally fell into a vat of mead and drowned while paying a friendly visit to Fridfródi the ruler of Zealand.
2 CONT
2 CONT Other mentions
2 CONT
2 CONT Fjölnir is also another name for Odin, found in Grímnismál when the god revealed himself to Geirröd, and in Reginsmál when he was standing on a mountain addressing Sigurd and Regin. Snorri also mentions it as an Odinic name in Gyl
2 CONC faginning.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Fjölner var son till Yngvefrey och styrde över svearna och Uppsala öd. Han var mäktig och gav god årsskörd och fred. Fjölner omkom när hav var på besök hos sin vän Frode på Jylland. Denne hade ett stort kar som var flera alnar hög
2 CONC t och fyllt med mycket stakt mjöd. Detta stod på en hylla under det loft som var Fjölners sovplats. Under natten gick Fjölner ut, mycket sömnig och drucken, för att uträtta sina behov. På återvägen miste han fotfästet och föll i m
2 CONC jödkaret och drunknade.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Fjölnir, Fjölner, Fjolner or Fjolne (1st century BC – early 1st century AD) was a Swedish king of the House of Yngling, at Gamla Uppsala. He appears in a semi-mythological context as the son of Freyr and Gerd.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fjölnir drowned in a vat of mead visiting Peace-Fróði, an equally mythological king of Zealand, where Denmark later appeared. Fjölnir was then succeeded by his son Sveigðir.
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fj%C3%B6lnir
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Fjolne (Fjolnir) eller Fjølne er namnet på ein tidleg konge av Ynglingeætta i Svitjod, rekna som son av guden Frøy og gygra Gjerd Gymesdotter. Han lyt reknast som mytisk, og er omtala i Ynglingesoga og i Den eldste Noregshistoria.
2 CONC  Båe kjelder er samde om at han mista livet då han drukna i ei mjødtønne eller eit bryggekar.
2 CONT
2 CONT Snorre Sturlason har den mest utmåla skildringa av Fjolne. Han er skildra som "Mektug, årsæl og vensæl". Han er nært knytt til Fred-Frode, som var konge i Leidra (Lejre) på Sjælland. Bryggekaret er her "mange alner høgt", og Fjoln
2 CONC e sov på svala over. Om natta laut han opp for å late vatnet, og var fælt drukken, slik at han snåva og ramla uti bryggekaret. Her drukna han.
2 CONT
2 CONT Tjodolv frå Kvine fortel om hendinga i Ynglingatal:
2 CONT
2 CONT Feig var det sagt
2 CONT
2 CONT at Fjolne var,
2 CONT
2 CONT i garden hans Frode
2 CONT
2 CONT han fekk det å kjenna
2 CONT
2 CONT Der var han lagi,
2 CONT
2 CONT at log åt mjødhorn
2 CONT
2 CONT hans bane vart
2 CONT
2 CONT i vindlaus sjø.
2 CONT
2 CONT Fjolne er var til Svegde, som tok riket etter han.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Andre tilvisingar til Fjolne [endre]
2 CONT
2 CONT Namnet Fjolne finst att i Grimnesmål, og er då eit av dei mange dekknamna til Odin. Sameleis nyttar Odin dette namnet om seg sjølv i Soga om Volsungane, der han helsar Sigurd Fåvnesbane under dette namnet.
2 CONT
2 CONT Det har vore påpeikt at det er eit litterært samantreff at namnet Fjolne går att som nemning på Odin og samstundes på ein son av Frøy. Dette kan vera ei samanblanding av namn, men og ei påminning om at Odin i nokre overleveringar 
2 CONC har hatt Frøy som far. Ein meir tradisjonell Odin-variant er oppført øvst i lista over kongar i Svitjod hjå Snorre, før Ynglingeætta i røynda tek til med Njord. I Den eldste Noregshistoria står Ingve øvst i denne lista, heller enn
2 CONC  Odin. Ingve er rekna som far til Njord i denne teksten. Det er soleis Snorre som har sett Odin inn øvst i kongerekka.
2 CONT
2 CONT Henta frå «http://nn.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fjolne»
2 CONT
