0 @N6000000000944958777@ NOTE Lendmann. Levde ca. 910. 
1 CONT Vikinge-Kåre var lendmann på Voss i Hordaland. Hans ætt satt både på Voss og på Obrestad på Jæren.
1 CONT Vikinge-Kåre levde på Harald Hårfagres tid og antagelig også under Halvdan Svarte, muligens ca. 910
0 @N6000000000945071798@ NOTE Vikinge-Kåre var lendmann på Voss i Hordaland. Hans ætt satt både på Voss og på 
1 CONT Obrestad på Jæren.
1 CONT Vikinge-Kåre levde på Harald Hårfagres tid og antagelig også under Halvdan Svarte,
1 CONT muligens ca. 910.
0 @N6000000003635716592@ NOTE [4707] "Bloodline ... ", p 427-31
0 @N6000000003949700863@ NOTE
1 CONT In the 850's CE Ketil was a prominent viking chieftain. He conquered the Hebrides and the Isle of Man. Some sources refer to him as "King of the Sudreys" but there is little evidence that he himself claimed that title. The Norwegi
1 CONC an king appointed him the ruler of these islands, but he failed to pay tribute to Harald Fairhair and was outlawed, and his family emigrated to Iceland.
1 CONT Among the Norwegian Viking chieftains who fell afoul of King Harald was Ketil Flatnose, son of Bjorn Buna, from whom a great many of the most prominent early settlers of Iceland were descended. Ketil conquered the Hebrides at Hara
1 CONC ld's request, but then refused to pay tribute to Harald. In retaliation, Harald confiscated all of Ketil's Norwegian lands and outlawed Bjorn the Easterner, the only one of Ketil's five children who had remained in Norway. Ketil a
1 CONC nd his daughter, Jorunn Wisdom-Slope, stayed in the Hebrides, but Bjorn and his other three children, son Helgi Bjolan and daughters Aud the Deep-Minded and Thorunn Hyrna, all went to Iceland.
0 @N6000000007329359330@ NOTE [MAGNUS.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Hadde ʻttefeide med Harald Hårfagre. Herjet i Skottland med sin dattersønn
1 CONT Torstein.
0 @N6000000003232690271@ NOTE Alias:<ALIA> Amblaib or Olaf inn /Hviti/
0 @N6000000003232724818@ NOTE Alias:<ALIA> Amblaib or Olaf inn /Hviti/
0 @N6000000003635723643@ NOTE [4705] "Bloodline ... ", p 427-31, Ruler of Hebrides
0 @N6000000003949700841@ NOTE Jones, Gwyn. The Norse Atlantic Saga: Being the Norse Voyages of Discovery and Settlement to Iceland, Greenland and America. Toronto: Oxford, 1964, pp. 163-187.
1 CONT There was a king named Olaf who was known as Olaf the White: he was a son of king Ingjald, son of Helgi, son of Olaf, son of Gudrod, son of Halfdan Whiteleg the Upplanders' king. Olaf went raiding in the west and conquered Dublin 
1 CONC in Ireland along with the Dublin territory, and made himself its king. He married Aud the Deep-minded, the daughter of Ketil Flatnose, son of Bjorn Buna, a man of rank from Norway; and the name of their son was Thorstein the Red. 
1 CONC Olaf fell in battle there in Ireland, after which Aud and Thorstein made their way to the Hebrides, where Thorstein married Thurid the daughter of Eyvind Eastman and sister of Helgi Magri. They had many children.
1 CONT
0 @N6000000003220314097@ NOTE (Samme slekt som Harald Hårfagre kom fra).
0 @N6000000007329349953@ NOTE [MAGNUS.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Ifølge irske annaler kom det en konge fra Norge til Olav i 870 og bad om hjelp
1 CONT mot fiendene sine. Og Olav dro til Norge med en stor hær, og etter dette hører
1 CONT vi ikke mer om ham. My tyder på at han kjempet mot Harald Hårfagre og falt i
1 CONT Hafrsfjord.
0 @N6000000003635700323@ NOTE [4706] "Bloodline ... ", p 427-31
0 @N6000000003949700852@ NOTE Thorstein became warrior-king and allied himself with earl Sigurd the Mighty, the son of Eystein Glumra, and they conquered Caithness and Sutherland, Ross and Moray, and more than half Scotland. Thorstein made himself king there, 
1 CONC till the Scots betrayed him and he fell there in battle. Aud was in Caithness when she heard tell of Thorstein's death. She had a merchant ship built secretly in the forest, and once she was ready hoisted sail for the Orkneys and 
1 CONC found a husband there for Thorstein the Red's daughter Groa, the mother of that Grelod whom earl Thorfinn Skull-splitter married. After that she set off to seek Iceland and had twenty freemen on board her ship. She reached Iceland
1 CONC  and spent the first winter in Bjarnarhofn with her brother Bjorn. Later Aud took in settlement all Dalelands between Dogurdara and Skraumuhlaupsa. She made her home at Hvamm and had a place for her devotions at Krossholar, where 
1 CONC she had crosses erected, for she had been baptized and held strongly to the Christian faith.
1 CONT Many notable men accompanied her to Iceland who had been taken prisoner during the raiding west and were, in a manner of speaking, slaves. One of these was called Vifil. He was a man of good family who had been taken prisoner over
1 CONC  the western sea and was, nominally at least, a slave till Aud set him free. When Aud gave homes to her ship's crew, Vifil asked why she didn't give him one like the rest of them, but Aud said it would signify little. He would be 
1 CONC counted a fine man, she said, whatever his position. Still, she gave him Vifilsdal and he made his home there. He married a wife, and their sons were Thorgeir and Thorbjorn. These were promising men and grew up with their father.
1 CONT Jones, Gwyn. The Norse Atlantic Saga: Being the Norse Voyages of Discovery and Settlement to Iceland, Greenland and America. Toronto: Oxford, 1964, pp. 163-187.
1 CONT Outrageous Women of the Middle Ages by Vicki Leon, 1998
0 @N6000000003232674434@ NOTE Alias:<ALIA> Eyvind Eastman Rolvson //
