0 @N6000000003051238992@ NOTE Haithabu is the predecessor kingdom of Norway. Also recorded born in 750 and died in 804 in the Irish Sea.
1 CONT He was sometimes known as Wartooth and may be legendary. He was ambitious and warlike and overcame his rivals in Denmark including the islands of Jutland and Skåne and then the ancient kingdom of Gautar and possibly Uppland. He li
1 CONC ved to be very old and preferred to die in battle and had a huge battle at Bravellir with nephew Sigurd Hring who may have been a subking or king of parts of Sweden and was killed.
1 CONT Sometimes recorded as Harald I, King of Denmark
0 @N6000000003635713151@ NOTE [3984] "The Illustrious Lineage of the Royal House of Britain", or Harold, 2nd name difficult to read on original; his father may be Aud "the Deepminded" (chart is not very clear)
0 @N6000000003949839413@ NOTE He was sometimes known as Wartooth and may be legendary. He was ambitious and warlike and overcame his rivals in Denmark including the islands of Jutland and Skåne and then the ancient kingdom of Gautar and possibly Uppland. He li
1 CONC ved to be very old and preferred to die in battle and had a huge battle at Bravellir with nephew Sigurd Hring who may have been a subking or king of parts of Sweden and was killed.   Sometimes recorded as Harald I, King of Denmark
1 CONT The following post-em by Curt Hofemann, curt_hofemann@yahoo.com, puts severe doubts as to the accuracy of the pedigree of the "Kings" of Haithabu, which, even though it is contained in "ES" (a fairly reliable source), is a mixture
1 CONC  of historical and legendary figures:   For a run-down of these purported kings of Haithabu visit Miroslav Marek’s website at: http://genealogy.euweb.cz/scand/norway1.html
1 CONT He relies on ES as his source.
1 CONT On the other hand, a serious caveat emptor per the Gen-Medieval archives:  in my opinion, the "Haithabu" chart (which, as I understand it, was taken from ES) is completely worthless. For the most part, the editors of ES seem to us
1 CONC e reasonably good sources, but they really got careless when they let this chart get into their collection. This chart is typical of many noncritical histories in which the author would take a collection of sources, and then shuff
1 CONC le them together without any regard for whether or not they were reliable, filling in all of the missing pieces with "brilliant deduction" (i.e., pure guesswork). Of the numerous flaws, I will mention a couple:
1 CONT (1) There is no good reason to call these people "kings of Haithabu." The individuals on the chart who appear in the contemporary records and are called kings of "Haithabu" on the chart are called kings of the Danes by the contemp
1 CONC orary Frankish annalists. In fact, there is no good reason to believe that the kingdom ruled by these kings was significantly different than the kingdom ruled later by Gorm the Old (d. 958). In fact, even though the continuous pro
1 CONC ven genealogy of the Danish kings begins with Gorm the Old, it is possible to make a reasonably good list of Danish kings from the late eighth century on (with annoying gaps here and there). Unfortunately, there are no known prova
1 CONC ble descents from these earlier Danish kings. To my knowledge, not a single one of the individuals who are called kings of "Haithabu" in the chart appear under that title in the primary records.
1 CONT (2) Although there are numerous tantalizing pieces to the puzzle, there does not seem to be any general agreement among scholars about how these early Danish kings are related. If you look at different attempts, you generally get 
1 CONC different charts. In such a case, one should always be suspicious about a chart like the "Haithabu" chart, which claims to know too much. I suggest attaching a "Pure Garbage" warning label to the chart. [Ref: Stewart Baldwin 17 Ju
1 CONC n 1996]
1 CONT In fact, one only has to look at ES's chart of the kings of "Haithabu" (Hedeby) in order to find a chart which is so ridiculous that the entire chart should be removed from the book… The Haithabu chart is in Schwennicke's version,
1 CONC  volume 2, table 104. [Ref: Stewart Baldwin 30 Dec 1996]
1 CONT For example, that hideous "Haithabu" chart which appears in ES should never be allowed into a database which has pretensions of being reliable. [Ref: Chris Pitt-Lewis 25 Jan 1998]
1 CONT ...Schwennicke's Europaische Stammtafeln, which is usually a pretty good source, but blundered completely in this "Haithabu" table, which should never have been included. Indeed, although Hedeby (Haithabu) was an important commerc
1 CONC ial center during the period, there is, to my knowledge, no evidence whatsoever that there ever was a "kingdom" named Haithabu. The "Haithabu" table in Schwennicke is a curious attempt to combine the Danish kings mentioned in cont
1 CONC emporary sources with Norwegian kings from the sagas. Some of the kings given in the "Haithabu" chart were in fact historical, and were referred to as kings of the Danes in the contemporary Frankish chronicles, but the relationshi
1 CONC ps given in the tables cannot be trusted. [Ref: Stewart Baldwin 1 Mar 1998]
1 CONT There was never a "kingdom of Haithabu" (or Hedeby, as it was called in Norse). It was the mightiest king (kind of high-king) in the area that controlled Haithabu. [Ref: Frank H. Johanson 1 Mar 1998]
1 CONT Haithabu should never be used. It brings back too many bad memories of what is perhaps Schwennicke's greatest mistake. Besides, (Stewart has, I think, addressed this before) [Ref: Todd A. Farmerie 5 May 1998]
1 CONT See Stewart Baldwin's review of early Danish royalty for this individual. Any source that claims there were kings of Haithabu should not be trusted. [Ref: Todd A. Farmerie 17 Mar 1999]
1 CONT Jim, it is Hedeby in Danish & Haddeby in German. Haithabu bei (near) Haddeby is in the German state of Schleswig-Holstein & both are now incorporated into 24866 Busdorf. It is 1 km south of the city of Schleswig across the western
1 CONC  end of the Schlei (fjord/firth/estuary) & is very small.
1 CONT For a picture showing where Haithabu used to be (now consisting of a very interesting Viking museum business park) go to: http://www.neufi.de/ehaithabu.htm
0 @N6000000001353915961@ NOTE Called "Mikillati" (the magnificent), King of Vestfold and Roumarike;ruled in Norway and in Denmark; probably the "Godfrey the Proud" (andso identified by Moncreiffe, I. 1982) of the Franks who opposed the Emperor Charlemagne. [Ro
1 CONC derick W. Stuart, Royalty for Commoners, 3rd ed., Genealogical Publishing Company, Baltimore MD, 1998]
1 CONT Sources: A. Roots 243A; RC 166, 204; Kraentzler 1609; AF.
1 CONT Roots: Gudrod, "the Magnificent," also called "the Hunting-King," sonof
1 CONT Halfdan "White-Leg" and Asa, according to Moriarty, but Sturleson says
1 CONT great-grandson of Halfdan "White-Leg. Gudrod was King of Vermaland, Vestfold
1 CONT and Vingulmark and was murdered 810-827 at the instigation of (2) wife Asa in
1 CONT revenge for forcibly abducting her and killing, about 800, her fatherand
1 CONT brother. Married (1) Alfhilde, daughter of Alfrim, rulter of Vingulmark;
1 CONT married (2) Asa, daughter of Harald, "Redbeard," King of Agdir.
1 CONT RC: "Mikillati" (the magnificent). King of Vestfold and Roumarike. Ruled in
1 CONT Norway and in Denmark. Probably the "Godfrey the Proud" of the Frankswho
1 CONT opposed Charlemagne. Killed 810.
1 CONT K: Gudrod Halfdansson.
1 CONT RC Note: Moncreiffe adds two generations between Gudrod and Halfdan
1 CONT Olafsson. He says Gudrod's father was Halfdan "the Stingy," King of Vestfold.
1 CONT And he calls Gudrod Godfrey and Proud. Halfdan's father, he says, wasEystein
1 CONT "the Fart," King of Roumarike. "Though the work carries no bibliography,
1 CONT Moncreiffe was an outstanding authority, and pending proof otherwise,may well
1 CONT be considered correct."
