0 @N6000000002187913555@ NOTE Olof Trätälja
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Olaf Tree Feller (Old Norse: Ólafr trételgja, Swedish: Olof Trätälja, Norwegian: Olav Tretelgja) was the son of the Swedish king Ingjald Ill-ruler of the House of Yngling according to Ynglingatal.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Heimskringla
1 CONT
1 CONT His mother was Gauthild, a princess of West götaland, whose maternal grandfather was Olof the Sharp-sighted, the king of Nerike.
1 CONT
1 CONT His mother sent him to his foster-father Bove in West Götaland, where he grew up with his foster-brother Saxe who was surnamed Flette.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Olof heard of his father's death, he assembled the men who were willing to follow him and went to his kinsmen in Nerike, because after his father's atrocities, the Swedes had grown hostile towards the Ynglings.
1 CONT
1 CONT When the Swedes learnt that Olof and his kin had sought refuge in Nerike, they were attacked and had to head west through deep and mountainous forests (Kilsbergen) to Lake Vänern and the estuary of Klarälven (where Karlstad is pre
1 CONC sently situated). Here, they settled and cleared land. Soon they had created a whole province called Värmland, where they could make good living.
1 CONT
1 CONT When the Swedes learnt that Olof was clearing land, they were amused and called him the Tree-feller. Olof married a woman named Solveig who was a daughter of Halfdan Guldtand of Soleyar. Olof and Solveigh had two sons, Ingjald Olo
1 CONC fsson and Halfdan Hvitbeinn, who were brought up in Soleyar in the house of his mother's uncle Sölve.
1 CONT
1 CONT Because of king Ivar Vidfamne and his harsh rule many Swedes emigrated to Värmland, and they became so numerous that the province could not sustain them. The land was afflicted by famine of which the Swedes accused the king. It wa
1 CONC s an old tradition in Sweden of holding the king responsible for the wealth of the land (see Domalde). The Swedes accused Olof of neglecting his sacrifices to the gods and believed that this was the cause of the famine.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Swedish settlers thus rebelled against Olof, surrounded his house on the shores of lake Vänern and burnt him inside it. Thus he was sacrificed to Odin, like his ancestor Domalde.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Ynglingatal and Historia Norwegiae
1 CONT
1 CONT However, Historia Norwegiae says that Olof succeeded his father and ruled as the king of Sweden in peace until his death.
1 CONT
1 CONT     diu et pacifice functus regno plenus dierum obiit in Swethia
1 CONT
1 CONT The lines of Ynglingatal appear to say that he was a Swedish prince (svía jöfri), and that he was burnt inside his hall and disappeared from Gamla Uppsala.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Archaeology
1 CONT
1 CONT     Source
1 CONT
1 CONT Along the lower parts of the river Byälven in Värmland, there are three large barrows, which legend attributes to Olof Trätälja. Moreover, there are many hillforts near this river and the northern shore of Lake Vänern testifying t
1 CONC o a violent period. Archaeological excavations from one of the hillforts, Villkorsberget, show that it was burnt in a period corresponding to Olof (510–680).
0 @N6000000003051240627@ NOTE The kingly line [of Norway], which Snorri [Sturlusson, 13th century historian] traces claimed descent from the ancient Yngling kings who ruled at Uppsala in Sweden. Their legendary Yngling ancestor was Olof Tretelgia Ingjaldsson, 
1 CONC who had escaped the aftermath of his father's conflagration by fleeing to Norway.
1 CONT Her King Olof earned his nickname, which means "the Woodcutter", by clearing the forest and cultivating the land. He named his new domain Varmland and such a large group of Swedes followed him there "that the land could not give t
1 CONC hem sustenance."
0 @N6000000003319700228@ NOTE <blank>
0 @N6000000003949647267@ NOTE "Olaf, the son of Ingjald the Ill-Advised, king in Sweden, cleared Vermaland. He was called Olaf the Tree-Feller. He was fostered in West Gautland by a man named Bofi. Bofi's son was named Saxi, who was called the Plunderer. Olaf'
1 CONC s mother was Gauthild, the daughter of King Algaut, who was the son of King Gautrek the Mild, son of Gaut, after whom Gautland is named. Alof was the mother of Gauthild, daughter of King Olaf the Clear-Sighted, king in Naeriki. At
1 CONC  that time, Ivar Widegrasp had conquered all Denmark and Sweden, and so Olaf and a great multitude of his folk fled, and were proclaimed outlaws by King Ivar. They went north to Vaeni, and cleared the forests and settled in a larg
1 CONC e area that they called Vermaland, and the Swedes elected Olaf the Tree-Feller, and he was their king until old age."
1 CONT His mother sent him to his foster-father Bove in West Götaland, where he grew up with his foster-brother Saxe who was surnamed Flette.
1 CONT When Olof heard of his father's death, he assembled the men who were willing to follow him and went to his kinsmen in Nerike, because after his father's atrocities, the Swedes had grown hostile towards the Ynglings.
1 CONT When the Swedes learnt that Olof and his kin had sought refuge in Nerike, they were attacked and had to head west through deep and mountainous forests (Kilsbergen) to Lake Vänern and the estuary of Klarälven (where Karlstad is pre
1 CONC sently situated). Here, they settled and cleared land. Soon they had created a whole province called Värmland, where they could make good living.
1 CONT When the Swedes learnt that Olof was clearing land, they were amused and called him the Tree-feller. Olof married a woman named Solveig who was a daughter of Halfdan Guldtand of Soleyar. Olof and Solveigh had two sons, Ingjald Olo
1 CONC fsson and Halfdan Hvitbeinn, who were brought up in Soleyar in the house of his mother's uncle Sölve.
1 CONT Because of king Ivar Vidfamne and his harsh rule many Swedes emigrated to Värmland, and they became so numerous that the province could not sustain them. The land was afflicted by famine of which the Swedes accused the king. It wa
1 CONC s an old tradition in Sweden of holding the king responsible for the wealth of the land (see Domalde). The Swedes accused Olof of neglecting his sacrifices to the gods and believed that this was the cause of the famine.
1 CONT The Swedish settlers thus rebelled against Olof, surrounded his house on the shores of lake Vänern and burnt him inside it. Thus he was sacrificed to Odin, like his ancestor Domalde.
0 @N6000000004287328507@ NOTE Sources: RC 166; Kraentzler 1650; AF.  RC: "Trekalia" (tree-hewer). King of Vermaland in Sweden. Sacrificed his own people in time of famine. The last Yngling ruler of Uppsala. Settled in West Sweden.
0 @N6000000005597761126@ NOTE Sources: RC 166; Kraentzler 1650; AF.  RC: "Trekalia" (tree-hewer). King of Vermaland in Sweden. Sacrificed his own people in time of famine. The last Yngling ruler of Uppsala. Settled in West Sweden.
0 @N6000000008799898708@ NOTE [Jeremiah Brown.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Olaf immigrated with his followers from Uppsala in Sweden to Vermeland, which he made habitable by clearing away a great deal of the timber, from which he gets his name Tretelgja, tree-feller.
