0 @N6000000002187913599@ NOTE Ingjald
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Ingjaldr hinn illráði or Ingjald illråde ("ill-ruler"), ca 640 - ca 650, was a legendary Swedish king of the House of Ynglings. He was the son of Anund.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Youth
1 CONT     * 2 The deceit
1 CONT     * 3 Wars
1 CONT     * 4 Downfall
1 CONT     * 5 Secondary sources
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Youth
1 CONT
1 CONT The Heimskringla relates that the viceroy of Fjädrundaland was named Ingvar and he had two sons, Alf and Agnar, who were of the same age as Ingjald. Svipdag the Blind was the viceroy of Tiundaland, the province of Uppsala where th
1 CONC e Tings and the Yule (Midwinter) sacrifices were held (see the Temple at Uppsala).
1 CONT Ingjald's unmanliness is reported
1 CONT Enlarge
1 CONT Ingjald's unmanliness is reported
1 CONT
1 CONT One Midwinter, when Ingjald and Alf were six years old, many people had assembled at Uppsala for the sacrifices. Alf and Ingjald played, but Ingjald found that he was the weaker boy and became so angry that he almost started to cr
1 CONC y. His foster-brother Gautvid led him to his foster-father Svigdag the Blind and told Svipdag about Ingjald's lack of manliness and strength. Svipdag said that it was a shame and the next day he gave Ingjald a roasted wolf's heart
1 CONC  to eat. From that day, Ingjald became a very ferocious person and had a bad disposition.
1 CONT
1 CONT Anund arranged a marriage for his son Ingjald with Gauthild, the daughter of the Geatish king Algaut, who was the son of Gautrek the Mild and the grand-son of Gaut. Gautrek consented as he believed that Ingjald had inherited his f
1 CONC ather's disposition. Gauthild's maternal grandfather was Olof the Sharp-sighted, the king of Nerike.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT The deceit
1 CONT
1 CONT Snorri Sturluson relates that when his father Anund had died, Ingjald became the king of Sweden. The kings at Uppsala were the foremost among the kings of the various provinces since Odin ruled the country, and they were the supre
1 CONC me chiefs of the other kingdoms since the death of Agne and Sweden was divided between Erik and Alrik. The descendants of these two kings had spread, cleared land and settled new territories, until there were several petty kings.
1 CONT
1 CONT In honour of his own ascendance to the throne, Ingjald invited the kings, the jarls and other important men to a grand feast in a newly built hall, just as large and sumptuous as the one in Uppsala. It was called the hall of the s
1 CONC even kings and had seven high seats. Algaut the Geatish king of West Götaland, King Ingvar of Fjädrundaland with his two sons Agnar and Alf, King Sporsnjall of Nerike and King Sigvat of Attundaland came but not King Granmar of Söd
1 CONC ermanland. The kings filled all seven seats but one. All the prominent people of Sweden had seats, except for Ingjald's own court whom he had sent to his old hall in Uppsala.
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the custom of the time for those who inherited kings and jarls, Ingjald rested at the footstool until the Bragebeaker was brought in. Then he was supposed to stand up, take the beaker and make solemn vows, after which
1 CONC  he would ascend his father's high seat. However, when the beaker was brought in, he took a bull's horn and made the solemn vow that he would enlarge his own kingdom by half towards all the four quarters, towards which he pointed 
1 CONC his horn, or die.
1 CONT
1 CONT When all the prominent guests were drunk, he ordered Svipdag's sons, Gautvid and Hylvid, to arm themselves and their men and to leave the building. Outside, they set fire to the building which burnt down and those who tried to esc
1 CONC ape were killed.
1 CONT
1 CONT Thus Ingjald made himself the sole ruler of the domains of the murdered kings.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Wars
1 CONT
1 CONT Granmar won allies in his son-in-law the sea-king Hjörvard of the Ylfings and his father-in-law Högne the Geatish king of East Götaland. They successfully withstood Ingjald's invasion where Ingjald realised that the men from the p
1 CONC rovinces he had conquered were not loyal to him. After a long standstill there was peace for as long as the three kings lived. However, one night Ingjald and his men sourrounded a farm where Granmar and Hjörvard were at a feast an
1 CONC d burnt the house down. He late disposed of five more kings, and he thus earned the name Illråde (ill-ruler) as he fulfilled his promise.
1 CONT
1 CONT Snorri Sturluson tells that it was a common saying that Ingjald killed twelve kings by deceiving them that he only wished for peace, and that he thus earned his cognomen Illråde (ill-ruler or ill-adviser).
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Downfall
1 CONT Ingjald and his daughter Åsa
1 CONT Enlarge
1 CONT Ingjald and his daughter Åsa
1 CONT
1 CONT Ingjald had two children, a son Olof Trätälja and a daughter Åsa. His daughter had inherited her father's psychopathic disposition. She married king Gudröd of Skåne. Before she murdered her husband she managed to make him kill his
1 CONC  own brother Halfdan the Kind, the father of the great Ivar Vidfamne.
1 CONT
1 CONT In order to avenge his father, Ivar Vidfamne gathered a vast host and departed for Sweden, where he found Ingjald at Ræning. Ræning is probably Rällinge on the island of Fogdö in Lake Mälaren, or Rönö Hundred in Södermanland, ment
1 CONC ioned on a runestone as "rauniki". When Ingjald and his daughter realized that it was futile to resist, they set the hall on fire and succumbed in the flames.
1 CONT
1 CONT It is interesting to note that Ynglingatal does not appear to describe Ingjald as an evil king. It calls his life a brave life frœknu fjörvi.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ingjald has often been seen as the one who unified Sweden.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Anund Semi-legendary king of Sweden Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ivar Vidfamne
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Secondary sources
1 CONT
1 CONT Nerman, B. Det svenska rikets uppkomst. Stockholm, 1925.
