1 CONT myth
1 CONT
1 CONT Date: 17 Aug 2004
0 @N6000000003949636549@ NOTE Another Yngling king in Sweden was Ingjald Illrade. From his saga, we learn something about how kings were chosen. When a king died, his successor was supposed to attend the funeral feast and there sit at the foot of the throne.  
1 CONC A huge horn beaker was brought in. The heir had to pledge to do some mighty deed of valor, and then drain the beaker to the bottom. After this ceremony, he was led to the throne and proclaimed king.
1 CONT When Ingjald planned the funeral of his father Anund the Cultivator, he invited all the petty king and jarls (earls) of the neighboring areas to attend the feast. There he stood up, made a vow to increase his kingdom by the half o
1 CONC n every side, and drained the beaker. He then allowed his guests to drink until they became intoxicated. At this point, he left the hall, surrounded it with his men, set it on fire, and thus killed all his potential rivals. The pe
1 CONC ople hated him for his treachery, and named him Ingjald Illrade, or ill-ruler.
1 CONT King Ingjald is said to have been the last king of the Yngling dynasty to rule in Sweden. According to the sagas, he died about 600 AD, by setting fire to his palace on Lake Malaren and thus destroying both himself and his daughte
1 CONC r. It was a fitting end for a man who had murdered his vassals by that very method.
1 CONT In Swede, Ingjald Illrade was succeeded by Ivar Vidfamne (Wide Reacher). Ivar started a new dynasty, called Ivarska after himself.
1 CONT The line of the ill-ruling Ingjald, however, did not die out. His son Olof Tretelgia (Olaf Tree-Hewer) escaped to Norway, where he became the progenitor of the Norwegian Yngling kings.
1 CONT [Royal Families of Medieval Scandinavia, Flanders, and Kiev by Alen & Dahquist, 1997
1 CONT ]
0 @N6000000003051111490@ NOTE He was a very ferocious person with an altogether disagreeable personality. When his father died, he ordered a great hall built and and a great feast prepared for the beginning of his reign, and to it he invited the six other loca
1 CONC l kings: Algaut, his father-in-law; Yngjvar, King of Fjadrydaland, and his two sons; Spornsjall, Kingf of Nerike; Sughvat, King of Aattundaland; and Granmar, King of Sondermanland, who did not attend. That night, he spoke this Bra
1 CONC ge: "I will increase my dominions by one-half, towards all the four corners of the world", and, later, after everyone was drunk and having a good time, Ingjvar had fire set to the new hall and burned everyone to death. Those that 
1 CONC managed to come running out were killed on the spot.
1 CONT
1 CONT DEATH: He and his company were surrounded at Reaning by Ivar Vidfavne's army, and seeing no way of escape, and expecting no quarter, he and his men and daughter set fire to the hall around them and commited mass suicide.Another Yn
1 CONC gling king in Sweden was Ingjald Illrade. From his saga, we learn something about how kings were chosen. When a king died, his successor was supposed to attend the funeral feast and there sit at the foot of the throne. A huge horn
1 CONC  beaker was brought in. The heir had to pledge to do some mighty deed of valor, and then drain the beaker to the bottom. After this ceremony, he was led to the throne and proclaimed king.
1 CONT When Ingjald planned the funeral of his father Anund the Cultivator, he invited all the petty king and jarls (earls) of the neighboring areas to attend the feast. There he stood up, made a vow to increase his kingdom by the half o
1 CONC n every side, and drained the beaker. He then allowed his guests to drink until they became intoxicated. At this point, he left the hall, surrounded it with his men, set it on fire, and thus killed all his potential rivals. The pe
1 CONC ople hated him for his treachery, and named him Ingjald Illrade, or ill-ruler.
1 CONT King Ingjald is said to have been the last king of the Yngling dynasty to rule in Sweden. According to the sagas, he died about 600 AD, by setting fire to his palace on Lake Malaren and thus destroying both himself and his daughte
1 CONC r. It was a fitting end for a man who had murdered his vassals by that very method.
1 CONT In Swede, Ingjald Illrade was succeeded by Ivar Vidfamne (Wide Reacher). Ivar started a new dynasty, called Ivarska after himself.
1 CONT The line of the ill-ruling Ingjald, however, did not die out. His son Olof Tretelgia (Olaf Tree-Hewer) escaped to Norway, where he became the progenitor of the Norwegian Yngling kings.
