0 @N6000000002187913829@ NOTE Eanmund
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
1 CONT
1 CONT Eanmund was a Swedish prince of the Scylfing dynasty. Unlike his relatives, Eanmund is only mentioned in Beowulf. Eanmund was the son of Ohthere and the brother of Eadgils. If he existed in real life, his real name was probably Pr
1 CONC oto-Norse *Aiwamunduz (Old East Norse Emund) or *Amunduz (Old East Norse Amund).
1 CONT
1 CONT Ohthere died and his younger brother Onela, usurped the Swedish throne. Since, their uncle had usurped the throne, Eanmund and Eadgils sought refuge among the Geats. This caused Onela to attack the Geats, an attack which was also 
1 CONC motivated by the fact that the Geatish king Heardred's father had killed Onela's grand-father Ongentheow. During the battle, Eanmund was killed by Onela's champion Weohstan and Heardred was killed as well. Eadgils, however, surviv
1 CONC ed and later, Beowulf helped him avenge Eanmund and Ohthere by slaying Onela, an event which also appears in Scandinavian sources.
1 CONT
1 CONT Weohstan took Eanmund's sword which was inherited by his son Wiglaf who used the sword when fighting the dragon together with Beowulf.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Secondary sources
1 CONT
1 CONT Nerman, B. Det svenska rikets uppkomst. Stockholm, 1925.
0 @N6000000002187913754@ NOTE Eadgils
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Eadgils was a 6th century king of Sweden who appears in the Old English epic Beowulf.
1 CONT
1 CONT Eadgils is generally considered to be the same king as Aðils, or Athisl, who appears in the Norse sagas and Gesta Danorum. This king was also the son of a king named Ohthere and just like in Beowulf, Adils killed a king named Onel
1 CONC a (Ali) with foreign assistance (see also Origins for Beowulf and Hrólf Kraki).
1 CONT Adils pursuing Hrolf Kraki on the Fýrisvellir
1 CONT Enlarge
1 CONT Adils pursuing Hrolf Kraki on the Fýrisvellir
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Name
1 CONT     * 2 Beowulf
1 CONT     * 3 In the Hrólf Kraki traditions and Heimskringla
1 CONT     * 4 Archaeology
1 CONT     * 5 Gesta Danorum
1 CONT     * 6 Primary sources
1 CONT     * 7 Secondary sources
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Name
1 CONT
1 CONT The Norse forms are based an older (Proto-Norse) *Aþagislaz. However, the Anglo-Saxon form is not etymologically identical. The A-S form would have been *Ædgils, but Eadgils (Proto-Norse *Auða-gislaz, Old West Norse Auðgísl, Old E
1 CONC ast Norse Øðgisl) was the only corresponding name used by the Anglo-Saxons.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Beowulf
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the oldest source, Beowulf, Eadgils' uncle Onela usurped the Swedish throne. Eadgils rebelled together with his brother Eanmund and fled to the Geats. Then Onela attacked the Geats and the Geatish king Heardred was ki
1 CONC lled, as was Eanmund by Onela's champion Weohstan. Then Beowulf became king of the Geats and helped Eadgils in defeating Onela, who was killed by Eadgils.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT In the Hrólf Kraki traditions and Heimskringla
1 CONT
1 CONT In the Hrólf Kraki tradition, Adils (Eadgils) captured and married Yrsa (Yrs), the mother of Hrólf Kraki (Hrothulf). Not long after Hrólf became king, Eadgils requested his assistance against his uncle Áli (Onela). Hrólf sent his 
1 CONC twelve companions, led by Bödvar Bjarki (who it is often remarked has similarities with Beowulf), Áli was defeated in the Battle on the Ice and Adils gained the kingdom. But when Adils refused to pay Hrólf's men the expected tribu
1 CONC tes for their help, Hrólf Kraki set off to Gamla Uppsala. As Adils was away, gathering reinforcements, Hrólf's mother and sister Yrsa then gave him a horn filled with gold and jewels and a famous ring called Sviagris 'Pig of the S
1 CONC wedes'. With the treasure given them by Yrsa, Hrólf and his men try to escape over the Fyrisvellir (Fyris Wolds). When Adils and his men pursued them, Hrólf desperately spilled out the gold to occupy the pursuers with treasure col
1 CONC lecting instead. Adils, however, overtook Hrólf who desperately threw away Sviagris. When Adils stooped to pick it up with his spear Hrólf ungloriously cut him in the back screaming that he had bent the back of the most powerful m
1 CONC an in Sweden, stole the ring once again and fled.
1 CONT
1 CONT Snorri Sturluson relates that Adils loved good horses and had the best horses in his days (interestingly, the contempary Gothic scholar Jordanes noted that the Swedes were famed for their good horses). One horse was named Slöngve 
1 CONC and another one Raven, which he had taken from Áli. From this horse he had bred a horse also named Raven which he sent to king Godgest of Hålogaland, but Godgest could not manage it and fell from it and died, in Omd on the island 
1 CONC of Andøya. Adils himself died in a similar way at the Dísablót. Adils was riding around the Disa shrine when Raven stumbled and fell, and the king was thrown forward and hit his skull on a stone. He was buried at Uppsala, and was 
1 CONC succeeded by Östen.
1 CONT
1 CONT     Witch-demons, I have heard men say,
1 CONT     Have taken Adils' life away.
1 CONT     The son of kings of Frey's great race,
1 CONT     First in the fray, the fight, the chase,
1 CONT     Fell from his steed -- his clotted brains
1 CONT     Lie mixed with mire on Upsal's plains.
1 CONT     Such death (grim Fate has willed it so)
1 CONT     Has struck down Ole's [Onela's] deadly foe. (Laing's translation [1])
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Archaeology
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Snorri he was buried in one of the royal mounds of Gamla Uppsala, and he is believed to be buried in Adils' Mound (also known as the Western mound or Thor's mound) one of the largest mounds at Uppsala. An excavation i
1 CONC n this mound showed that a man was buried there c. 575 on a bear skin with two dogs and rich grave offerings. There were luxurious weapons and other objects, both domestic and imported, show that the buried man was very powerful. 
1 CONC These remains include a Frankish sword adorned with gold and garnets and a board game with Roman pawns of ivory. He was dressed in a costly suit made of Frankish cloth with golden threads, and he wore a belt with a costly buckle. 
1 CONC There were four cameos from the Middle East which were probably part of a casket. The finds show the distant contacts of the House of Yngling in the 6th century.
1 CONT
1 CONT Snorri's account that Adils had the best horses of his days, and Jordanes' account that the Swedes of the 6th century were famed for their horses find support in archaeology. This time was the beginning of the Vendel Age, a time c
1 CONC haracterised by the appearance of stirrups and a powerful mounted warrior elite in Sweden, which rich graves in for instance Valsgärde and Vendel.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Onela Semi-legendary king of Sweden Succeeded by:
1 CONT Östen
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Gesta Danorum
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Saxo Grammaticus, Eadgils (Athisl) was defeated by Roulf (Hrólf Kraki, Hrothulf) and replaced. For a discussion about this peculiarity, see Heoroweard.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Primary sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Annals of Lund
1 CONT     * Beowulf
1 CONT     * Bjarkarímur
1 CONT     * Gesta Danorum
1 CONT     * Prose Edda
1 CONT     * Heimskringla
1 CONT     * Historia Norvegiæ
1 CONT     * Hrólf Kraki's Saga
1 CONT     * Skjöldunga saga (what remains of it)
1 CONT     * Ynglingatal
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Secondary sources
1 CONT
1 CONT Nerman, B. Det svenska rikets uppkomst. Stockholm, 1925.
1 CONT
1 CONT See also
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Nordisk familjebok
0 @N6000000003051295065@ NOTE The twentieth king of the Yngling dynasty in Sweden was said to be Adil. This legendary monarch was married to Yrsa, the daughter of King Helge of Denmark. Yrsa, although Helge's daughter, had also been his lover and had born him 
1 CONC a son named Rolf, who later became king of Denmark.
1 CONT There was, predictably, a great deal of jealousy and fighting between Adil of Sweden and Helge of Denmark. In the end, Helge was treacherously murdered by Adil.
1 CONT Adil did not live long after this. While at a great sacrifice in Uppsala, he died by falling from a horse, a death which was considered shameful. Yrsa's son Rolf, on the other hand, died in battle, which was considered glorious. R
1 CONC olf's praises were then sung over all of Scandinavia.
0 @N6000000003220311408@ NOTE {geni:comment} http://lind.no/nor/index.asp?lang=gb&emne=asatru&person=Adils%20Ottarsson
1 CONT {geni:comment} http://www.look.no/anita/slekt/webcards/ps05/ps05_008.htm
1 CONT {geni:comment} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Eadgils
0 @N6000000003319700278@ NOTE
1 CONT also known as: Athils
0 @N6000000009304945706@ NOTE #Générale#Profession : Roi de Uppland
0 @N6000000003949721514@ NOTE He was a long time king, became very rich, and went also for several summers on viking expeditions. On one of these he came to Saxland with his troops. There a king was reigning called Geirthjof, and his wife was called Alof the G
1 CONC reat; but nothing is told of their children. The king was not at home, and Adils and his men ran up to the king's house and plundered it, while others drove a herd of cattle down to the strand. The herd was attended by slave-peopl
1 CONC e, churls, and girls, and they took all of them together. Among them was a remarkably beautiful girl called Yrsa. Adils returned home with this plunder. Yrsa was not one of the slave girls, and it was soon observed that she was in
1 CONC telligent, spoke well, and in all respects was well behaved. All people thought well of her, and particularly the king; and at last it came to this that the king celebrated his wedding with her, and Yrsa became queen of Sweden, an
1 CONC d was considered an excellent woman.
1 CONT King Halfdan's son Helge ruled at that time over Leidre. He came to Sweden with so great an army, that King Adils saw no other way than to fly at once. King Helge landed with his army, plundered, and made a great booty. He took Qu
1 CONC een Yrsa prisoner, carried her with him to Leidre, took her to wife, and had a son by her called Rolf Krake. When Rolf was three years old, Queen Alof came to Denmark, and told Queen Yrsa that her husband, King Helge, was her own 
1 CONC father, and that she, Alof, was her mother. Thereupon Yrsa went back to Sweden to King Adils, and was queen there as long as she lived. King Helge fell in a war expedition; and Rolf Krake, who was then eight years old, was taken t
1 CONC o be king in Leidre. King Adils had many disputes with a king called Ole of the Uplands; and these kings had a battle on the ice of the Venner lake, in which King Ole fell, and King Adils won the battle. There is a long account of
1 CONC  this battle in the "Skjoldunga Saga", and also about Rolf Krake's coming to Adils, and sowing gold upon the Fyrisvold. King Adils was a great lover of good horses, and had the best horses of these times. One of his horses was cal
1 CONC led Slongve, and another Raven. This horse he had taken from Ole on his death, and bred from him a horse, also called Raven, which the king sent in a present to King Godgest in Halogaland. When Godgest mounted the horse he was not
1 CONC  able to manage him, and fell off and was killed. This accident happened at Omd in Halogaland. King Adils was at a Disa sacrifice; and as he rode around the Disa hall his horse' Raven stumbled and fell, and the king was thrown for
1 CONC ward upon his head, and his skull was split, and his brains dashed out against a stone. 
