0 @N6000000002187913848@ NOTE Ongenþeow
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Ongenþeow, Ongentheow, Ongendþeow, Egil, Egill, Eigil, or Angantyr (- ca 515) was the name of one or two semi-legendary Swedish kings of the house of Scylfings, who appear in Anglo-Saxon and Scandinavian sources.
1 CONT
1 CONT The names are different and have little etymological connection. Ongenþeow would in Proto-Norse have been *Anganaþewaz, whereas Egil would have been *Agilaz. The reason why they are thought to have been the same is that they have 
1 CONC the same position in the line of Swedish kings and are described as the fathers of Ohthere and grand-fathers of Eadgils. As will be shown below, it can be argued that they are based on the same person and the same events, but it s
1 CONC hould be noted that not every scholar is open to the historicity of the characters in Beowulf and in the Norse sagas.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Scandinavian tradition
1 CONT     * 2 Anglo-Saxon sources
1 CONT     * 3 Comments
1 CONT     * 4 Primary sources
1 CONT     * 5 Secondary sources
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Scandinavian tradition
1 CONT
1 CONT In Ari Þorgilsson's Íslendingabók and in Historia Norwegiae, he was called Egil Vendelcrow (Vendilcraca/Vendilkráka, a name traditionally given to those living at the royal estate of Vendel in Sweden). Snorri Sturluson, however, g
1 CONC ave the name Vendelcrow to Egil's son Ottar (Ohthere). In these sources, Egil was the son of Aun the Old, and like him, not very warlike. After he had made the thrall Tunni (or Tonne) responsible for the treasury, Tunni rebelled a
1 CONC gainst Egil. They fought eight battles after which Egil fled to Denmark, according to the Ynglinga saga (Ynglingatal does not mention where he fled and Historia Norwegiae does not mention any escape at all). Snorri wrote that Fród
1 CONC i, the Danish king, aided Egil in defeating Tunni, and made Egil a tributary to the Danish king.
1 CONT
1 CONT Egil was killed by a bull during the sacrifices at the Temple at Uppsala.
1 CONT
1 CONT     Ok lofsæll
1 CONT     ór landi fló
1 CONT     Týs óttungr
1 CONT     Tunna ríki,
1 CONT     en flæming
1 CONT     farra trjónu
1 CONT     jötuns eykr
1 CONT     á Agli rauð.
1 CONT     Sás of austr
1 CONT     áðan hafði
1 CONT     brúna hörg
1 CONT     of borinn lengi,
1 CONT     en skíðlauss
1 CONT     Skilfinga nið
1 CONT     hœfis hjörr
1 CONT     til hjarta stóð.[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT     The fair-haired son of Odin's race,
1 CONT     Who fled before fierce Tunne's face,
1 CONT     Has perished by the demon-beast
1 CONT     Who roams the forests of the East.
1 CONT     The hero's breast met the full brunt
1 CONT     Of the wild bull's shaggy front;
1 CONT     The hero's heart's asunder torn
1 CONT     By the fell Jotun's spear-like horn."(Laing's translation [2])
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Anglo-Saxon sources
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Beowulf, his wife and two sons Onela (Áli) and Ohthere (Ottar) were captured by the Geatish king Hæþcyn. The old Ongentheow saved them, killed Hæþcyn, and captured the Geats in a location called Hrefnesholt.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Geats were, however, saved by their new king Hygelac. Ongenþeow and his men sought refuge on higher ground in a fortification, but it was overrun by the Geats. Eofor killed the hoary-bearded Ongenþeow during a vicious battle w
1 CONC here the old man wounded Eofor's brother Wulf.
1 CONT
1 CONT Ongenþeow is also mentioned in passing by the earlier poem Widsith as the king of Sweden:
1 CONT lines 30–33:
1 CONT Wald Woingum, Wod þyringum, Wald the Woings, Wod the Thuringians,
1 CONT Sæferð Sycgum, Sweom Ongendþeow, Saeferth the Sycgs, the Swedes by Ongendtheow,
1 CONT Sceafthere Ymbrum, Sceafa Longbeardum Sceafthere the Ymbers, Sceafa the Lombards,
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Comments
1 CONT
1 CONT The two versions seem contradictory, but it has been shown that the two stories may very well describe the same event (Schück H. 1907, Nerman B. 1925), and that Ynglingatal was probably misinterpreted by Snorri due to a different 
1 CONC dialectal meaning of the word farra. In Ynglingatal, it says
1 CONT
1 CONT     en flæming
1 CONT     farra trjónu
1 CONT     jötuns eykr
1 CONT     á Agli rauð.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT If there is any authenticity behind the traditions, the origin of Ynglingatal was most probably a Swedish poem which has not survived (see also Sundquist 2004). In Old Swedish, farra did not mean "bull" but it meant "boar". Moreov
1 CONC er, in Old Norse Trjóna normally meant a pig's snout (modern Scandinavian tryne). Flæmingr meant "sword" (originally a flemish sword imported by Vikings).
1 CONT
1 CONT Moreover, the sword of the snout can hardly refer to the horns of a bull, but it is more natural to interpret it as the tusks of a boar. In English, the lines can be translated as but the giant beast coloured its tusk red on Egil.
1 CONT
1 CONT In Anglo-Saxon, the name eofor meant "boar" and consequently Ynglingatal could very well relate of Eofor (the boar) killing Egil with kennings for boars. These kennings, sung originally by Swedes, were later misinterpreted by Norw
1 CONC egians and Icelanders as literal expressions due to the different dialectal meanings of farra.
1 CONT
1 CONT Moreover, according to Schück, the name Tunni which has no meaning in Old Norse should in Proto-Norse have been *Tunþa and derived from *Tunþuz. Consequently, it would have been the same word as the Gothic Tunþus which meant "toot
1 CONC h". This would mean that the name of Egil's enemy, actually meant "tooth" and Tunni and the bull/boar would consquently have been the same enemy, i.e. Eofor.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Aun Semi-legendary king of Sweden Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ohthere
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Primary sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ynglingatal
1 CONT     * Ynglinga saga (part of the Heimskringla)
1 CONT     * Historia Norwegiae
1 CONT     * Beowulf
1 CONT     * Widsith
1 CONT     * Íslendingabók
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Secondary sources
1 CONT
