0 @N6000000000437075068@ NOTE !NAME: Nickname: "The Aged"
0 @N6000000000437403163@ NOTE !NAME: Nickname: "The Aged"
0 @N6000000002187913866@ NOTE Aun
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Ane, On, One, Auchun or Aun the Old (Audhun, the same name as the A-S name Edwin) was the son of Jorund and one of the Swedish kings of the House of Yngling, the ancestors of Norway's first king, Harald Fairhair.
1 CONT
1 CONT Aun was a wise king who sacrificed greatly to the gods, but he was not a warlike king and preferred to live in peace. Consequently, he was attacked by the Danish prince Halfdan (the son of Fródi, the son of Dan the Arrogant, the f
1 CONC ounder of Denmark). Aun lost the battles and fled to the Geats in Västergötland, where he stayed for 25 years until Halfdan died in his bed in Uppsala and was buried in a mound.
1 CONT
1 CONT King Aun could return to Uppsala, but he was 60 years old. In order to live longer he sacrificed his own son to Odin who promised him that he could live for another 60 years. However, after 25 years, Aun was attacked by Halfdan's 
1 CONC cousin Ale the Strong. Aun lost several battles and had to flee a second time to Västergötland. Ale the Strong ruled in Uppsala for 25 years until he was killed by Starkad the old.
1 CONT
1 CONT After Ale the Strong's death, Aun could return to Uppsala. Once again, Aun sacrificed a son to Odin, but this time Odin said that he would live as long as he sacrificed a son every ten years and that he had to name one of the Swed
1 CONC ish provinces after the number of sons he sacrificed.
1 CONT
1 CONT When Aun had sacrificed a son for the seventh time, he was so old that he could not walk but had to be carried on a chair. When he had sacrificed a son for the eighth time, he could no longer get out of his bed. When he had sacrif
1 CONC iced his ninth son, he was so old that he had to feed by suckling a horn like a little child.
1 CONT
1 CONT After ten years he wanted to sacrifice his tenth and last son and name the province of Uppsala the ten lands. However, the Swedes refused to allow him this sacrifice and so he died. He was buried in a mound at Uppsala and succeede
1 CONC d by his last son Egil. From that day, dying in bed of old age was called Aun's sickness among the Scandinavians.
1 CONT
1 CONT     Knátti endr
1 CONT     at Upsölum
1 CONT     ána-sótt
1 CONT     Aun of standa,
1 CONT     ok þrálífr
1 CONT     þiggja skyldi
1 CONT     jóðs alað
1 CONT     öðru sinni.
1 CONT     Ok sveiðurs
1 CONT     at sér hverfði
1 CONT     mækis hlut
1 CONT     enn mjávara,
1 CONT     es okhreins
1 CONT     óttunga hrjóðr
1 CONT     lögðis odd
1 CONT     liggjandi drakk;
1 CONT     máttit hárr
1 CONT     hjarðar mæki
1 CONT     austrkonungr
1 CONT     upp of halda.[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT     In Upsal's town the cruel king
1 CONT     Slaughtered his sons at Odin's shrine --
1 CONT     Slaughtered his sons with cruel knife,
1 CONT     To get from Odin length of life.
1 CONT     He lived until he had to turn
1 CONT     His toothless mouth to the deer's horn;
1 CONT     And he who shed his children's blood
1 CONT     Sucked through the ox's horn his food.
1 CONT     At length fell Death has tracked him down,
1 CONT     Slowly, but sure, in Upsal's town. (Laing's translation [2])
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Jorund Mythological king of Sweden
1 CONT First reign  Succeeded by:
1 CONT Halfdan
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Halfdan Mythological king of Sweden
1 CONT Second reign  Succeeded by:
1 CONT Ale the Strong
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Ale the Strong Mythological king of Sweden
1 CONT Third reign  Succeeded by:
1 CONT Egil Ongenþeow
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Primary sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ynglingatal
1 CONT     * Ynglinga saga (part of the Heimskringla)
1 CONT     * Historia Norwegiae
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Secondary sources
1 CONT
1 CONT Nerman, B. Det svenska rikets uppkomst. Stockholm, 1925.
0 @N6000000003051245174@ NOTE King Aun sacrificed one of his sons to Odin every ten years in order to prolong his life. After he had sacrificed nine sons and attained to the age of one hundred and ninety, his subjects forbade him to sacrifice his tenth son, an
1 CONC d he died of old age, so weak that he had to suck nourishment from a horn like a baby. While his life was ignobly prolonged, he missed out on the greatest glory a Viking could imagine - that of dying nobly in battle. (legends of S
1 CONC wedish kings) [Royal Families of Medieval Scandinavia, Flanders, and Kiev]
0 @N6000000000435173664@ NOTE King in Uppsala
0 @N6000000003319700310@ NOTE
1 CONT also known as: Ani
0 @N6000000003949530510@ NOTE King Aun sacrificed one of his sons to Odin every ten years in order to prolong his life. After he had sacrificed nine sons and attained to the age of one hundred and ninety, his subjects forbade him to sacrifice his tenth son, an
1 CONC d he died of old age, so weak that he had to suck nourishment from a horn like a baby. While his life was ignobly prolonged, he missed out on the greatest glory a Viking could imagine - that of dying nobly in battle. 
0 @N6000000003796207358@ NOTE ==================================================================
1 CONT
1 CONT A Grandchild's Heritage URL:  http://www.reach.net/~roanne/macmurrough.html
1 CONT
1 CONT Generation No. 53-59
1 CONT
1 CONT 53 Cu-Corb: his son; King of Leinster. 
1 CONT He had the following sons: 
1 CONT Niadh Corb, who is described below 
1 CONT Messincorb, a quo Dal Messincorb 
1 CONT Cormac, a quo Dal Cormaic, and who was the ancestor of Quirk 
1 CONT Cairbre Dluitheachar 
1 CONT
1 CONT 54 Niadh Corb: his son. He was a most valiant and warlike prince, "Nia" signifies hero. Had following sons: 
1 CONT Cormac Gealtach, who is described below 
1 CONT Ceathramhadh 
1 CONT
1 CONT 55 Cormac Gealtach: his son. He succeeded his father and was a great general, and led the Irish army into Scotland, to assist the Picts and Scots against the Romans, who were commanded by Agricola. The battle with the Romans on th
1 CONC e Grampian Hills was fierce and bloody; but the superior discipline of the Roman legions made it decisive in their favor. At his death he was succeeded by his son... 
1 CONT
1 CONT 56 Felim Fiorurglas: his son. Had the following children: 
1 CONT Cathair Mor, who is described below 
1 CONT Main Mal, the ancestor of O'Kelly of Cualan (in Wicklow), O'Tighe, and O'Cuallan 
1 CONT Eithne 
1 CONT
1 CONT 57 Cathair Mor, 109th Monarch of Ireland in the beginning of the 2nd century: son of Felim Fiorurglas. 
1 CONT
1 CONT 58 Fiacha Baicheda: youngest son of Cathair Mor; died 220. His father praised him for his bravery and spirit, and for the universal love he gained. He was called "the lame" on account of a wound he received in the battle of Moigh 
1 CONC Acha, where his father was slain. From his posterity came the majority of the kings of Leinster 
1 CONT
1 CONT 59 Breasal Bealach (large lipped): his son; was the 2nd Christian King of Leinster. Had following sons: 
1 CONT Labhradh, who is described below 
1 CONT Enna Niadh, who begins family of O'Tooles 
1 CONT
1 CONT The ancient kings of Leinster (Ui Cinnsealaigh = Hy Kinsella) had fortresses or royal residences at Dinn Righ, near the river Barrow, between Carlow and Leighlin; at Naas, in Kildare; and, in after times at the city of Ferns in We
1 CONC xford, which was their capital; and also at Old Ross in Wexford; and at Ballymoon in Carlow. The Ui Cinnsealaigh were inaugurated as kings of Leinster at a place called Cnoc-an-Bhogha, attended by O'Nolan, who was the King's Marsh
1 CONC al, and Chief of Forth in Carlow; by O'Doran, Chief Brehon of Leinster; and by MacKeogh, their Chief Bard. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The major Leinster families begin to split off at this point. 
1 CONT
1 CONT ===================================================================
0 @N6000000007329383169@ NOTE [MAGNUS.FTW]
1 CONT
1 CONT Skal ha levd samtidig med kong Adils i Uppsala. Adils sendte ham en hest som
1 CONT het Ravn. En dag Godgest var ute og red, løp hesten ut for ham, og han ble
1 CONT drept.
