0 @N6000000003796269150@ NOTE The Yngling Saga of Heimskingla
1 CONT The Saga of the Norse Kings by Snorri Sturleson
1 CONT (dates may be off):
1 CONT
1 CONT 13. JORUND - King in Uppsala, Sweden from 302 until he was hanged in 312 A.D. He went on many expeditions, which suddenly ended when he was captured and hanged in 312. His son was:
1 CONT
1 CONT 14. AUN (ON OR ANE) THE OLD 
0 @N6000000002187690904@ NOTE Jorund
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Jorund or Eorund was a Swedish king of the House of Yngling. He was the son of Yngvi, and he had reclaimed the throne of Sweden for his dynasty from Haki (the brother of Hagbard, the hero of the legend of Hagbard and Signy, and it
1 CONC  should be noted that Snorri cites two kennings from this legend Sigar's steed and Hagard's fell noose, when telling of Jorund).
1 CONT
1 CONT Snorri Sturluson relates that when Jorund was young he used to travel the seas and plunder with his brother Erik, and they were great warriors. One summer they plundered in Denmark where they met another pillager, King Gudlög of H
1 CONC ålogaland (a province in Norway) with whom they fought. They took him prisoner and carried him ashore at Stromones where they hanged him. Gudlaug's surviving companions raised a mound over him there.
1 CONT
1 CONT Snorri then cites a Norwegian skald named Eyvindr skáldaspillir:
1 CONT
1 CONT     En Guðlaugr
1 CONT     grimman tamdi
1 CONT     við ofrkapp
1 CONT     austrkonunga
1 CONT     Sigars jó,
1 CONT     er synir Yngva
1 CONT     menglötuð
1 CONT     við meið reiddu.
1 CONT     Og náreiðr
1 CONT     á nesi drúpir
1 CONT     vingameiðr,
1 CONT     þar er víkur deilir,
1 CONT     þar er fjölkunnt
1 CONT     um fylkis hreyr
1 CONT     steini merkt,
1 CONT     Straumeyjarnes.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT     By the fierce East-kings' cruel pride,
1 CONT     Gudlog must on the wild horse ride --
1 CONT     The wildest horse you e'er did see:
1 CONT     'Tis Sigur's steed -- the gallows tree.
1 CONT     At Stromones the tree did grow,
1 CONT     Where Gudlog's corpse waves on the bough.
1 CONT     A high stone stands on Stromo's heath,
1 CONT     To tell the gallant hero's death.(Laing's translation [1])
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT This act rendered the Swedish princes, Eric and Jorund, even more famous and they were thought of as even greater men. When they learnt that King Haki no longer had his forces around him, they decided to take care of their enemy. 
1 CONC They assembled a large force that was joined by Swedes as they approached. They entered Mälaren (a bay at the time) and steered towards Uppsala. They left their ships at the Fyris Wolds and were met by Haki who had less men. Haki 
1 CONC was a brutal fighter and managed to turn the tide of the battle. He slew Erik who held the banner and Jorund retreated with his men. Luckily, Haki had been seriously wounded and died.
1 CONT
1 CONT Jorund then ruled Sweden at Uppsala, but he usually spent the summers pillaging. One summer, he plundered in Jutland and entered Limfjorden, where he continued the pillaging. They anchored in Oddesund (before a storm in 1825, it w
1 CONC as near the innermost part of the fjord and almost 200 km from its mouth) but were discovered by the Norwegian pirate Gylaug of Hålogaland, the son of Gudlaug. Gylaug and his men attacked them and were joined by local forces who w
1 CONC anted revenge. As Jorund was vastly outnumbered (and had to run an almost 200 km long gauntlet to get out of the fjord), he lost the battle, and Gylaug had him hanged.
1 CONT
1 CONT Snorri illustrates this event with the stanza from Ynglingatal:
1 CONT
1 CONT     Varð Jörundr
1 CONT     hinn er endr of dó,
1 CONT     lífs of lattr
1 CONT     í Limafirði,
1 CONT     þá er hábrjóstr
1 CONT     hörva Sleipnir
1 CONT     bana Goðlaugs
1 CONT     of bera skyldi;
1 CONT     ok Hagbarðs
1 CONT     hersa valdi
1 CONT     höðnu leif
1 CONT     at halsi gekk.[2]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT     Jorund has travelled far and wide,
1 CONT     But the same horse he must bestride
1 CONT     On which he made brave Gudlog ride.
1 CONT     He too must for a necklace wear
1 CONT     Hagbert's fell noose in middle air.
1 CONT     The army leader thus must ride
1 CONT     On Horva's horse, at Lymfjord's side.[3]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Haki Mythological king of Sweden Succeeded by:
1 CONT Aun
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Primary sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ynglingatal
1 CONT     * Ynglinga saga (part of the Heimskringla)
1 CONT     * Historia Norwegiae
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Secondary sources
1 CONT
1 CONT Nerman, B. Det svenska rikets uppkomst. Stockholm, 1925.
0 @N6000000003796207338@ NOTE ==================================================================
1 CONT
1 CONT A Grandchild's Heritage URL:  http://www.reach.net/~roanne/macmurrough.html
1 CONT
1 CONT Generation No. 53-59
1 CONT
1 CONT 53 Cu-Corb: his son; King of Leinster. 
1 CONT He had the following sons: 
1 CONT Niadh Corb, who is described below 
1 CONT Messincorb, a quo Dal Messincorb 
1 CONT Cormac, a quo Dal Cormaic, and who was the ancestor of Quirk 
1 CONT Cairbre Dluitheachar 
1 CONT
1 CONT 54 Niadh Corb: his son. He was a most valiant and warlike prince, "Nia" signifies hero. Had following sons: 
1 CONT Cormac Gealtach, who is described below 
1 CONT Ceathramhadh 
1 CONT
1 CONT 55 Cormac Gealtach: his son. He succeeded his father and was a great general, and led the Irish army into Scotland, to assist the Picts and Scots against the Romans, who were commanded by Agricola. The battle with the Romans on th
1 CONC e Grampian Hills was fierce and bloody; but the superior discipline of the Roman legions made it decisive in their favor. At his death he was succeeded by his son... 
1 CONT
1 CONT 56 Felim Fiorurglas: his son. Had the following children: 
1 CONT Cathair Mor, who is described below 
1 CONT Main Mal, the ancestor of O'Kelly of Cualan (in Wicklow), O'Tighe, and O'Cuallan 
1 CONT Eithne 
1 CONT
1 CONT 57 Cathair Mor, 109th Monarch of Ireland in the beginning of the 2nd century: son of Felim Fiorurglas. 
1 CONT
1 CONT 58 Fiacha Baicheda: youngest son of Cathair Mor; died 220. His father praised him for his bravery and spirit, and for the universal love he gained. He was called "the lame" on account of a wound he received in the battle of Moigh 
1 CONC Acha, where his father was slain. From his posterity came the majority of the kings of Leinster 
1 CONT
1 CONT 59 Breasal Bealach (large lipped): his son; was the 2nd Christian King of Leinster. Had following sons: 
1 CONT Labhradh, who is described below 
1 CONT Enna Niadh, who begins family of O'Tooles 
1 CONT
1 CONT The ancient kings of Leinster (Ui Cinnsealaigh = Hy Kinsella) had fortresses or royal residences at Dinn Righ, near the river Barrow, between Carlow and Leighlin; at Naas, in Kildare; and, in after times at the city of Ferns in We
1 CONC xford, which was their capital; and also at Old Ross in Wexford; and at Ballymoon in Carlow. The Ui Cinnsealaigh were inaugurated as kings of Leinster at a place called Cnoc-an-Bhogha, attended by O'Nolan, who was the King's Marsh
1 CONC al, and Chief of Forth in Carlow; by O'Doran, Chief Brehon of Leinster; and by MacKeogh, their Chief Bard. 
1 CONT
1 CONT The major Leinster families begin to split off at this point. 
1 CONT
1 CONT ===================================================================
