0 @N6000000000436481196@ NOTE King in Sweden
0 @N6000000003796269174@ NOTE The Yngling Saga of Heimskingla
1 CONT The Saga of the Norse Kings by Snorri Sturleson
1 CONT (dates may be off):
1 CONT
1 CONT 12. YNGVE ALRICKSSON - King in Svitjod from 280 until killed by his brother in the year 306 A.D. King Alf was very jealous of his brother Yngve. Alf's wife taunted him by making flattering remarks about Yngve, and preferred the co
1 CONC mpany of her brother-in-law to that of her husband. One night after a drinking bout where the guests were so drunk they did not see the King enter the room, he went to the high seat where his wife sat with Yngve. Alf stabbed Yngve
1 CONC  to death, but not before Alf returned the favor, and both fell dead. Yngve's son was:
1 CONT
1 CONT 13. JORUND 
0 @N6000000002187690922@ NOTE Yngvi and Alf
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Ynglingatal, Historia Norwegiae and Ynglinga saga, Yngvi and Alf were the sons of Alrik.
1 CONT
1 CONT Snorri Sturluson relates that Yngvi was an accomplished king: a great warrior who always won his battles, the master of all exercises, generous, happy and sociable. He was both loved and famous.
1 CONT
1 CONT Alf was unsociable and harsh and stayed at home instead of pillaging in other countries. His mother was Dageid, the daughter of king Dag the Great from whom is descended the Dagling family. Alf was married to Bera who was happy an
1 CONC d alert and a very lovable woman.
1 CONT
1 CONT One day in the autumn, Yngvi returned to Uppsala from a very successful viking expedition which had rendered him famous. He used to spend time at the drinking table until late in the night, like Bera, and they found it pleasant to
1 CONC  talk to each other. Alf, however, preferred to go to bed early and he started to tell her to go to bed early as well so that she did not wake him. Then Bera used to answer that Yngvi was much better for a woman than Alf, an answe
1 CONC r that was getting on Alf's nerves.
1 CONT
1 CONT One evening, the jealous Alf entered the hall and saw Yngvi and Bera converse on the high seat. Yngvi had a short sword in his lap and the other guests were too drunk to see that Alf had arrived. From under his cloak Alf drew a sw
1 CONC ord and pierced Yngvi. Yngvi, mortally wounded, got up, drew his own short sword and slew Alf. They were buried in two mounds on the Fyrisvellir (Fyris Wolds).
1 CONT
1 CONT Alf was succeeded by his son Hugleik.
1 CONT
1 CONT The poem in Ynglingatal:
1 CONT
1 CONT     Ok varð hinn,
1 CONT     er Alfr of vá
1 CONT     vörðr véstalls,
1 CONT     of veginn liggja,
1 CONT     er dölingr
1 CONT     dreyrgan mæki
1 CONT     öfundgjarn
1 CONT     á Yngva rauð.
1 CONT
1 CONT     Var-a þat bært
1 CONT     at Bera skyldi
1 CONT     valsœfendr
1 CONT     vígs of hvetja,
1 CONT     þá er brœðr tveir
1 CONT     at bönum urðusk,
1 CONT     óþurfendr,
1 CONT     of afbrýði.[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT     I tell you of a horrid thing,
1 CONT     A deed of dreadful note I sing --
1 CONT     How by false Bera, wicked queen,
1 CONT     The murderous brother-hands were seen
1 CONT     Each raised against a brother's life;
1 CONT
1 CONT     How wretched Alf with bloody knife
1 CONT     Gored Yngve's heart, and Yngve's blade
1 CONT     Alf on the bloody threshold laid.
1 CONT     Can men resist Fate's iron laws?
1 CONT     They slew each other without cause. (Laing's translation [2])
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Erik and Alrik Mythological king of Sweden Succeeded by:
1 CONT Hugleik
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Hervarar Saga and the Saga of Orvar-Odd
1 CONT     * 2 Ari Frodi's Younger Íslendingabók
1 CONT     * 3 Gesta Danorum
1 CONT     * 4 Primary sources
1 CONT     * 5 Secondary sources
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Hervarar Saga and the Saga of Orvar-Odd
1 CONT
1 CONT In the Hervarar saga and the saga of Orvar-Odd, Yngvi was the father of Ingeborg, the princess who was in love with the Swedish hero Hjalmar.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Ari Frodi's Younger Íslendingabók
1 CONT
1 CONT According to Ari Frodi's line of Swedish kings Yngvi was the son of Agne, and not of Agne's son Alrik.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Gesta Danorum
1 CONT
1 CONT In Gesta Danorum, Alf (Alverus) was the father of Yngve (Ing) and Ingjald (Ingild). Ingjald, in his turn was the father of Sigurd Ring and the grand-father of Ragnar Lodbrok.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Primary sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ynglingatal
1 CONT     * Ynglinga saga (part of the Heimskringla)
1 CONT     * Historia Norwegiae
1 CONT     * Hervarar saga
1 CONT     * Orvar-Odd's saga
1 CONT     * Íslendingabók
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Secondary sources
1 CONT
1 CONT Nerman, B. Det svenska rikets uppkomst. Stockholm, 1925.
