0 @N6000000000436552078@ NOTE King in Sweden
0 @N6000000003796269206@ NOTE The Yngling Saga of Heimskingla
1 CONT The Saga of the Norse Kings by Snorri Sturleson
1 CONT (dates may be off):
1 CONT
1 CONT 11. ALRIK - King from 260 until 280. He was born in 240 A.D. and had a brother, Erik. The Saga tells that the two brothers rode out into the fields one day and did not return. Upon investigating, they were found dead, their heads 
1 CONC crushed. As they had no weapons, it was believed they had used the bridles of their horses to beat each other to death. Alrik's son was:
1 CONT
1 CONT 12. YNGVE ALRICKSSON 
0 @N6000000002187690947@ NOTE Alrek and Eirík
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Alrek and Eirík (Old Norse Alrekr and Eiríkr ) were two legendary kings of Sweden.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 In the Ynglinga saga
1 CONT     * 2 In Gautreks saga and Hrólfs saga Gautrekssonar
1 CONT     * 3 In Gesta Danorum
1 CONT     * 4 Commentary
1 CONT     * 5 Secondary sources
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT In the Ynglinga saga
1 CONT
1 CONT According to the Ynglinga saga, Alrek and Eirík were sons and heirs of the previous king Agni by his wife Skjálf. They shared the kingship. They were mighty in both war and sports, but were especially skillful horsmen and vied wit
1 CONC h one another about their horsemanship and their horses.
1 CONT
1 CONT One day they rode off from their retinue and did not return. They were found dead with their heads battered but no weapons with them save the bridle bits of their horses. Accordingly it was believed that they had quarreled and com
1 CONC e to blows and had slain each other with their bridle bits. They were succeeded by Alrik's sons Yngvi and Alf.
1 CONT
1 CONT However, in other sources, only Alrek died, and in the piece of Ynglingatal quoted by Snorri Sturluson it is only Alrek who dies explicitly. Erik's death seems to be a misunderstanding on Snorri's part due to an influence from the
1 CONC  succeeding kings (see also the other sources below):
1 CONT
1 CONT     Fell Alrekr,
1 CONT     þar er Eiríki
1 CONT     bróður vápn
1 CONT     at bana urðu,
1 CONT     ok hnakkmars
1 CONT     með höfuðfetlum
1 CONT     Dags fríendr
1 CONT     of drepask kváðu;
1 CONT     frá-at maðr áðr
1 CONT     eykja greiði
1 CONT     Freys afspring
1 CONT     í folk hafa.[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT     Alric fell, by Eric slain,
1 CONT     Eric's life-blood dyed the plain,
1 CONT     Brother fell by brother's hand;
1 CONT     And they tell it in the land,
1 CONT     That they worked the wicked deed
1 CONT     With the sharp bits that guide the steed.
1 CONT     Shall it be said of Frey's brave sons,
1 CONT     The kingly race, the noble ones,
1 CONT     That they have fought in deadly battle
1 CONT     With the head-gear of their cattle? (Laing's translation [2])
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Agne Mythological king of Sweden Succeeded by:
1 CONT Yngvi and Alf
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT In Gautreks saga and Hrólfs saga Gautrekssonar
1 CONT
1 CONT Gautreks saga also makes Alrek and Eirík sons of Agni by Skjálf and co-kings and it was to them that the warrior Starkad fled after his slaying of King Vikar. Starkad served them first as a companions on their viking expeditions a
1 CONC nd then, after Alrek and Eirík had settled down, went on further Viking expeditions alone.
1 CONT
1 CONT But King Alrek had a short life, for Eirík struck Alrek dead with a bridle when they were out to train their horses and then ruled as sole ruler over Sweden. This version says that Eirík reigned for a long time as told in Hrólfs s
1 CONC aga Gautrekssonar (Saga of Hrólf son of Gautrek).
1 CONT
1 CONT This second saga introduces Thornbjörg, the daughter of King Eirík and Queen Ingigerd, who was a skillful shieldmaiden and ruled over part of the kingdom. Thornbjörg even called herself King Thorberg. But eventually she fell in lo
1 CONC ve with Hrólf son of Gautrek and agreed to marry him, at which point she gave up her weapons to her father King Eirík and took up embroidery.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT In Gesta Danorum
1 CONT
1 CONT Saxo Grammaticus in Book 5 of his Gesta Danorum introduces Ericus Desertus, that is Erik the Eloquent, son of a champion named Regnerus (Ragnar), both Norwegians in the service of King Gøtarus (Götar) of Norway, a monarch otherwis
1 CONC e unknown. This Erik is likely to be the Eirík the Eloquent or Eiríkr the Wise in Speech mentioned by Snorri Sturluson in the Skáldskaparmál as being of Ylfing lineage. But he otherwise has left no clear record in surviving Norse 
1 CONC literature.
1 CONT
1 CONT Saxo makes up for it by telling at greath length of Erik's amusing deeds. He relates how Erik outwitted all foes with clever tricks and became the counselor of Fródi son of Fridleif, king of Denmark. Erik's expeditions on Fródi's 
1 CONC behalf always went well because of Erik's cunning and way with words. Erik finally married Fródi's sister Gunvara and Erik's elder half-brother Rollerus (Roller) was made king of Norway.
1 CONT
1 CONT Saxo then brings in a king of the Swedes named Alricus (Alrik) who corresponds to Alrek of the Norse tradition. Alrik was at war with Gestiblindus king of the Gautar (Geats) and Gestiblindus now sought Fródi's aid. (In the Norse H
1 CONC ervarar saga Gestumblindi is the name assumed by the disguised Odin and it is possible that this Gestiblindus is also Odin in disguise.)
1 CONT
1 CONT Erik and Skalk the Scanian pursued the war and slew Alrik's son Gunthiovus (Old Norse Gunnþjófr) leader of the men of Vermland and Solongs. Then occurred a parley and secret interview between Alrik and Erik in which Alrik attempte
1 CONC d to win Erik over to his cause. When this failed, Alrik asked that the war be settled by a single combat between himself and Gestiblindus. Erik refused the offer because of Gestiblind's unfitness and advanced years but made a cou
1 CONC nter-offer to fight such a duel with Alrik himself if Alrik were willing. The fight occurred straightaway. Alrik was slain and Erik seemed to be fatally wounded so that a report actually came to King Fródi that Erik was dead. Inde
1 CONC ed Erik was long in recovering. However Fródi was disabused when Erik himself returned announcing that Fródi was now also king of Sweden, Värmland, Helsingland, and Soleyar. Fródi then gave all those lands to Erik to rule directly
1 CONC  and also gave Erik the two Laplands, Finland, and Estonia as dependencies paying annual tribute.
1 CONT
1 CONT Saxo explains that this Erik was the first Swedish king to be called Erik but that after him it became a very common name among the Swedish kings. He also writes that Erik met and helped the champion Arngrim, an account that agree
1 CONC s with Hervarar saga, where Arngrim's sons meet Erik's successor Yngvi (see e.g. Angantyr and Hjalmar).
1 CONT
1 CONT That the duel occurred at the end of a "secret interview" suggests that Alrik and Erik were alone when they fought just as were their counterparts in the Norse accounts. That Erik was believed to have died suggests knowledge of th
1 CONC e Ynglinga saga version in which both fighters met their death. There is no mention of horse bridles. But Erik is not elsewhere a great duelist or champion but instead a trickster who wins through stratagems and deceiving words so
1 CONC  that is it likely that Saxo or his source passed over a stratagem in which a horse bridle played a part.
1 CONT
1 CONT Saxo also mentions Starkad's stay in Sweden in Book 6 in a summary of Starkad's life up to that point in his history. But Saxo does not indicate what king or kings then ruled Sweden, saying only:
1 CONT
1 CONT     ... he went into the land of the Swedes, where he lived at leisure for seven years' space with the sons of Frø.
1 CONT
1 CONT Frø is of course the god Frey, the ancestor of the Swedish dynasty.
1 CONT
1 CONT At the beginning of Book 6, Saxo notes that Erik died of a disease and was succeeded by his son Haldanus (Halfdan). Halfdan was later slain by rivals for the throne but the warrior Starkad established Halfdans' heir Siward as the 
1 CONC new king. Siward's daughter Signe was married to King Harald of Denmark who was co-king his brother Fródi. Later Harald's son Halfdan, now king of Denmark, slew Siward in war. But Siward's grandson Erik, the son of Halfdan's uncle
1 CONC  Fródi by Signe, the direct heir to the throne, now rose up against Halfdan. After a long war this second Erik was captured by Haldfan and left in the woods in chains to be devoured by beasts. With him, it seems, the Swedish line 
1 CONC of Erik the Eloquent, as set forth by Saxo, came to an end.
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Agne(?) Saxo's kings of Sweden Succeeded by:
1 CONT Halfdan Eriksson
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Commentary
1 CONT
1 CONT It is not clear whether or not the accounts in the Gesta Danorum and the accounts in the Ynglinga saga' tales of a Danish king named Halfdan who became king of Sweden are at all related. See Halfdan.
1 CONT
1 CONT Traditions of twin brothers connected with horses appear are a commonplace in Indo-European cultures as are foundation legends about two twin brothers, one of whom kills the other. It is possible that Alrek and Eirik are reflexes 
1 CONC of such traditions.
1 CONT
1 CONT Saxo's identification of the legendary Eirík the Eloquent with the legendary Swedish king Eirík probably originated as a flourish by a pro-Danish or pro-Norwegian story teller.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Secondary sources
1 CONT
1 CONT Nerman, B. Det svenska rikets uppkomst. Stockholm, 1925.
0 @N6000000003949700624@ NOTE Heimskringla or The Chronicle of the Kings of Norway
1 CONT # Note: The Ynglinga Saga, or The Story of the Yngling Family from Odin to Halfdan the Black
1 CONT OF ALRIC AND ERIC - The sons of Agne were called Alric and Eric, and were kings togetherafter him. They were powerful men, great warriors, and expert at allfeats of arms. It was their custom to ride and break in horses both to wal
1 CONC k and to gallop, which nobody understood sowell as they; and they vied with each other who could ride best, andkeep the best horses. It happened one day that both the brothers rodeout together alone, and at a distance from their f
1 CONC ollowers, with theirbest horses, and rode on to a field; but never came back. The peopleat last went out to look after them, and they were both found deadwith their heads crushed. As they had no weapons, except it might betheir ho
1 CONC rses' bridles, people believed that they had killed each otherwith these. So says Thjodolf: --
