0 @N6000000003796269263@ NOTE The Yngling Saga of Heimskingla
1 CONT The Saga of the Norse Kings by Snorri Sturleson
1 CONT (dates may be off):
1 CONT
1 CONT 10. AGNE - King from 220 Co 260. Agne took an army to Finland where he defeated the chieftain Froste and took his daughter Skjalv and her brother Loge. He married Skjalv and prepared a great burial feast in honor of her father, Fr
1 CONC oste. Agne now was in possession of the gold ornament which Visbur had refused to return to his first wife. It was securely tied about Agne's neck, when he fell into a drunken stupor after a great drinking bout at the burial feast
1 CONC . Skjalv, his wife, fastened a noose under the ornament while Agne slept, and with the help of her men threw the rope over a branch of the tree above and hanged Agne. His son was:
1 CONT
1 CONT 11. ALRIK
0 @N6000000002187690973@ NOTE Agne
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Agne or Agni Skjafarbonde was a mythological king of Sweden, of the House of Yngling.
1 CONT
1 CONT Snorri Sturluson relates that he was the son of Dag the Wise, and he was mighty and famous. He was also skilled in many ways.
1 CONT
1 CONT One summer, he went to Finland with his army where he pillaged. The Finns gathered a vast host under a chief named Frosti (the Jotun Frosti who was the father of Snær the Old, and consequently Agne's great-great-great-great-grandf
1 CONC ather).
1 CONT
1 CONT A great battle ensued which Agne won and many Finns were killed together with Frosti. Agne then subdued all of Finland with his army, and captured not only great booty but also Frosti's daughter Skjalf and her kinsman Logi (in the
1 CONC  older Ynglingatal only her kinsman, but in Heimskringla he was her brother, which seems to be a mistake by Snorri Sturluson).
1 CONT
1 CONT Agne returned to Sweden and they arrived at Stocksund (Stockholm) where they put up their tent on the side of the river where it is flat. Agne had a torc which had belonged to Agne's great-great-great-grandfather Visbur (who, inte
1 CONC restingly, was the son of Skjalf's niece Drífa). Although, they were related, Agne married Skjalf who became pregnant with two sons, Erik and Alrik.
1 CONT
1 CONT Skjalf asked Agne to honour her dead father Frosti with a great feast, which he granted. He invited a great many guests, who gladly arrived to the now even more famous Swedish king. They had a drinking competition in which Agne be
1 CONC came very drunk. Skjalf saw her opportunity and asked Agne to take care of Visbur's torc which was around his neck. Agne bound it fast around his neck before he went to sleep.
1 CONT
1 CONT The king's tent was next to the woods and was under the branches of a tall tree for shade. When Agne was fast asleep, Skjalf took a rope which she attached to the torc. Then she had her men remove the tent, and she threw the rope 
1 CONC over a bough. Then she told her men to pull the rope and they hanged Agne avenging Skjalf's father. Skjalf and her men ran to the ships and escaped to Finland, leaving her sons behind.
1 CONT
1 CONT Agne was buried at the place and it is presently called Agnafit, which is east of the Tauren (the Old Norse name for Södertörn) and west of Stocksund, i.e. in what is still to this day called Agnehögen (Agne's mound) in Lillhersby
1 CONC .
1 CONT
1 CONT     Þat tel ek undr,
1 CONT     ef Agna her
1 CONT     Skalfar ráð
1 CONT     at sköpum þóttu,
1 CONT     þar gœðing
1 CONT     með gullmeni
1 CONT     Loga dís
1 CONT     at lopti hóf
1 CONT     svalan hest
1 CONT     Signýjar vers.[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT     How do ye like the high-souled maid,
1 CONT     Who, with the grim Fate-goddess' aid,
1 CONT     Avenged her sire? -- made Swithiod's king
1 CONT     Through air in golden halter swing?
1 CONT     How do ye like her, Agne's men?
1 CONT     Think ye that any chief again
1 CONT     Will court the fate your chief befell,
1 CONT     To ride on wooden horse to hell?(Laing's translation [2])
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Dag the Wise Mythological king of Sweden Succeeded by:
1 CONT Erik and Alrik
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Primary sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ynglingatal
1 CONT     * Ynglinga saga (part of the Heimskringla)
1 CONT     * Historia Norwegiae
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Secondary sources
1 CONT
1 CONT Nerman, B. Det svenska rikets uppkomst. Stockholm, 1925.
