0 @N6000000003796269425@ NOTE The Yngling Saga of Heimskingla
1 CONT The Saga of the Norse Kings by Snorri Sturleson
1 CONT (dates may be off):
1 CONT
1 CONT 4. VINLANDE or VINLANDI- a Swedish king who married Driva, daughter of Snow the Old from Finland, where he spent one winter. Vinlande left Finland in the spring, promising to return within three years. When he had not returned aft
1 CONC er ten years, Driva sent the son she had by Vinlande to his father in Sweden and sought: the help of a witch to either kill Vinlande or force him ro rerurn ro Finland. During a nightmare, Vinlande was trampled to death by "Mara" (
1 CONC the nightmare or female ghost). Their son was:
1 CONT
1 CONT 5. VISBUR
0 @N6000000002187696092@ NOTE Vanlade
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Vanlade, Vanlande was a Swedish king at Uppsala of the House of Yngling in Norse mythology. He was the son of Sveigder. Snorri Sturluson wrote in the Ynglinga saga, that Vanlade was a great warrior.
1 CONT
1 CONT Once he stayed for the winter in Finland with Snær the Old, and was married to his daughter Drífa. When he left in spring, he left Drífa behind. Although he had promised her to come back in three years time, she had to wait ten ye
1 CONC ars. Then she sent her and Vanlade's son Visbur to Sweden, and sent a message to the völva Huld. She asked the völva to either bring Vanlade back to Finland or kill him.
1 CONT
1 CONT As the völva worked to bring Vanlade back, he was staying at Uppsala and he felt a great need to travel to Finland. His advisors and friends told him not to go and claimed that it was the witchraft of the Finns that made him want 
1 CONC to go. Having resisted the wish to leave, he became drowsy and went to bed. A little later he screamed that he was hagridden and his men came to help him. However, when they grasped his head the Mara stepped on his legs and when t
1 CONC hey held his legs she trod on his head. This caused him to die. The Swedes burnt his body by the River Skyt (one of the tributaries of the River Fyris) and raised a menhir over him.
1 CONT
1 CONT Snorri quoted some lines from Ynglingatal composed in the 9th century:
1 CONT
1 CONT     En á vit
1 CONT     Vilja bróður
1 CONT     vitta véttr
1 CONT     Vanlanda kom,
1 CONT     þá er trollkund
1 CONT     of troða skyldi
1 CONT     liðs grímhildr
1 CONT     ljóna bága;
1 CONT     ok sá brann á beði Skútu
1 CONT     menglötuðr,
1 CONT     er mara kvalði.[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT     And Vanlande, in a fatal hour,
1 CONT     Was dragg'd by Grimhild's daughter's power,
1 CONT     The witch-wife's, to the dwelling-place
1 CONT     Where men meet Odin face to face.
1 CONT     Trampled to death, to Skytaa's shore
1 CONT     The corpse his faithful followers bore;
1 CONT     And there they burnt, with heavy hearts,
1 CONT     The good chief killed by witchcraft's arts.(Laing's translation [2])
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Sveigder Mythological king of Sweden Succeeded by:
1 CONT Visbur
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Geography
1 CONT
1 CONT Geographical note: the river skutá is today called Björklingeån. According to the encyclopedias Nationalencyklopedin and Nordisk familjebok it passed its name onto the village and the parish of Skuttunge. The area does not only co
1 CONC ntain raised stones, but also 45 grave fields (most from the Iron Age), including a dolmen.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ynglingatal
1 CONT     * Ynglinga saga (part of the Heimskringla)
1 CONT     * Historia Norwegiae
