1 CONT 5. VISBUR
0 @N6000000003796269474@ NOTE The Yngling Saga of Heimskingla
1 CONT The Saga of the Norse Kings by Snorri Sturleson
1 CONT (dates may be off):
1 CONT
1 CONT 3. SVEGDE - King of Svitjod as well as a place in Russia from where he got his wife who was named Vana. Svegde was born in 1 B.C. and died in 34 A.D. The Saga tells of his strange demise, also in a state of drunken-ness like his f
1 CONC ather. One night when Svegde and his men had been drinking excessively and were about Co retire for the night, Svegde looked out and saw a large stone with a dwarf sitting under it. He rushed to the stone where the dwarf opened a 
1 CONC door and urged Svegde Co enter in order chat he could see Odin. Svegde went in and never came out. He and Vana had a son who was:
1 CONT
1 CONT 4. VINLANDE or VINLANDI
0 @N6000000002187696111@ NOTE Sveigder
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Sveigder or Swegde was a Swedish king of the House of Yngling in Norse mythology. He was the son of Fjölner.
1 CONT
1 CONT The Ynglinga saga relates that at his father's death Sveigder vowed to go to Godheim (Asgard) and visit Odin. He took his twelve hirdmen and went through the world. He came to Turkland and Great Sweden (Russia), but after five yea
1 CONC rs he returned home. He got himself a wife from Vanaheim (a Vanir?) who was called Vana and they had the son Vanlande. Then Sveigder resumed his search for Odin and came to a mansion east of Sweden called Stein (see also Ingvar) w
1 CONC hich was called so because there was a stone as big as a house. After sunset, Sveigder was going to bed from having drunk with the hosts, and passing the stone he saw that a dwarf (see elf) was sitting under it. Sveigder and his c
1 CONC ompanion were drunk and ran for the dwarf. The dwarf called to Sveigder to come into the stone and see Odin which Sveigder did and he never came back.
1 CONT
1 CONT Snorri also quoted some lines from Ynglingatal composed in the 9th century:
1 CONT
1 CONT     En dagskjarr
1 CONT     Dúrnis niðja
1 CONT     salvörðuðr
1 CONT     Sveigði vétti,
1 CONT     þá er í stein
1 CONT     enn stórgeði
1 CONT     Dusla konr
1 CONT     ept dvergi hljóp,
1 CONT     ok salr bjartr
1 CONT     þeira Sökmímis
1 CONT     jötunbyggðr
1 CONT     við jöfri gein.[1]
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT     By Diurnir's elfin race,
1 CONT     Who haunt the cliffs and shun day's face,
1 CONT     The valiant Swegde was deceived,
1 CONT     The elf's false words the king believed.
1 CONT     The dauntless hero rushing on,
1 CONT     Passed through the yawning mouth of stone:
1 CONT     It yawned -- it shut -- the hero fell,
1 CONT     In Saekmime's hall, where giants dwell.(Laing's translation [2])
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Fjolner Mythological king of Sweden Succeeded by:
1 CONT Vanlade
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ynglingatal
1 CONT     * Ynglinga saga (part of the Heimskringla)
1 CONT     * Historia Norwegiae
0 @N6000000003051219754@ NOTE Sveigthir was lured into a rock by a dwarf, and there remained trapped forever, according to legend. [Royal Families of Medieval Scandinavia, Flanders, and Kiev]
0 @N6000000001351731909@ NOTE Said to be descended from the Aesir God Freyr
0 @N6000000003796269501@ NOTE The Yngling Saga of Heimskingla
1 CONT The Saga of the Norse Kings by Snorri Sturleson
1 CONT (dates may be off):
1 CONT
1 CONT 2. FJALNE or FJOLNIR- born-about 32 B.C. and died in 14 A.D. The saga tells of his unusual death. While on a visit to Frode, the King of Denmark, a great feast was prepared in Fjalne's honor Co which the King invited many guests f
1 CONC rom throughout the country. A great vat had been constructed on the lower floor of Frode's big house, above which was a loft. A hole had been made in the floor of the loft to accommodate the preparation of mead (a strong alcoholic
1 CONC  liquor) in the vat. Fjalne and his party had rooms on the level of the loft above the vat of mead. During the night Fjalne felt "nature's call" and left his room to relieve himself. The saga states: "he was very sleepy and exceed
1 CONC ingly drunk." On his return to his room he slipped from the gallery into the vat of mead and drowned. His son was:
1 CONT
1 CONT 3. SVEGDE 
0 @N6000000002187696130@ NOTE Fjölnir
1 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
1 CONT
1 CONT Fjölnir, Fjölner or Fjolner was a Swedish king of the House of Yngling, at Gamla Uppsala.
1 CONT Contents
1 CONT [hide]
1 CONT
1 CONT     * 1 Grottasöngr
1 CONT     * 2 Ynglinga saga
1 CONT     * 3 Ynglingatal
1 CONT     * 4 Gesta Danorum
1 CONT     * 5 Ballad of Veraldur
1 CONT     * 6 Other Uses
1 CONT     * 7 Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Grottasöngr
1 CONT
1 CONT Grottasöngr relates that he was the contemporary of Caesar Augustus. Fjölnir was a mighty king and the crops were bountiful and peace was maintained. At his time, king Fródi ruled in Lejre in Zealand. The two kings were great frie
1 CONC nds and they often visited each other, but their friendship was to cause them to inadvertently kill each other. Grottisong relates that when Frodi once visited Uppsala he bought two giantesses, Fenja and Menja, but they were to be
1 CONC  his undoing (see Grottasöngr).
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Ynglinga saga
1 CONT
1 CONT Heimskringla relates that he was the son of Freyr himself and the giantess Gerd, but he was the first of his house who was not to be deified. Once Fjölnir went to see Frodi in Zealand and a great feast had been prepared to which m
1 CONC any people were invited. Frodi had a large house where he stored a huge vessel full of very strong mead. Above the vessel there was an opening in the ceiling from which mead was poured into it, by men standing in the loft above. A
1 CONC fter the banquet, Fjölnir was taken to stay the night in an adjoining loft. However, at night he felt that all the mead he had drunk forced him to leave his bed and to seek his way out into the bushes to relieve himself. Since he 
1 CONC was very drunk and very tired he stumbled through the wrong door and staggered across the floor above the vessel. He slipped and fell through the opening into the vessel of mead where he drowned.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Ynglingatal
1 CONT
1 CONT Snorri also quoted some lines of Ynglingatal, composed in the 9th century:
1 CONT
1 CONT     Varð framgengt,
1 CONT     þars Fróði bjó,
1 CONT     feigðarorð,
1 CONT     es at Fjölni kom;
1 CONT     ok sikling
1 CONT     svigðis geira
1 CONT     vágr vindlauss
1 CONT     of viða skyldi.
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT     In Frode's hall the fearful word,
1 CONT     The death-foreboding sound was heard:
1 CONT     The cry of fey denouncing doom,
1 CONT     Was heard at night in Frode's home.
1 CONT     And when brave Frode came, he found
1 CONT     Swithiod's dark chief, Fjolne, drowned.
1 CONT     In Frode's mansion drowned was he,
1 CONT     Drowned in a waveless, windless sea.(Laing's translation [1])
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT Preceded by:
1 CONT Yngvi-Freyr Mythological king of Sweden Succeeded by:
1 CONT Sveigder
1 CONT
1 CONT
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Gesta Danorum
1 CONT
1 CONT In Gesta Danorum, Book 1, Frodi corresponds to Hadingus and Fjölnir to Hundingus, but the story is a little different. It relates how King Hundingus of Sweden believed a rumor that King Hadingus of Denmark had died and held his ob
1 CONC sequies with ceremony, including an enormous vat of ale. Hundingus himself served the ale, but accidentally stumbled and fell into the vat, choked, and drowned. When word of this came to King Hadingus of this unfortunate death, Ki
1 CONC ng Hadingus publicly hanged himself (see Freyr).
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Ballad of Veraldur
1 CONT
1 CONT Dumézil (1973, Appendix I) cites a Faroese ballad recorded in 1840 about Odin and his son Veraldur. It is believed that this Veraldur is related to Fjölnir and Freyr, as per Snorri's statement that Freyr was veraldar goð ("god of 
1 CONC the world").
1 CONT
1 CONT In this ballad Veraldur sets off to Zealand to seek the king's daughter in marriage despite Odin's warnings. The king of Zealand mislikes Veraldur and tricks him into falling into a brewing vat in a "hall of stone" where Veraldur 
1 CONC drowns. When Odin hears the news, he decides to die and go to Asgard where his followers will be also be welcomed after death.
1 CONT
1 CONT The tale is similar to that of the death of Fjölnir, son of Freyr, who accidentally fell into a vat of mead and drowned while paying a friendly visit to Fridfródi the ruler of Zealand.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Other Uses
1 CONT
1 CONT Fjölnir is also another name for Odin, found in Grímnismál when the god revealed himself to Geirröd, and in Reginsmál when he was standing on a mountain addressing Sigurd and Regin. Snorri also mentions the name in Gylfaginning.
1 CONT [edit]
1 CONT
1 CONT Sources
1 CONT
1 CONT     * Ynglingatal
1 CONT     * Ynglinga saga (part of the Heimskringla)
1 CONT     * Historia Norvegiæ
1 CONT     * Gróttasöngr
1 CONT     * Gesta Danorum
1 CONT     * Gimle: Hedniska ballader: Balladen om Oden och Veraldur (Frö) (Text of the ballad of Veraldur).
